      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>SIY DEAR KIMLIE</p>
        <p>The indoor dresses for girls are light and pretty just now. The newest are in tarletan, white, or pale-tinted, freaked all over with balls of chenille. Some of them are vandyked in a pattern with little fluff's, which are always of the same colour as the tarlatan. In white this dainty mate- rial looks remarkably well, and also in pale blue, green, apple-blossom pink, mauve, and yellow. In black it is suitable for old wearers. It is very in- expensive. White alpaca,. is a favourite material for girls' dresses. It is in a fine make, with a silky surface, and the skirts sold ready-made are lined with a very light muslin. Most of these alpacas are untrimmed, their simplicity being suitable for quite young wearers, who are just beginning their social life. These dresses may be worn in the even- ing if the owners care to do so, in fact two of my friends have evening dresses made of the above materials.</p>
        <p>Gradually, the picture hats are on the decline, although I have heard that several ladies at South Coast resorts still adopt that style of headgear. Toques are made of chincilla, and trimmed with mauve or blue velvet and violets or primroses. The camellia is rather in favour for millinery, but the gardenia holds its own. A large chrysan- themum is often seen upholding ihe raised brim of the capote or hat at the lest side. The pokes have enjoyed a success. Their only fault is that they suit pretty faces only. One I have is lined with pale blue mirror velvet, rtitched rather closely. The brim is covered with brown velvet laid on in fine pleats. The feathers are shaded brown and blue.</p>
        <p>Outdoor costumes are engaging th 3 attention of Ahose people who do any amount of walking about. Ef one takes a walk, almost anywhere, it is notice- ible that the first costume in order of popularity aonsists of a long, tight skirt of face-cloth in some leep but decided shade, such as dahlia, royal blue, ir mulberry, trimmed with braiding arranged to timulate a robed front, and very smartly lined with frilled silk. With such a skirt as this is worn an extremely short and tight Eton coat, either of velvet of a slightly darker shade than he skirt, or of fine caracol. This coat is not made iouble-breasted, but fastened with four or six aidden buttons, while the revers and collar—often iined with ermine or chinchilla—arc' unless the weather be very cold indeed, thrown back from a lill cravat and the ordinary shaped linen collar and front.</p>
        <p>I am inclined to think that a half-mourning evening gown, sure of acceptance just now when so many are lamenting the loss of a relative or friend at the front, is one of which the material is black velvet with white spots, white lace com- posing the yoke and underskirt, bath mounted' on doll white silk veiled with chiffon. It is quite out-of-date to give lace a lining of anything shiny. The eff,et should be as \misty and sot as possible. Rack velvet straps over the shoulders and cut jet bucklec and buttons coin- plate the picture. It .is sad to see the numbers of persons who aro now wearing very deep mourning. The manu- facture of crape has now bean bronght to such perfaction that it neithei crushes nor spots with rain. A widow's dress I saw yesterday was of cloth, bordered with closely-set pipingq, forms the tunic, and the m tv- 3f the same. The collar. revers were in the thane, edged with fringe, and the c'nemisette was hi dud black corded silk, piped across with ins.ny rows. The little lawn collar was turned down over the top of a piped silk collar, and the usual lawn cuffs came Up over plain cloth sleeves.</p>
        <p>Whilst-visiting at a friend's the other thy I saw a sp!endid specimen black pearl set in a ring with a large, ticely•eut hrillie,nt on either Eide. In other rings a magnificent ruby and a large, square emerald were set respectively in the same way. The olivine is in great favour for rings. I always think it a prettier green than that ot the emerald, except when one nolds the latter up to the light, which is not always feasible. The peridot, too, is very much worn just now, when large enough to become of importance. One I saw in a bracelet was magnificently cut. Now and then a curious, violet light shone out from its lake of green radiance, a strange effect, and one that certainly fired the imaginations of Your loving,</p>
        <p>-Married Ladies. FREE. Information. of priceless value FREE on rece43t, of stamped</p>
        <p>envelope and addre;3s. TESTIMONIALS DAILY.</p>
        <p>MRS,</p>
        <p>ARLLIGII,</p>
        <p>(Box 63,) 160 HACKNEY ROAD, LONDON, N.E</p>
        <p>„..he</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The new hospital ship, the " Princess of Wales." now on her way to the Cape, has been admirably fitted for the purpose, and everything has been done to conduce to the comfort of the wounded men whom it will bring home. The Princess of Wales has manifested the greatest interest in all the details, and has, by her per- sonal efforts contributed very considerably to the suc- cess of the undertaking. The wards have been appro- priately named after the Princess and her daughters,io far as the list would go, and there seems to be every reason to anticipate that they will do credit to their names. Despite the physical weakness entailed by advancing years, Miss Florence Nightingale retains a remarkable mental vigour and vivacty, and is manifesting the keenest interest in the present war and the nursing arrangements. Clayton House, Buckinghamshire, where much of her time is now spent, is a veritable store- house of artistic objects. It contains an extensive and valuable collection of paintings and sculptures, but per- haps one of the most notable features of the mansion is its magnificent staircase inlaid with ivory and ebony, with exquisitely wrought iron balustrades, representing clusters of growing corn. Lady Mabella Knox, who died at Bath recently, was present at the Coronetien of George IV. and at a child- ren's ball, given by him when Prince Regent, in 1817. There remains now but one old lady who can boast of being present at the same two functions, Lady Mary Saurin. She was born in the same year, 1801. Another link with the past is Lady Louisa Tighe. Burn in Lon- don 1803, she went later on, with tier mother and father,the fourth Duke of Richmond to Brustelsewhere they remained all through the stirring timss that pre- ceded and followed the Battle of Waterloo. She has a most vivid recollection of all those wondrous scenes, and relates with considerable energy the consternation that reigned when the bugle ssundeci, calling the men to arms, during the ball at her father and mother's house in Brussels, on the eve of Waterloo, at which all the flowers of the Army, and else the reigning beauties were present. And she tells of the Duke of Wellington looking out on the map, the probable position the enemy would take. Ihe friends of Miss Beatrice Harraden. author of "Ships that Pass in the Night" have suffered much annoyance ot late, through a statement which has been circulated by an American interviewer to the effect that Miss Harraden's sympathies are all on the side of the Boers in the present war. Such a statement is entirely without foundation and thoroughly misrepresents MiS3 Harraden's feelings on the subject Miss Harraden is slight and fragile, with short black hair, brushed carefully back from her brows, but with a knack of returning nails, and hanging in soft masses upon her temples. Her eyes are dark', and expressive, and somewhat hid by the pince-nez which she is com- pelled to wear. Her face looks singularly sweet when she smiles, and a little sad in repose. Miss Harraden usually dresses in loose neglige gowns, which become her delightfully. The coal merchants are everywhere making the' war an excuse for putting up the price of crate which, at this time of year, is a serious matter to many people. There is no reason why the war should make any appre- ciable difference in the price of coal, any more than in other commodities. The amount ef shipping diverted from commerce by the transport of troops to the Cape, and the amount of steam coal thus consumed is infin- itesimal as compared with the bulk of trade, and in the natural course of things would scarcely affect prices. But dealers and speculators invariably seize upon the outbreak of war as a means to making money. One of the first results ot the war between the United States and Spain, was to greatly put up the price of corn, but there is still less reason than there was in this case, for making the war in South Afrca the grounds for iccreasing the price of house coal. In London, fuel is now almost as dear as it wae at the time of the great coal strike in the Midlands, five or six years ago, and every one interested in the trade is busy predicting that it will go much higher. In the Crimean War, coal was not always easy to get at £2 104 per ton, and no doubt this is the price coal merchants would like to see again. One reason why coal is dear is of course to be found in the eeneral prosperity of trade which always means a largely increased demand for fuel for industrial and commercial purposes, and this makes it easy for the dealers to cultivate the war fiction as a means for run- ing coal up to fancy prices. We can only hope that a mild and genial winter will do much to defeat these schemes. . .</p>
        <p>The public in general has little notion of the extent to which preserving and colouring materials are used in the food trade, and it therefore takes little interest in the proceedings of the Departmental Committee, appointed a short time since by the Local Gewrnment Board, to inquire into the whole subject. All the expert witnesses seem agreed that the use of boracic and salicigtic acids in milk, to keep it from turning are no more harmless than the use of arsenic. A minute quantity may do no great harm, but taken continually into the system, in however small doses, its effect is notably poisonous, and especially harmful for invalids and children. Another brug, formaldehyde, which is of a still more potent character, is also being largely used as a preservative, and there is no doubt that the committee will recommend some regulation in the use of these drugs, now so commonly used to preserve milk, cream, butter, jam, hams, and other articles of food. There is another matted which the authorities would also do well to take in hand, namely to irsist-that all the coleuring matter used in the manufacture of toys, should be harmless. In Germany the law already pro- vides for this. When the change was made large quan- tities of German toys were rendered unsaleable in that country, and were consequently sent over here, and for all anybody krows or cares, a large proportion of our children's toys which are nearly all imported, are still coloured with poisonous compounds. .</p>
        <p>Fans tbi season are small. a much more convenient fashion than the gigantic idea. Those prepared for Christmas gifts are mostly finished with monograms or initials in diamonds, or some favourite stone, a delight- ful, but somewhat expensive procedure. 'lhe Empress of Germany possesses a fine collection of fans, many of them having been given her by the Emperor. One of these of which she is very fond, is made from the feathers of, a capercailzie, which was shot by His Majesty. - In the early days of our Queen's life,it was the custom for women to coyly carry scarves acro-,s their arms, or over their shoulders. This fashion is being revived, many up-to-date girls exhibiting dainty specimens with demure effect. Long practice and a natural grace only however, can enable a girl to manipulate the scarf properly, and this to same extent, will stand in the way of a more general adoption of the mode. " MADGE."</p>
        <p>OLYPHON.</p>
