      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>T7!E UXAMiNFP, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 1, 1899.</head>
        <p>T7!E UXAMiNFP, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 1, 1899.</p>
        <p>him. Ire wanted more air, and he tried the garden ;loon It was locked, but he had a duplicate kez and passed out. What were the Feltons doing at this moment? _Doubtless maturing their plans!</p>
        <p>"His friends!" he said. Then he seeded to 63 struck by a strange feeling of hatred towards these friends who would spare no one. Then a voice seemed to say to him: "Have you spared others ? " No, he had not spared the man who had done him no wrong. The squire passed into the garden. He looked up at Ruby's window. Ail was dark and silent. Then he passed round to where a light burnt. It was a large, ground floor room shared by the Feltons. They also had the key of the little garden door and passed freely in and out. Walking round by the stables the squire heard sounds of footsteps. He paused and listened. "James," he said to himself, and went on till he came upon the young man smoking a cigar, walk- ing up and down opposite the stables. "Hulloa, squire! You out ? "</p>
        <p>"It is a still night; I came out to smoke." "And Ito mature plans. I can get in without difficulty, but, of course, one has to make sure that he won't wake."</p>
        <p>"But you told me that you had-" "Yes, one has everything necessary, but the thing to provide against is a chance.of failure. It is only bunglers who.,leave traces." "Look here, James, if we get his signature be content. He already admires Ruby and will do what I ask. You can then have the wife you want and the money." "The money I must have. You don't think, my good sir, that we :have taken all this trouble only for Ruby's sake ? " "No, I don't think so. Sometimes I wonder if-"</p>
        <p>"Oh, come now, don't humbug. If we had not rut conditions you might have become a spendthrift and done away with it all. Father did you a good turn by making you save the half for us. "None of your impudence," muttered Sir Harry. "What I want to say is this: You had better be contented with the signature and let Beauworth alone. He will go back to his work and'you can take Ruby to Australia, or leave her here with me." "Thanks, but the fellow might fall in love and make another will. No, the chance against that is too great. Let him fall in love with Ruby, that won't hurt anyone, and then he can-" The conversation Wu Capped suddenly by a shriek from the stable just above their heads. Both men uttered exclamations of disgust and rushed in. The loft was reached by a ladder and Sir Harry ran up. Bob was sitting up blubbering, and in answer to the forcible language of his master he stuttered forth that he had had a nightmare, and dreamt that the devil was after him, and thereupon had started up screaming. It was only the night- mare, he had often had it at home. He was terrible sorry; he didn't know that his master was there nor the other gen'leman. The squire gave Bob a searching glance, but the boy's face only expressed mortal terror. James favoured him with a kick, but as there was nothing else to be done both men beat a retreat down the ladder followed by Bob, who humbly begged for forgiveness. His mother had often tried to cure him of dreams, but it was the way with him. He felt sure it was the devil hisself as was after him. At this James Felton laughed inordinately as he and Sir Harry walked off. "The boy was not shamming, was he ? " said James, suddenly stopping short. _ .</p>
        <p>"Bob? Oh, -no, caoilldri't do it. But I think he had better come indoors and not disturb the night with his howls."</p>
        <p>"I shall make him useful. Yes, a good,idea I I have it. Rabbitts, the father, is safe if the boy blab?" "Safe! I should think so. Tied hand and foot."</p>
        <p>Bob waited till he saw the last of the pair dis- appear, when his blubbering turned into broad _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Well, I niver! To come disturbing a poor lad with their talk. They'll find it inconvenient to let me stay here, and there's Mr. Alec's so careless as to shut his window. They is hatching it quick. He is only here a week, but I'll be even with them!"</p>
        <p>Bob chuckled again with amusement. "I'm awful sleepy now. I wonder if it's safe to have a wink. I'll go first and see if the young 'tin is gone in." He crept down and with cat- like stealthiness, born of much practice, he made a round of the house. "Wish I could get in," he thought, as he stood under Alec's window. "But they won't do nothing to-night. There's some- thing Mr. Alec's got to do—sign something or t' other. I'll tell him to-morrow not to do it."</p>
        <p>When Bob at last returned to the loft he fell into an uneasy slumber, but before anyone else was .‘tir in the house he had made his way in.</p>
        <p>'6-'6e-house he had work to do, and, having noted that the squire had at last retired to bed, he crept up the stairs to Alec's room and softly—very softly—he opened the door which was hidden by the curtains on one side of the bed. James knew some secrets which Bob had not yet found out, namely, that behind the great poster bed there was a tiny door which slid back and which had been used in old days for a means of escape. He had just reached this spot when Alec awoke, as we have already seen, and felt that something was in the room. Had it not been that, instead of going t) the door he went to the window, he would have found himself face to face with James. As it was the latter had time to slide back the panel and to disappear. The tiny chamber behind communicated with another passage, enabling anyone to escape into the other wing of the house and thus to elude all pursuit. Thus it happened that when Alec examined his room there was no trace of the intruder.</p>
        <p>The next morning, however, when,Bob brought Alec his water he told the boy that ho was sorry to have forgotten his window, and then he told him how he had imagined that he heard something moving in his room. "Eh, but it wasiike ghost!" said Bob, with a broad grin. "If IWad been there I could have seen it, Mr. Alec. , I expect I'm going to sleep indoors. The squire don't like my sleeping in the loft. I have horrid nightmares, I have. Had it last night. Thought the devil was after you." , Alec laughed. "You still believe in my enemies. I have almost forgotten the incident, so don't dream of me, Bob, my friend." "I dreamt he wanted you to sign away your soul, Mr. Alec. Don't you sign nothing." "Certainly I won't sign away my soul. I don't know where to get another." Alec dismissed his admirer with a good laugh, but then the difficulties of his situation returned in full force to his mind. How could he save Ruby from marrying James Felton? When he went down he met Ruby on the stairs. "Cousin Alec, father says that he will let me walk with you and with him to-day. I am so glad that the Australian cousins are going out for the day." "Are you ? " said Alec, looking at the smiling face so unconscious of the evils of the world.</p>
        <p>"Yes, you and I will enjoy it much more. Papa is so glad of an excuse to take a walk with me. He says always that he is too busy." "Would you like me to live here always, Ruby ? " "Of course I should, always. Will you ? "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</head>
        <p>NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</p>
        <p>BY ESME STUART,</p>
        <p>Author of "In the Dark," "Inscrutable," "A Woman of Forty," "Sara's Mother," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: On his way to visit his cousin, Sir Harry Beauworth, Alec Beauworth, heir to the title, is set upon by two men and knocked senseless. Sir Harry Beauworth has a bad reputation. Bob Rabbitts, son of the evil-looking landlord of the Dog and Duck, revives Alec, and determines, as he has the chance, to enter Sir Harry's service for a time to see that Alec comes to no harm. Sir Harry has one daughter, Ruby, a beautiful and unsoplpsticated girl, who has never been allowed to see the world, and whom her father intends to marry to an .Australiam James Felton, who, with his father, Tom Felton, appears to have a powerful influence over him. These Feltons are the men who tried to kill Alec, though he never saw their faces, and it is obvious that they acted at the instigation of the, baronet, who has some secret reason for wishing to be rid of the heir. Alec, when he sees Ruby, falls in love with her, and not know- ing his danger determines, if possible, to stay and win her. Though he has no intention of allow- ing Ruby to marry Alec, Sir Harry Beauworth plays upon Alec's affections in order to induce him to make a will in Ruby's favour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER IX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IX.</p>
        <p>When the squire was left alone, he went softly upstairs as usual, and locked the great partition door which led to Ruby's rooms. "At least you ate safe for the present," he said to himself as he retraced his steps, and then he sat down in the leathern arm-chair and fell into musings which so absorbed him that he did not hear the two Feltons come in till the younger one touched him on the shoulder.</p>
        <p>"Eh! Sir Harry in a very brown study ? what luck? You see we have kept out of the way to let you and Ruby have your full swing." Sir Harry slightly shook off the young, strong hand which seemed to oppress him, but slight as the action was it was noticed by James Felton. "Am I too familiar, proud Baronet ? " ho laughed. 14 "You were not so particular in our old days before you came into the title," said the elder Felton. "Come, be sociable, and tell us what luck you have had." "My usual luck," said Sir Harry, but there was no smile on his face.</p>
        <p>"You always have been desperately lucky," said Felton, sitting down and stretching out his legs towards the flame.</p>
        <p>"You have but one weakness—it is that child," said James. "Without her you would have no failures, but—"</p>
        <p>"You can't understand," said Sir Harry im- patiently. "No, we can't. Out there father and I have often remarked that she was the only spoke in the wheel."</p>
        <p>:"Ruby's happiness ought to come before any- thing else." "Oh, come, that's all very well; originally you sang another tune." "You are running great risks," said the squire, looking at the elder Felton. "Not any, if you can he trusted! Look here, leave the young prig to us and manage Ruby. She will be very happy with .James. You have kept that part of your contract at all events. She knows no one else. Girls are such ticklish cattle; ignorance is the only chance."</p>
        <p>•-"You had better let Beauworth alone." "We werq unlucky, I grant, but everything legal before-</p>
        <p>"lucre must be other ways. Ruby was very happy to-day. You may find that the experiment is too risky." "My good fellow," said Mr. Felton, "leave us to settle our affairs. James is clever, very clever; I have never known him fail. He was pitted against the sharpest fellow in the colony and beat him! What do you say to that ? " "Besides," said James, beaming with conscious pride at the character his father gave him, "besides, father stuck to you when you wanted him, and it would be mean to be squeamish now." Sir Harry folded his arms and turned towards the elder man.</p>
        <p>"You can't say that I have been false so you. I lure drained the estate as much as was possible. I am hated by every man, woman and child here, and all that you might never say that I had played you false." "That was the bargain. You played the game well, but you are not made of , the tough fibre necessary for great risks." "Ask any of the people here. They won't tell you that I am soft-hearted." "And what about Ruby? Why, you are on the verge of shewing the white feather because Rnby has a little flirtation with her cousin."</p>
        <p>Both the men laughed heartily. "No, no, I don't care a straw about him, only if Ruby might have had more time. She is a loving child. She is not used to your life, and you cannot imagine James living a quiet life with her."</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir Harry," said James, in quiet scorn, "I think that after your bringing up, Ruby would best enjoy a little knocking about. Perhaps, when all is settled, you might keep her a bit longer—safe, of course. You have brought her up the wrong way. She is not afraid of you." Sir Harry gave a slight wince. "Come, Harry," said the elder Felton, de- cidedly, "no shirking. To-morrow morning you must bring us the paper signed and witnessed." "You must reckon with chance. I can only persuade him." "We trusted to chance and• you see the result. By the way, Sir Harry, who is that boy you have got here?" "Rabbitts's son."</p>
        <p>"Not likely to have qualms then?" "Oh, no; he is obliged to obey his father. The boy is lazy at home, but he seems to do well here; a stupid lad otherwise." "He's always about, but looks half-baked if you speak to him." "Bob won't hear or see anything; besides, if he did, I have Rabbitts.entirely in my power. Look here, Felton--"</p>
        <p>"Yes, I'm all attention, Sir Harry." "I'll get the signature and let the young man go back to London."</p>
        <p>"Oh, I dare say Look here, we know all about his diggings. Have we not waited a long time till that old maid was dead? No, now is the time. His landlady will be easy to settle." "And you will leave Ruby with me?" "We didn't say that. Why you know that,that is your weak point. Ruby once married is safer. As to leaving her here afterwards" "But we need not settle that now," added the elder man. • "Important business in Australia. Ruby will be broken-hearted, and her dear father must consent." James spoke ironically, and Sir Harry bit his lip. "You will ruin her life." "Come, you have always a remedy. You can-" "Leave him to think it out, father." The three friends separated, but with no great show of friendship. Left alone the squire threw himself into the great chair and once more went over all the events of past years. They seemed to resolve themselves into the one word "Ruby." How could he spare her? How could he fight for her? Out of the mesh which entangled him how could he extricate her and himself? He did not give one thought to Alec except where his actions touched Ruby. If he could save Ruby by sacrificing him he knew he should not hesitate. The fire burnt down, and he forgot to replenish It, and then an uneasy feeling came over him, and he went through the hall and out into the passages leading to the disused rooms, which had been so lately opened for the use of the Feltons. He walked cautiously so as not to be heard if by chance they were about. But all was silent. When he came to the door of the passage stairs leading up to Ruby's rooms the squire paused. He had given orders that this door was to be locked by Nancy at night and the key taken by her. He tried it, half fearful that she had forgotten, but all was safe. Ruby was well guarded. Nancy would certainly let no one come near the girl from that side. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"But, after all, there is no fear till James has married her, and before that time I must find a way to save her. I must. Why not let this Alec Beau- worth take her. But how could I? It is impossi- Me ; I am powerless. As well ask a thrush to spare the worm it has in its mouth as to expect pity from the Felions. The game was well played if it had not been for RTiby. Child, you little guess wis9g ;Piot have done. '? Ind settkirele4 as if the house were too_ small for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHAPTER I.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER I.</p>
        <p>It was a brilliant winter's day. One of those days which makes one think that spring has for- gotten to come at her right time and has by mistake tripped into the cold world too soon. The squire had been called away to a cottage, and had again left Ruby and her cousin alone, Bob following at a respectful distance carrying a gun, which Ruby had begged might not be used as she hated to see anything killed.</p>
        <p>Alec was sinkinideeier into the sea of his mis= fortunes, for the more he tried to look upon Ruby as only his distant cousin, the more he fell in love with her. She, on her side, was as much at home with him as if she had known him all her life.</p>
        <p>"To-day is Tuesday. How quickly a—N-ve-e-li will go. And must you really go when it is over ? " "I must be back at the office next week. lam only a clerk." "Tell me what you do at the office. You draw, I think. Will you teach me to draw? I should love to do it, only there is no one here to help me."</p>
        <p>"I am afraid that in a week it is hardly possi- ble. I draw plans and things which you would think very uninteresting. But lam also very fond of sketching. When my adopted mother was alive I used to Law for her, but now I have no one who cares to see what I do."</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes, now you have me and papa," said Ruby, smiling. Alec felt that he ought to resist the temptstion to smile back again, but he did not.</p>
        <p>"Do you ea-re, Ruby? That will be a grea pleasure to think about, but I shall, perhapst never see you again." .. "Never see me again! Why?" ' "Because you may soon be married, and then ten will have to follow Tour husband."</p>
        <p>"Oh, I am only a child, and children don't have lovers,"</p>
        <p>"Girls marry very' young sometimes," said Alec, iooking away and feeling angry with the squire._ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Do they? Poor girls -do, but not ladies. Nancy rays so." "All girls should marry only when they love someone very much. Do you understand that, Ruby 1' " Alec felt that he was betraying his trust. It was roost important that Ruby should know nothing abut love.</p>
        <p>"Yes, of course. Papa says that all girls love their husbands after they are married, but not before. it is not ladylike to fall in love he said once."</p>
        <p>Alec hid a smile. He was finding out how the squire had educated Ruby. Though he was amused he was also very angry. "But, Ruby, sometimes a girl hates her husband after she has married him, and then-" • "Then what does she do? Oh, it must be dreadful; but, of course, she leaves him ? " "No, that is not right either," said Alec, beginning to feel puzzled how to teach Ruby. "It is wiser not to marry unless the girl loves her intended husband very much, as much as you love your father, Ruby." Ruby laughed. "I shall remember what you say, Cousin Alee. If papa said that I was to marry you I think I should not mind very much." Ruby's innocence was very charming, but also a little alarming. 'Wouldn't you? But your father will not tell you that." "But suppose he did," said Ruby, happily, "what would you say? Shall we pretend, and then you can tell me what you would like." Ruby laughed, but her laugh sounded sad in Alec's ears. She was certainly too ignorant to be safe in the world. How had the squire dared to bring her up like this? "Let us pretend," she tepeated.</p>
        <p>"Where are</p>
        <p>people rararied ? " "In church. Don't you ever go to church, Ruby ? " Ruby shook her head. "No, but I should like to go very much. Nancy goes sometimes when papa stays with me. Does it take long to get married?" "Not long. But you know, Ruby, it is for life, so it is a very serious thing." "Oh, yes, for always. But if you get tired of your husband, I suppose you must tell him so." "I am afraid that is of no use."</p>
        <p>in when we were married we would go and see the world. I should like to see Australia. Papa says I ought to wish to see it as I was born there. But I should like to see more of England, too. I remember my mother was always wishing to get back to England because she said perhaps there she might find papa." "Then he did not live with you i" said Alec, wonderingly. • "No. You know I told you that he came to Die after mamma died and said that we were going home now. I remember so well his asking me if I remembered his going away, but I didn't." "Why did he leave you ? "</p>
        <p>"I dOn't know. Yu said that wives did not leave their husbands."</p>
        <p>"But, Ruby, what should we do next after going about?" "We would go to balls and parties and dance, and I should like to see pictures, and we would be very good to poor people and make everybody happy, and papa would live with us, but not here." "Don't you love your home? I think it is the most lovely place in the world." Ruby shook her head. "Oh, I love it, too, but I feel like--" "Like what, Ruby?" "Like a prisoner. I have read in my history about prisoners. I feel like that." "I would let you do as you liked if it was good for you. Love teaches us to please people." "Yes, I love papa, and I know he loves me, but he will not let me do as I like."</p>
        <p>"He is so afraid that you should get into any harm. But he is letting you walk with me now, because he knows that you will not come to any harm with me."</p>
        <p>"Would he not let me walk with my other cousin? But I don't want to."</p>
        <p>know, Ruby.</p>
        <p>But we are only</p>
        <p>"I don't pretending." "Yes, but it is nice." "If we were married I would take you to Paris and to Venice. By the way, Ruby, how has your education been carried on? Do you know geography and history ? " "Papa has taught me what he thinks is necessary for a girl." - "But girls learn a great deal in these days." "Papa says that girls must sew well. I can make all my dresses, and I can draw a little because I love that, and I can sing, but I can't play much, only by ear. Papa could not teach me. He has forgotten, for he could play when he was young." "You know enough to be charming, Ruby, so we won't quarrel about your accomplishments." "Would you like me to know a great deal? If I were to try I might learn. Perhaps if papa knew you cared he would let me have masters." "I fear not, Ruby." "Perhaps he could not afford it. He often wishes he were richer." "But this estate is not poor." __ _</p>
        <p>"Oh, but papa has many claims. He often says so. Look, Alec, there is the 'village road; I don't think papa meant me to come here, but with you it is different."</p>
        <p>"Yes, quite different. Shall we come to the village? Let me call Bob and he will know." When Bob appeared he smiled at Miss Rubf. "Bob is my friend, Ruby, do you know that ? " "Oh, then, he is mine. Bob, can you tell me if we can get to the village this way. It is odd, but I have never been here before. Papa said we were to come back by the spintiy." "Best not do that, Miss Ruby," said Bob. "The squire wouldn't like it otherwise." "Bob is right. You see he has an old head on young shoulders." When they retraced their steps Ruby remarked: "When we get home I will shew you the old letters. Papa must have loved his mother very much when he was a boy, but now he never talks of her." "You have yet to find out, Ruby, that we are not the same when we are men as when we were boys." _ _ _</p>
        <p>&amp;7o they walked along talking of simple things, but Ruby shewed that she had thought in her limited way, using all advantages that were allowed her and making the best of them. Bob dropped behind, but had Alec not been absorbed he would have seen that the boy kept a sharp look-out on all the by-paths. Once when Alec was talking earnestly to the girl, whose eyes were fastened on his face, Bob noticed a slight rustle in the copse. It was very slight, but not too slight for the boy's sharp ears; besides, he was on the look-out. He noticed that James Felton was following them on a parallel line, but so well hidden that no one but Bob would have seen any trace of him. "What does that mean ? " thought Bob. "The squire knows Mr. Alec is here. He wouldn't have had no chance. Wish I could ask mother. Mr. Alec is sweet on Miss Ruby; he has no eyes for nothing else." The two went on, never thinking of the danger of being overheard. They.were both as happy as beings can be who are unconscious of everything but the happiness of the moment. As yet Alec felt that he was making a dangerous experiment, for he was helping Ruby to learn that which would only teach her the meaning of pain. He told her that there was a choice in love as in other things. Would it not be better for her to let her accept her fate than to teach her what true love means?</p>
        <p>"When will you come back here ? " she said at last, the first sad look Alec had seen creeping into her eyes. "When I go away I don't suppose I shall come back, Ruby. "When you go it will be worse for me, because I did not know before how nice it was to have a friend."</p>
        <p>"Do you trust me, child ? " asked Alec, pausing in the wood and feeling that he was deceiving himself as well as her. "I want you to be able to write to me and ask me to come to you if ever you want a friend."</p>
        <p>"Oh, yes, I trust you quite—quite. I will write to you very often." "I am going to do something which I think will help you in the future." "What is that ?" asked Ruby.</p>
        <p>"You know, perhaps, that -this property goes only to men folk till it comes to the last Beau- w ort h. "</p>
        <p>"That is not fair, because men have so much the best of things as it is. They can go about and do as they like." "In old days people did not think much of women, Ruby." "Do they now? Papa says lam only a child." "Tha last Beanworth can will away his property to anycae he likes best."_</p>
        <p>Oh, you are me last Deauwortnz vi i you leave it to someone you like ? " _</p>
        <p>"Yes. to someone I love."</p>
        <p>The: was a silence between them and Ruby looked down. After this she no longer played at being married. Alec hgd suddenly awakened in her that strange self-consciousness whiSh accompanies first love; it was as if he had taken away a veil from her eyes. But the silence was happiness. When they neared the house they found that the squire was beforehand with them.</p>
        <p>-"I took a 'short cut home, Ruby, for I thought you young people would return in time for your meal."</p>
        <p>"0 papa, we have had a lovely time " ; and Ruby put hex arm round her father's neck and kissed him.</p>
        <p>The squire looked at Ruby and smiled. When he looked at her there was no hardness in his face. After lunch, at which the other gentlemen did not appear, the squire said to Ruby: . 'Your cousin and I are going to do some business. You must go to your sitting-room, and when we have done we will come up." Ruby walked upstairs very thoughtfully. Life appeared under a new aspect now, and she meditated. "How could I have been so foolish—such a child—as to pretend about being married? He thinks, as papa does, that I am a child, or he would never have said what he did."</p>
        <p>The little god of Love had sent an arrow by chance, and he had hit two human beings with one arrow.</p>
        <p>As Alec followed the squire to his study he quickly reviewed the situation. Could he hope that the squire would relent and let him try to win Ruby for himself? No, whatever was the motive for the arrangement it must be a strong one. James Felton, for some unexplained reason, was anxious to marry a girl he did not love. But what was best for Ruby? Before he could settle anything the squire spoke. "Here, Beauworth, are the papers about which I spoke. Have you made up your mind about the property ? " "I have been thinking deeply on the subject." The squire's face expressed a- certain degree of anxiety, but Alec did not notice this. He was wondering if he could, by chance, save Ruby from the clutches of James Felton.</p>
        <p>"I wish everything to be perfectly legal, and that there shall be no disputing of y-Tur signature.* "Why should there be ? " "Well, you know that people will dispute any- thing in these days." "If I sigi:.! this-"</p>
        <p>"You will leave• what can be of no use to you, unless you marry, and then, of course, I shall be the first to wish you to revoke this paper." "If I could but marry Ruby, then--" The squire did not raise his eyes. He looked haggard and Alec was struck by the expression on his face.</p>
        <p>"No, that is impossible." "But may I say one thing? If you are, being unduly pressed, and if it would help you for me to refuse this signature, then only give me a hint and I shall be only too glad." "No, no, it would not help me at all," hurriedly answered the squire. "Years ago I promised Mr. Felton that Ruby should marry his son if nothing came to hinder it. To refuse your signature would not alter it in the least, it would only make more complications." "If I were to die without being married, then Ruby would be the better for it ? " _ _</p>
        <p>"that is only a remote possibility. My dear fellow, I sincerely hope that you will soon marry and that you will have children to succeed you. The squire looked more cheerful as he saw that Alec was yielding. Ruby's face rose before Alec more beautiful and captivating because she could be nothing to him. Ruby had taken his heart by storm. He wished to protect her against all these men who wished to sell her or—What was the real reason? "But suppose something happened, and that Ruby refused to marry James Felton, what then ? "</p>
        <p>"If something hap- pened I would say try your fortune."</p>
        <p>"I will sign," said Alec, after a pause. The squire breathed a low sigh of relief, though he spoke as if the matter were of little consequence when Alec's signature was witnessed by the two old servants.</p>
        <p>"What doe 3 it matter?" thought Alec, still feeling a little puzzled. "If I marry it will be altered; if I die, then Ruby is the right person to inherit it."</p>
        <p>When they were alone again Alec remarked: • . "I don't feel sure that Ruby will be benefited." --crOf course not ; but the wnole thing ie a precau— tion. Come, we must •do something to amuse you better than walks ivith Ruby. She is a dear child, but young in her ways. I expect you found her very much behind other voun?. ladies ? " She has certainly been kept back, but she is: young still, and there is time. "I ought to tell you that to-day Ruby will have to be told about James. I want you to help nie to —to prepare her." "But surely I-- I did not understand that," exclaimed Alec. "It is too cruel."</p>
        <p>"She is young, and she will do what I tell her. Indeed, Beauworth, I wish I had known you yearn ago, then I should have shaped a different course."</p>
        <p>"Butevennow it i 3 not too late. Ruby is not, married; she knows nothing about it If you ,</p>
        <p>"No, I assure you it is too late. Now I must thank you for being kind to my little girl." "Won't you spare her, Sir Harry? You love her, then throw all other considerations to the winds and let Ruby decide her own fate. If she likes this young man—" "It is too late," repeated the squire, with a strange look in his eyes. "it is quite—quite too late.</p>
        <p>"No, not too late. Think of her happiness, think of her youth, her innocence; it cannot be too late."</p>
        <p>"You are pleading for yourself," said the squire, a little scornfully. There were steps heard in the hall and the squire's voice changed, "Thank you for your signature. It is quite formal, but-"</p>
        <p>"Then I tell you honestly, squire, that I shall try to influence Ruby." Sir Harry smiled. "Shall you? I doubt if you will be able. Ah, here is James Felton. I was telling Beauworth that he will act as guardian to Ruby."</p>
        <p>James looked at the table. . "I see you are busy." "No, we have done," said the squire, slowly. "Sir Harry is a very busy man," said James,</p>
        <p>laughing. - "Perhaps you will like to smoke, Beauworth?" said the squire, rising. "Thank you, don't think about me." Alec' anger was rising visibly. He had a feeling that James was triumphing over him. James and the squire went out together. "Your extraordinary run of luck has not for-. saken you, Sir Harry?" said Mr. Felton, when the three were in the old library. "So you have really done it safe and sound. By Jove! yob are mighty clever." "It's your coolness that takes in fellows, squire,l said James. "Now that you have got what you want, won't. you leave me Ruby ? " said Sir Harry, looking pale and speaking very slowly. "Well, the Governor and I have been discussing the very question, but we have come to the conclu- sion that it's impossible." " Impossible Why ? " "You can't be trusted when that girl is in ques- tion, that's the truth! You might get a separation and Heaven knows what else, and baulk us after all."</p>
        <p>"You would not shew fight," sneered the squire. "It is hard to sacrifice the girl." "Then throw it up and let--" "No, no, it is too late, I know that, butt' you might have left her with me." "Come, don't act the fond parent too much with us! It don't go down easy," said James. "I'm not acting. I wish I were." "Never mind, we have waited pretty long for interest, eh?" "Ten years," said the squire; "and so many things might have happened in that time." "Yes, so they might, but they didn't. You see we might have been killed or died of some epidemic or otherwise have disappeared—but we were mercifully spared. "You staked your play on the chance," said Felton.</p>
        <p>"Yes, I did." "And we staked ours on the opposite chance and we have won. But you are awfully clever all the same, Sir Harry." "You had better be satisfied with chance. Let the young fellow alone." "Oh, I say! Are you turning tender-hearted? That would be a new thing. And now I am going to woo and win the fair Ruby."</p>
        <p>,(To be continued.)</p>
