      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TRAMWAYS FOR WARRINGTON. SPECIAL MIE-7E.--TiNG OF THE COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRAMWAYS FOR WAR- RINGTON. SPECIAL MIE-7E.--TiNG OF THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington. Tovvneouncil was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, Warrington, on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presided, and there werealso i—Alderman Wright (deputy Mayor), Aldermen Francomb, Fairclough, Councillors Heaton, John Evans, James Evans, A. J. Jolley, Duncan, Bennett, Lee, Jolley, :!:haw Green, Rogers, Baird, Ellison, Shilicock, Greening, Plumpton, John Roberts, F. R Roberts, Tinnien, Smethurst, Burton,- with Mr. J. Lyon Whittle town clerk), Mr. S. Wilson (deputy (town clerk), Mr T. Longdin (borough surveyor), Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. J. Su;cliffe (assistant surveyor). Tile meeting had been called to pass or otherwise the following resolutions :—(1) " That an application be made by memorial to the Board of Trade for a provi- gional order to authorize the Council to construct and. work tramways in some or other of the follow- ing streets within the borough, viz :—Winwick- road, Winwick-street, 13orsetnarket-street, Bridge- street, Bridge-foot, Si, James'-street, Knutsford- road, Wilderspool-causeway, Old London-road, Liverpool-road, Sankey-street, But':erma,ket-street, Irlam-street, Cnurch-street, Manchester-road, Scotland- road, Academy-street, Wrsey-street, Rylands-street, Suez-street, Bold-street 4 Be wsey -street, Padgate-lane, Winmarleigh-strest new street off Academy-street, Pal- myra-lqu are, and such other trainvvays as the Council may consider necessary, and that the common seat of the Council be affixed to such memorial." (2) That the Town Clerk be instructed to take all necessary steps in carrving tne fo,egoing resolutioa into effect :" (3) "That *the pans, sections, diagrams, estimates, and specifications be prepared bv Messrs. Preece and Cardew, ot 13, Queen Anne's-gate, Westminster, in respect of the , tramways named or, referred to in the foregoing resolu- tion, and be deposited in due course with the Board of Trade."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>SHORT OF QUORUNI.</head>
        <p>SHORT OF QUORUNI.</p>
        <p>The nvleting was suannocted to cJnimenco at 6 o'c'ock, but a difficulty was experienceA in birasing a quorum. Twenty-four members of the Council were required ta be prment but at 620 only twenty-two members had. put in an appearance. Messengers were sent out and at 6.25, Alderm.in Wright arrived. I' he M&amp;YOR t tea to Met cnair, wid at the suggestion of Councillor 833. w Green, he read the roll of members. Lie said they were only short of one member, and they would proceed with,tivt consideratlon of the subject in the hope that w:tnin afa tninutes they would have the necessary quaruin. Councillor Barton F. h )rtiv afterwaris arrived and the requisite numb n. hivinz now been obtained, the Comic)l coutinneci to toe consideration of the subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>REPORC OF ME FOWN CLERK.</head>
        <p>REPORC OF ME FOWN CLERK.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR read Eh, fiiowing repart, which load ben prepared by the Town Clok:—"The tramway scheme is described as conei-tin,g of eleven tramways, but may be sh Irtly d scribed as bur main iines,one runningirom near the borough boundary at milderspool,over the level crossings te Warrington Bridge. where it is joined by another line running from Kniitsford-road, past the Old Warps to the bridge. Another line runs from the War- rington bridge no Bride-street, turtle int) Rylands-et., and through Palmyra-square into Winmerleigh-street, then along Sankey-street to the borough boundary in Liverpool-road. The other line starts at the borough boundary at Longford Bridge,runs down Win wick-road, turns into Scotland-road, and on through Buttermarket- street and Church-street past the Cemetery to Bruche Bridge. There isa branch tramway from the Cemetery along Padeate-lane to Padgate Bridge. There will also be two tranaways, which will not be used for truffle, running along Academy-street and Mersey-street, for the purpose of obtaining accees to the old gas works, where the tramcars will be kept. I have no doubt that the motive power will be electric, but power will be ob- tained in the Order to use either mechanical or anima power. It should be explained that the Tramways Com- mittee called in to advise them Majer Cardew (of the 'firm of Preece and Cardew. who was formerly with the Board of Trade, and, therefore, well able to advise toe committee as to the best routes and system to be adopted. Great activity is being displayee in the con- struction of light railways near the borough. In addi- tion to the ligot railway of which tiotice has been given to benonstructed between Northwich and Warriegton, other schemes are afleat for the construction of a light railway from Warrington to Knutsford, and also to Lymtn, and within the list few days the Corporation have le-en applied to for their Rapport to a scheme for the censrruction of a light reilway from Warrington, through Walton, Acton Grange,and Norton, to Runcorn. The committee have also heard that the promoters of the light railway on the north side Iron E urlestown and St. Helens proposed to bring a branch of that line not only to the borough, but through it, and we might have found ourselves in the same position as some other boroughs in Lancashire in which light railway companies have obtained powers to carry lines of railways thr vagh those boroughs with the result that the town would have. lost for ever the pecuniary advantages which tram- ways as a rule ensure. Moreover, in one town a light railway company which had obtained powers refused to allow the Corporation tramcars to run on their lines. It cannot be said that the tranaweys are being construc- ted for the purpose of keeping out these companies, for the Corporation last year decided to apply for powers on the earliest opportunity. In 1882 they gave their coo- sent to the construction of tramways within the borough, subject to conditions then agreed upon. At , the same time we are all agreed that we must keep Out these private companies at any cost. If the Order is granted it does not follow that the Corporation are bound to construct the whole of the tramways pro- posed. No doubt the first tramway to be constructed will be the one from Wilderspeol, and before any money is spent careful estimates will be prepared not only of the cost ot construction and equipment, but of the prob able revenue to be derived." The MAYOR said his attention had been called to the fact that certain streets which bad been originally ache- clulrd had been struck out, but they had been advised all through by Mujer Cardew that in the first place they should not overburden the apedication, which covered all the main arteries of the borough, and no scheme would be accepted or permitted which did not in the first instance include the main arteries. There were parts of the town whicp were populous, and which would ultimately have to bosomed by a tramway system. It would be a mistake to include all these subsidiary por- tions of the town in the preliminary application. If the scheme were overburdened it might be absolutely re- fused. If they once got permission to lay down tram- ways, and supplied the main arteries, there was no reason why. at an early date, provided the scheme proved to be a financial success, which they hoped it would be, the subsidiary portions should not be made. When he reminded the Council that the total expendi- ture involved in the scheme before them—and which was essential, for they must supply the main arteries— was £170,000, he thought they would agree that it was big enough to begin with. l'he cost of other applications would be very much smaller, and they would be able to see in the meanthne how (be other portions of the scheme proceedeo financially,and be governed by results. He mesle that statement now becawe he knew certain members of the Council felt disappointed that certain streets which had been originally scheduled for the tramway system hid been struck out for the purposes of the application. He thought after his explanation they would see that it would be a mistake to overburden the scheme, wpocially after the expert advice they had had. He would new move the first vsolution. Coencillor SHAW GREEN seconded. If it was found that any portion of the scheme was undesirable it could be dropped. If the Corporation obtained the power they would be able to prevent any other bady from in- terfering with their own town. Councillor PLIMIPTON said he should propose an amendment that some of the streets which had been crc ssed out r f the schedule be included in the scheme. He did not think any of the thoroughfares re (faired tramways • more than Bewsey-road, Folly-lane, and Lovelr-lane. At the present time no luses ran in those districts, and the result was that the public experienced great difficulty in getting to town Large works were to be erected at the top of Lovele -lane, and the result would be that a large number of buildings wou'd he erected. At the present time buildings were continu thy being put up in Levele-lane and streets off Lovely-lane. A great many residents had been looking forward to the introduction of tramways with the greatest of pleasure and anticipation, and it would come as a great disap- pointment when they hoard that the district was not in- cluded in the scheme. It tees eltogether unfair that the neighbourhood ehould be excluded. There would he an extra rate which the public would have to pep without any special advantage, and he moved, therefore, as an amendment, "That Folly-lane, Bewsey-road,and Lovely- lane be included in the scheme." Councillor GREENING observed that he came into the room preoared to vote against the resolution, but alter the explanation which had teen given by the Mayor he should support it. When they had obtained the power to which the Mayor had referred he took it that they could run through the town where they liked?</p>
        <p>Councillor PLI3INIPTON : There will have to be another application. The MAYOR replied that was so but having got the major aopiication granted, and ;he system in opera- tion, the second applicati )n would be purely a matter of form. Cnuncillnr GREENING, continuing, s tid he was not afraid of the expense of £l7o,ooo,because tramways paid all over the country, and Warrington was not so excep- tionally circumstanc'id that they would not pay here. Ws impression was that they would pay very well indeed. councillor SMETHURST seconded the arnwidment, and said he had intended to raise the quegtion which Coun- cillor P:umpton had raise 1. From 1,000 to 1,500 houses were being left out of the scheme, whereas it was pro- posed to go beyond the Cemetery to Brut.she Br:dge,</p>
