        <p>where thrra were comna-ativly !err hotly's. R: d not see the neoessity ,f ui g h:yon,l the Ctne,cery, and why was it necessary to go up Padgate-iane ? Eus Supposed the tram wass would cost £lO,OOO per mile, and it would be 13,-,ter trttucP, iasteau it .40oi! witerl thers were scarcely aay Mises to. co Is'ruct a tram line down 12tvis-5-,nad and L wely-lane neighb?urhood. It would be no benefit to him IJr the trams to gs dawn Bewsey-road, they would te more a nuistnze than any- thing else. (Laughter.) Aiderrian FRANCOMR said he ha,i vary are it pleasure in supporting the resolution. -Ho had, however, a great. deal of sympathy with th's remarks which had been male by Clanclior Plat Tipton. Tan route he named would he an excellent one, but if they went in for all the thoroughfares which were originallr named in the agenda they might lose the wh-sle Bch une, and that would 4a a very serious )Os. It would be a pity to stop. the tramways a the Cemetery, as buildings were being erected up to Buh Bridge. (Haar, hear.) Councillor SMETHURST : Yea bat in the district I mentionel the rP3(I9,'S are up and tenanted. Alderman Faartcomn : Ws might as well go to the Borough boundary when wo have gone as far as the Cemetery.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor BENNETT said he should al@o support the resolution on the broad ground that it ViREI desirabl- the municipality should have in its own control anything so nearly affecting the general welfare of the public as the means of locomotion. At the same time, he was hound to admit thst he had a great deal of symp any with some of the criticisms whicl had been urged in regard tl the present sceeme,and if he thought that by support- ing the resolution he should be pledged to all its derails he should find some difficulty in doing so. It seem dto , him that the whole of the routes might be subject to a certain amount of criticism, and with all possible sympathy with the i,cheme in its broad sew et he thought it w )uld have been better if they had had more informatio ion the subject and had been allowed a longer time in which to digest it. Be was disappointed to find how many narrow places were eche Jilted. He thoeuht if the doctrine he had preached had been pursued this question of narrow places would not have been such a. burning one. While he bowed to the superior wig tom of the advisers of the Council, there was a great deal to be said in favour of the amendment. He referred more particutar'y to Orford-lane, while on behalf of Padgate, which had not always b .en treated with the considera- tion it deserved.the remarks about thescanty plipulation were not correct. Alderman Franconab frau put the matter more cirrectiv when he said that bui!dings were springing up on all hakds, and on the whole, it was perhaps as well to go to the eorough benndary. Referr- ing to the m,theds adopted in carrying out a tramway scheme, and speaking about overhead wires he said that personally he did not like tnem. Teey were al eye-sore if not a possible danger, and he thought that before any scheme was finally decided upon each of the three alternaeive methods of locoinotion—overhead, un ler- ground,or motors—it should be thoroughly thrashed out, and that the one which was