        <p>Councillor BENNETT said he was under the impression that L verpo.A had the control of the whole of the means of locomotion. Although the tramway scheme involved an expenditure of £170,000, in the long run, instead of 13ein:z a burden, he trams would be found t) be a mtterial source of income. Councillor F. it. itonEar said he was of opinion that tin word 4 in the resouttioa, " and suck other tram- ways as the Council may consider necessary," shuull be eliminated. Toe L:c .1 Government Botri might ask them tn_ define what was meant by that clause.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : The Town Clerk advises that the words should come out as they would oJt be satin:lone:3 in the :application. Councillor HEATON in supporting the resolution said it was desirable that the Corporation should have the control of the tram cr574 on the live main roads in the t'iwn, viz , the Wilderspool-road, Bautsford-road, Man- chest T-road, Padgate-road and Winwick-road,in view of till introduction of- light railways. He had not the slightest hesitation in t•aying gat if they got the Order from the I,' CLI (Inviinnaeot Board, what this mover of the amendment asked would follow in due cours:.: They cou'd not do wroog in supporting the resolution.</p>
        <p>Councillor TioitioNT said the general principle of pro- viling tram vacs was almitted but he was somewhat in a difficulty in de•i ling upon the matter. Consilering the i rsnc t the scheme it would have been as well if those who had had the matter in hand had provided a chart of the town showing the rout. fie contessed that if he vot d f o* the resolution he did not understand the scheme. He was sorry to SO9 M trket-g ate crossed out as ha believel in through routes ;to ,have round- about mules was a mistake. If the scheme were carried out the users of the trams would be obliged to walk from one tram' terminus to anotnor. While he' was pre- pared to vote for the general principle he was of opinion that a little mote information should have been given as to the possibility of finding rr.kore suitable routes. Were they compeUed to carry out the whole of the scheme if 'power was obtained ?</p>
        <p>The Mivon : No, sir. Councillor TINNION : But we eannot increase without apnlicail on Toe MAYOR : No. If we imertest the'suggestion just mide as to running the lines past Market Gate it would damn the whol- scheme. The Board absolutely refused ten years ago to allow tramways to go past Market Gate, but the matter will corns out right when we slo 'come more impregnated with Counlillor Bennett's ideas about wideniiii the thor 111.4hfa,reJ. Hoar, hear.)</p>
        <p>Councillor A. J. JOLLEY remarked- that he would like to a,k a questiln with regard to t,te estimate. TOO Cost was said b £170,000. He should lik one informa- tion as to whetrier in:chat figure was included the so4t offlayiug the various lines, or whether any provision was made for widening any of the streets at prleat to 9 narrow ?</p>
        <p>Tne MAYOR : I am glad to to'l you that therm are only three plac.ls—three small placea—that will require widening, and the expenditure involved will be com- paratively small. _</p>
        <p>• Conoci,lor A. J. JOLLEY : Is the cost included in this estimate ? The MAYJEt : Yes. It is included in the estimite, which is only an approximate one based roughly on the cost per mile on the experience of other towns. Councillor JAMES Eyass pointed out that considerable nonev was to be spent in Bold-street. Suez-street and Itylands-street th,t might bs appropriated to the widen- ing of Bridge-stromt. If that were done it would do away with the difficulty which had been complaiued of at M rket Gate. Couescillor tin:mere:4 said as there was no chaoce of his amendment being c4rrie:l he would withdraw it. amuscillor SAIETEIURST approved. Toe MAYOR lan ObliZei to you both, and at the same time ask you to accept our assurance t sat what we are nroposine is merely to get our fool in so to sva.k. If this hCh9 133 succeeds or so oh portions as we commence with, theca is no reason why those who wish the auxin u distri :Is to be supplie I ea rly shoal I not press the matter f Tyr ard. TllO resolution was then pat and carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>Tne proposals instructing till To ant Clerk to take all noce.ssiry steps in earryies! the f aregoinz resolution into eff,!ct, and th anpoimovnt effitaisrs. l'r•!eci and Car- devr.of Weitmins-er.t ) prepare see' i Ins diagrams, estimates and sp.ciftcations, were algo agreed to. The Council then rose.</p>
        <p>Successful Sale of Work. Opening by the Mayor. A sale of work is an annual event in connection with the Immanuel Reformed Church of England, Bold-street, but its recurrence each November does not in any way slacken the efforts put forth by the workers in connection with it nor are there any Rive; of diminished interest and support. Depend- ing exclusively upon voluntary contributions for the support of the ministry and the maintenance of the church and school the finances sometimes give cause for anxiety and the sale of work is held in order to meet any deaciences that may take place. This year there was an adverse balance of £23 which it was hoped to wipe off. The promoters generally succeed in making the sale an attractive one and this year's proved no exception to the rule. There were numerous stalls tastefully set out and in addition cozy tea rooms had been provided where "the cup that cheers" was gracefully served by daintily attired maidens. The opening ceremony was performed by the Mayor and Mayoress last Wed- nesday afternoon. Amongst those present were the Rev. E. T. and Mrs. Reed, Rev. J. and Mrs. Yonge, Rev. J. F. Matthews, Rev. J. T. Schofield, Rev. Williams, and Mr. Page. - The following is a list of the stalls and stallholders : Mrs Reed's stall, Mrs Reed, Mrs Norman, Mrs Smith, and Miss Monk; Congregational atall, Mesdames Atherton, Brosdbent, Armstrong and Parsons, Miss Tetlow, arid Messrs Atherton and Parsons. School stall, Mr A Gandy. Mesdames H Landon J Lloyd, and A Dickens, and Misses Minards and Lloyd; Christian i-ndavour stall. Misses Lees, Booth, Hedge, Astibrouk Jenkins, and Mason, and Mesdames W H Dickens and A. Landm ; flower and fruit stall, Mr Benjamin Podmore at d Mrs Smithson ; gentlemen's stall, Messrs T Allen, E d J Allen and Stewart ; ad sertisement stall, Messrs Holland, Broadeeat, Smithson, J Lloyd, S Heald, J H6clze, J Heron, and members of Men's Bible Class, with Miss Allen ; refreshment stall, Mesdames T Allen, E Allen. H Gandy, Eccles, Yule and Misses Leach, Carriage and Hothe ;tea room, Mesdames Hallwood, Skelhorn, A.still and F Lloyd. After a hymn had been sung and prayer offered by the Rev. J. Yonge, The Rev. E. T. REED, in a few introductory re- marks said on behalf of the congregation connected with Emmanuel Church, it gave him great pleasure to extend a very cordial welcome to his Worship the Mayor and the Mayoress ot Warrington. (Ap- plause.) They welcomed the Mayor for two reasons. First, because he was the chief magistrate of the borough, and by virtue of his responsible office the representative of law and order; of that law which Lowell nescribed in one of his poems as" The deep august foundation, whereon Peace and Justice rest." There was nothing antagonistic between the rightful administration of the law which was the function of the magistrate and the proclamation of the Gospel which was the function of tbe Church. They were supplementary the one to the other, and as the common law of England was founded upon the Bible. it was obvious that both magistrates and churches derived their authority from the same source. It was appropriate' therefore, that the one should sympathise with and support the other, and it was because of this expression of sympathy with the work of one of ths churches of the town that they welcomed the Mayor and Mayoress. They welcomed him, secondly, because of the public ser- vices which he had rendered to the town. What an amount of gratuitous labour was expended for the benefit of their fellow-creatures by those who sat upon the various public bo lies! Many of the comforts which they enjoyed were due to their labours on our behalf. Amongst those who had so laboured in Warrington Mr. Alderman Roberts had been conspicuous both for ability and industry, and the honour which had recently been conferred upon im was a proof of the confidence which was re- posed in him by the community. While, however, there was much labour expended by those who sat on public bodies, it should also be remembered that a very large proportion of the work done in connec- tion with Christian churches and other Christian agencies was also performed by voluntary workers. If it were not for these, their churches, Sunday schools, missions and other agencies would have to be closed. So, in coming there that' day the Mayor and Mayoress were recognizing the self-denying labours of the many workers who had not only been instrumental in preparing the sale of work, Ibut who largely carried on the work of the church, Sunday school and other • agencies all the year round. For these two reasons, therefore, they wel- comed the Mayor and Mayoress and he had now much pleasure in asking the Mayor to occupy the chair during the remainder of the proceedings. The lklaYoa said he was very glad to accept the invi- tation to open that sale of work for several rear ins. One reason was because of the very high opinion ha had formed of Mr. Reed's personal character, and of the way he had done his work as minister of Emmanuel Church. (Hear, hear.) He looked upon Mr. Reed as one of the forward men in spiritual work in Warrington, and while he (the Mayor) as perhaps, an advanced thinker,did not go the whole length Mr. Reed did, still he could not fail to admire the courage with which he pursued the convictions he believed in, no matter at what ccst or risk. He had a good deal of sympathy with the work carried on at Bold-street. OF course they all knew he belonged to a church which many many years ago broke itself away from the trammels of the Established Chuech. They at the Emmanuel Church had done the same thin.. It was not a fitting accasion for him asi'Mayor of Warrington, to enter into any discussion as to the reasons which had caused it. There were reasons and sound reasons too, and he was not quite sure whether they at the Emmanuel Church were the right people who ought to have left the church. He thought it would have been better for the church and the country if those who had been the cause of the dissensions had left the Church. (Hear. hear.) But Nonconformist as be was it was no plea ure for him to dwell on the dissensions which existed in the Established Church. He would tar better prefer to recognise the magnificent work which the church was doing to-day, and was still capable of doing by the marvellous set&amp; sacrifice which characterised thousands of her clergy. In regard to the Emmanuel Church. as a Nonconformist he could !perhaps appreciate better than possibly a mem- ber of the Established Cnurch could do, the difficulties they had to contend with, inasmuch as they had to rely on free-will offerings and the personal self-sacrifice of their own conerezation and the assistatace of friends outside, in order that their good work might go on. He bad a friend—perhaps the best friend he had had—who raid that money worry was the worst worry a man c mild be afflicted with. He was also of opinion th meney worry war the worse worry a church cou'd be afflicted with. (Hear, bear.) He could appreciate and understand from the appeal whin had been issuid the difficulties they bad to contend with in order that they might keep on with the goad work of their church. It was the duty of each individual con- nected with it to do his or her best to make that difficulty as easy as possible, and also the duty of those who had strong sympathies with them to come forward and help them to the best of their ability. That was one of the ground.' why he was present that day to d what little he could to help them. In the first place as Mayor, he was anxious to support any gord cause, no matter from whit gu%Tter it might proceed; and secondly as a citizen and Nonconformist who, having true sympathiA with the lines of teaching. they adopted he hoped to do some little tohelp them in that direction. (Aholause.) The MAYORESS, who had a cordial reception, stated that when she received the invitation from Mr. Reeelohe thought all he required her to do WaS to spend a little money with them, and that her hushand had often told her,she was very well able to do. (Laughter.) Unfortun- ately for her Mr. Reei would not let her off quite so easily. She thought she need only say that the great devotion and earnestness with which they carried on their work had her sympathy and she hoped they would realise what they needed. Her husband and herself would be very glad to bear that on Friday night they had got even more than they had ventured to ask for. With her hest wishes that the sale migat be an unquali- fied success she had great pleasure in declaring-the sale open. (Applause.) The Rev. E. I'. REED proposed a lenity vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress for their kindness in coming to cp.n the sale of work. He wished to take that opportunity of announcing that he had received donations amounting to £l5 53. from several friends. (Applause.) He very much appreciated the kind weeds of the Mayor and Maynress, and he thought he might say on behalf of the congregation that they had a like feeling for them. He din not think it was out of place to say that the strain in order to pay their way had been very intense at ti ties, hut he had noticed amongat all their workers during the past few years a gradual increase of interest and effTt in order to make their church what they thought it oueht to he in the town— a greater power for good than ever. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Peon seconded the vote of thanks velich was car- ried with acclamation. The MAYOR, in acknowledging the vote of thanke,said he most ardently hcped that everything they did would be fully le Wised so that they might start the next part of their work free from any debt. The Rev. J. T. SCR ,FIELD having pronounced the benediction the sale commenced. Thursday's Proceedings. Last Thurclay,the sale was opened by Miss Naylor, of cuetelen Hall. Thelwall, who was accompanied by Mr It A Naylor, and amongst others present were the Rev E T Reed and Mrs Reed, sad the Revs J F Matthews, J S Hughes and C Finch. Brief addresses were delivered by Mr R A Naylor and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>VILLAGE INDUSTRY.</head>
        <p>A VILLAGE INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>About three miles from Cambridge lea valuable object lesson to the nation as to how the people may he kop: on Uri land, and be thereby prevented from crowding into already over-oopulated cities. twenty-four or twenty- five years ago a farmer turned his attention earnestly te fruit•farming, and made it pay froni tne ou'set. A few acres sufficed at fist ; to-day some thquaanis of acres are under fruit culture in till little old-world villatle of Histain and the adjacent district. What was the secret ot his great success ? It lay mainly in the foresight, ski!i, energy and hard work in adap'ing means to an end.</p>
        <p>A market was found for the fruit by the erection of a mothl faltory, rsinarkable for its equipment, daintiness and up-t)-date methods. The fresh fruit is made into Jam in silver-lined pans the sante day it is picked, with all the luscious taste of the fruis presertrol intact by the adlition of sugar. Ripe fruit juices are alsn employed to imp ort a thlicio,s and delicate II tvour to Chivers' Geti Medal Table Je'liei, which it one reason for their great supeiiority. We have said that the succer4s of this village industry is owing in great measure to the cleanli- ness and purity of the methods employed in the factory. An Alderman and J. P., writes to the firm : Doubtless the dsily freshly gathered fruit his given pcur ft.m vc.e high p,sition it enjoys cornna,..l with town tactori-s." An M.P. said in a spoech in 1894 : "I never had better Strawberry JAM. lam quite certain that so long as such exc-llence is m tint tin-.d the prosperity of the firm will c )ntinuP."</p>
        <p>Chi-Vert:l Gold 111-dal J4.ma and Jodie; are Rohl hv Gror n 1 S'ores through:ldt the United li'mgdotn. S. Chivers &amp; Soni, Fliaton and C tinhridga.</p>
