      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WARTNGrON LII3ERAL OL_UB.</head>
        <p>WARTNGrON LII3ERAL OL_UB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL IVIE1::TING.</p>
        <p>There was a large attoodance in the Liberal Club on Monday evening when the annual general meet- ing of the members was held. In the absence of the president (sir. A. H. Urosfield) Councillor Tinnion was voted to the chair. Amongst those present were Uou icillor William Pierpoint. Cluncillor Piumptoa, Councillor F R Robert's, Mr William Roberts, Mr John Harrison, Mr George Wellington, Mr J Critchley, &amp;tr J l Warren, Mr Locker, Mr T Pemberton (hon secretary) lnd others.</p>
        <p>TITIC REP3RT.</p>
        <p>The Hon Secretary Submitted his annual state- ment and balance sheet. It showed that improve- ments had been made ,at the Club and after meeting all liabilities a balance was carried forward to the following year. The CHAIRMAN is moving the adoption of. the report reviewed the work of tne com- mittee during the past year. He was pleased to see such a representative meeting and the keen interest which was taken in the management of the club. He thought they were able to congratulate themselves upon the prosper- ous condition of the club, which was largely due to the splendid and energetic secretary they had in Mr. Pemberton. (Hear, hear.) During the year several improvements had been effected in the read- ing room, the billiard room, the bar, and the bowl- ing green. Much as they regretted the separation of Mr. Mitchell, their late manager, he thought the members would admit that the new steward—Mr. Lamb—and his wife had fulfilled their duties to the satisfaction of every member of the club. (Hear. hear.) The report was unanimously approved of. THE PRESIDENT. _</p>
        <p>Councillor WILIAM PistiPOINT proposed the re-election of Mr. A. 11. Crosfield as president of .the club stating that uo words of his were Deeded to commend his name for their acceptance and on reerring to the sas fac- tory position of the club at the present time he spoke of the difficulties which had been enc -watered in the pat. The proposal was at 0114 time seriously consilered of doing away with`the club entirely but he was glad that that proo.sal was not carrid. They had now overcome their difficulties and were in a very satisfactory position. The balance sheet which had jus;; been read reflected the greatest credit on eververte concerned. (Hear. bear.) Mr. WILLIAM ROBERTS sesonded the proposition and referral to the valuable services which had been rendered to ..;he club by the late manager, Mr. Mitchell. (Hear, hear.) The CHAIRMAN said he had not gone into that matter that evening toecause it was to be the subject of a special meeting when a tangible recognition would be made of Mr Mitchell's services. (Hear. hear.) Councillor PLIMPTON supported the proposition which was carried with acclamation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>VICE-PRESIDENTS.</head>
        <p>THE VICE-PRESIDENTS.</p>
        <p>Vcting was taken by h-illot for three vice-preiidents. The three retiring gent-km.l9n wPre rc—lected,viz : Coun- cillors Pierpnt, Tinniqn and Plumpton.</p>
        <p>HON TREASURER AND HON SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. CO—Ulle.illOr JOHN CHOSFIELD was unanimously appointed hon. treasurer on the proposition of the Chairman. Mr. J. Ccitch'ey proposed and Mr George Wallington seconded the appointment of Mr. Thomas Pemberton as hors tsecrwary. Councillor F. R. Rob .rts in supporting the rosolution said Mr. Pemlonton was an ideal secretary. (Fi,ar, hear.) The proposition was unanimously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
