      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MRS. ORMISTON CHANT AT</head>
        <p>MRS. ORMISTON CHANT AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Meetings in the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>Services were conducted in the Parr Halt on Senday by Mrs. L, Oriniston Chant, of London, under the auspices of the Primitive Methodists of the town. Mrs. Chant has gained a reputation as a reformer, and it was therefore, not to be wondered, that the services were largely attended In the morning at 10.45 an' t in the evening at 8 o'clock, sermons were preached, and in the afternoon Mrs Chant gave an address on The present outlook." Mr. R. A. Naylor presided. The pro- ceedings comrnerced with the singing of a hymn, atter which prayer was offered by Mrs. Chant. and the Rev. C. Finch read a lesson. Mrs. A. Calvert gave an effec- tive interpretation of the.solo". The Worker" (Gmnod). The CHAIRMAN, during the curse of a brief speech, said that for Warrington, it must be a great occasion to receive a visit from a lady so well known as Mrs Chant. They all very muc'a admired the work of Mrs. Chant in the past. She had taken a very decided stand and she had always stood up for truth and righteousness. (Hear, hear.) Like everyone who took up such a position she had had to suffer very considerably in consequence. She had been abused and very much misrepresented, hut she had to day the satisfaction of knowing that she bad done something for the cause of the good Masler whom they all desired to serve and honour. The Rev. C. FINCH then Wade several announcements. after which, Mrs. CHART delivered hsr address. She said it was very important that at the end of the century they should look out upon their position as a nation and ask themselves vary solemnly upon what it was they were depending for present strength and future strength be- cause they were all of them trustees. They were trustees holding something for those who came after them. Their fathers and mothers held that trust before them and they had sometimes heel to be very sorry that they had not held it better and sometimes they thought that they held it very well. They had stronger influence with which to hold their trust telly than they ever had before. They had more sources of strength and more sources of weakness. They had greater armies for good, but they had stronger forces of evil to contend against than at any time in the world's history. At the end of the cen- tury it was necessary to take stock as it were, and ask what it was they had preserved and told against al enemies that should be a grand and meeniecent leeacy or capital to those who succeeded them. It was a truism to say that the strength of a chain was in its weakest linkonci it-was the smallest child present that afterneon with the temptations and hindr snces around it, that was the strength of the national chain. The present outlook was this—that they were in very great danger of losing sight of certain necessity qualities be- cause they could do things more easily thee those who preceded them I' was just as necessary to-day to train their children Co he honest, patient and to face difficulties. In a cuntortable age when luxuries were much more within reach of all men and women than be- fore peop!e were Ivry apt to let opportunities for good, slip, aud there was a great clanger of loaing some of the qualities that Jesus Christ set before thrm as absolutely necessary to the life that now is and the life that was to come. Mrs. Chant dwelt at considerable leng:h on the importance of a pure home life, and in cmclusion urged her hearers to maintain their dim Ales as best they could. Many a valuable legacy of merciful work had been handed on from the past, but the highest of all charity, the most beautiful gift of love they could hand on to the next genera.tioa was the embodiment in their lives individual!and national of the spirit and gospe of Jesus Christ their Master. They could not improve on that. - -</p>
        <p>A collectinn waa thmn taken in *id of theCirenit Fund</p>
        <p>The singing of till D nrolnec conludad tie 'wet ng. LECTURE. The Hall was well filled on Mmnday fvninT, when Mrs. Chant gave a lecture entitled " Through the Blockade to Crete." Mr. F. Monks pre4led. and amongst others pres-nt were :—The Revs. John Yonge, C. Finch, J. S. Hughes, and*E. T. Reed, and Messrs. T. Birchall and W. Hickman. After the usual devotional exercises had been gone through, Mrs. Cnant, who posserses a very sweet voice, gave a feeliog reedy ion of "A Dream of Paradise,", for which she was vigor nislc encorel, singing in response "The Lost Chord " with eqi:ally good effect. The Chairman then called upon Mrs. Chant to deliver her lecture, and observed that the Mission on which she was bent when she went to Crete, was one that should commend itself to all their hearts. (Hear, hear). Mrs. CHANT, then delivered a most interesting lecture. She gave a graphic narrative of her expetiezces in Crete where she went at the head of a bind of nurses, during the war between Turkey and Greece, when Crete vas blockaded by the six great powers. The scenery and historical buildings in Greece and Crete were fully de• scribed, and limelight views,from photographs taken by Mrs. Chant,were thrown on the screen,the lantern being manipulated by Mr. Knowles. The usual votes of thinks brought a successful meet- ing to a close.</p>
      </div>
