      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>VACCINATION. Mr. Ward Obtains an Exemption _</head>
        <p>VACCINATION. Mr. Ward Obtains an Exemption _</p>
        <p>Order:</p>
        <p>STRONG REMARKS BY THE MAYOR</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the ordinary business at the War- rington Pa!ice C nut, op Monday, Mr. Thomas Ward, who resides in attersby-lane, &amp;molted for a certificate exempting his two childrea from vaccination. Appli- cant had made two previous applications, bat on each occasion the ratgistrates not being satisfied that he had a conscientious objection hid refused to grant the order. . Applicant now stated that he had .a conscientous objection against vaccination. He believed th it vaceina.- tion would be injurious to the health of his two children. He thought the magistrates wale upon that bound to grant the application. The Mayor said that that Wa3 where applicant made a mistake. He was bound to satisfy the magistrates that be conscientiously objected to vaccination on the ground that it was calculated to be injurious to his children. A general objection to vaccination was not sufficient. All they had before them in regard to this case was a general objection. Applicant: I don't believe in it. I believe it would be injurious. Mr. Birchall : Have you a consnientioas objection?</p>
        <p>Applicant: You. - Mr. Itirchall Why did you not say so at first? The Mayor: You told U 3 you didn't believe in vacci- nation. That is not sufficient.</p>
        <p>Councillor Stiaw GreQn (to applicant) : You hear what the Mayor sacs. hit merely a general oejeltlOn to vaccination that you entertain ?</p>
        <p>I : I conscientiously object to vaccination. Mr. Birchall : D 3 you balieve in this particular CASO it would ba injurious to the children? Appiicant : Yea. The Mayor: Oa what ground ? Applicant • I think it would be injurious. Tne Mayor: I want to 'point out what oblgation rests upon you. You must satisfy the Bench that you have a conscientious objection on the izr ound that vacci- nation will be injurious to yeur children. Give us smile evidence of it.</p>
        <p>Applicant said that from -obiery tilos he ha' made and from what he had read he believed that vaccination would be iujurious. U conscientimily beli.vel that. The Mayor : Are these children in gool health? Applicant: Yes. The Mayor: Oa what ground do you think itwould be injurious ? Applicant: From my observations and whtt I have read.</p>
        <p>Councillor Shaw Green said Mr. Ward had hardly grasped till point. Wis he of opinion that the injection of this lymph would be injurious to his children? Applicant: Yes.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: I shall not be a party to the turning loose among her Majesty's subjects two possible instru- ments of further contamination. Look at Hull, where the epidemic has raged eice last March. It is a lunatic in my opinion who asks for an exemption from vaccina- tion.</p>
        <p>Councillor Shaw Green said this was a magis'erial act. Having heard th 3 apolicanes direct sta'ement he did not see how they could refuse the application. The Mayor : It has really been put in his mouth. You can do as you like I shall no; sign the certificates. His Worship than left the Bench.</p>
        <p>Birchall (who took the chair) Bald his difficulty was that they could not override the law. Aldermin Blackburst : We are tymad to carry it out.</p>
        <p>Mr. Birahall.did not think they hai a right to refuse.</p>
        <p>After consulting the other magistrates, he said the order would be granted. They were, he said, bound to administer the law, not t ) attempt to alter it. Councillor Shaw Gre3r) : I chink ths•Mavor was per. juslified in saying that a general objection is in- sufficient. Having now eKpre s3d a specific opinion, I think we are bmnd to give the applicant the order. The magiatrat.s on thl Reich were the Mayor (in the chair). Mr. Birchall, Alderman Blackhurit, Council- lors Shaw Green and Smethurst, and Mr. Hayward.</p>
      </div>
