      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>HE LABEL TELLS THE PRICE",</head>
        <p>HE LABEL TELLS THE PRICE",</p>
        <p>SWIMMING CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL GENERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The anal' Al general meeting ot the Wartiag—to-n Swim- ming Club was 11Id as the B tths, Legh-street, on Tues- day ev, fling, there being a large attendance. Mr. C. if. Wood, the treasurer, was elected to the chair in the absence of the president. Councillor Dr. Cannell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Garside, secretary, submitted his annual report, in which he stated that toe year had, on the whole been successful. There was a dscr,ase of sixteen members, making a total of 152 against 163 in the pre- vious year. Things were not very bright at the begin- ning of the year for the polo teams, owing to the retire- ment of several of the ()tier members. The younger members rose to the occasion, however, and the teams did as well this year as they had ever done. The first team had played eleven matche, won four, lost five and drawn two, the scoring biMg 18 to 27. The second team had played six matches, won tnree,lost two, and drawn one. Robertson had won further international honours, being selected to play a.eainst both Scotland and Wales. To have the honour of playing aeainst Scotland was the blue ribband of the water polo w zld. The licensed victuallers challenge shield competition and the Thornton challenge bowl competition had again been woes by members of the club. A. Ashton won tha former and ,W. Broadhurst•the latter. Frank Broadhurst won the School Board Championship of Manchester. Several other swimmers had won prizas at various matches at Manchester and Liverpool. W. Sutton and A. Baker won te club's certificates for proficiency in swimming. Two very important galas were held. The first was held with the co- ,peration of the Manchester Osborne Club and was the most successful gala ever held from the spectators point of view. The expenses, however, were too heavy to allow of i,:,,reat profit. The second gala at whiels an internatiorul poio inaten was held did not re- ceive the sepp JrC that was merited by toe enterprise of the COlTimir.toe in sv.uring so imp )rtant a fixture. It was the most important match ever held at Warrington. The report was uninimou,lv passed. THE BALANCE SHEET.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H WOOD, treasurer, sibmitted the balanc, she, which showed a batance in baud of £l4 153 20 The balauc,, sheet was gassed uauaimously. Tue CHAIRMAN thmn presnited certificates of profici- ency to W. Sutton and A Baker. He said he was s)rry that more certificate had not been Rained, as he was certain that more memblr3 were qutlified to do the re- quisite time and become eatitted to them.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that p3rh3p3 ttl3 majority of them did not know the extent to which they were indebted to the Town Hall, Park and Baths C laminae for allowing them te aio of the bath; ,Anci prernis,3 for galas and pnlo matches. Without their co-operation, the club would b 3" strangled" at onc., and he thurit that the c )molittee were worthy of toeir best thanks for all they did for them.</p>
        <p>A votA ot thinks was unanimously accorded to the committee.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was accorded to the officers for their work during the year. The CHAIRMAN SAid be thought it was incumbent upon them to send a special vote of thanks to their President, Councillor Dr. Cannel for his services, and at the same time to convey to hil.n their gratification on his restora- tion to health. fle hid had a nalst strioui illat'S3 and they hoped he would soon entirely recover. This course Wi9 unaiimouqly agreed to. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Tho following otficf,rs Were tneueleced :—President,, the Miyor; Vice-Presidents, all the members of the ro a Hall, Park, and Baths C immittee, Mr Robert Pierp3int,. M.P., Councillors W Pierpoint and Jehn Evans, and Messrs H Aahton, A H Crosfield, W J Forshaw, J' Houghton, R A Naylor, J C Parr, Luke Talbot, It Thornton, H Taft, F Turner aid Roger Parr; captain of the club, Mr I Locker; capt.in of the polo team, Mr VD Ashton; sub-captain, Mr A G Robertson; treasurer, Mr ff Wood ; secretarie. Ms tea A. G Roberson and J Gray; assistant secretary,Mr U Bacley;cominittee,Messrs A Ashton, G Haley, A Boscow, R T larding, J El Sher- lock, J Cronstaaw, El Bate, J Rogues, F Garside, and W McCready ; delegates, Mr 1' I.,cker (Northern Counties Association), an I Mr A. G Robertson (Liverpi,ol and Dis- trict Association) ; auditors, Messrs F Garside and R T Elardince.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. GARSIDE was af; tin oropo4eil as j int secretary- but declined to sand for re-el4ction. M. Gray was then unanimously elected. The CHAIRMAN thought they ought to accord a special vote of thanks to Mr. Garside for his servic,s as secretary to the club during the last fout• years. They all knew - what he had done for the club. He had been the back- bone of the club and had given a larger amount of work and time than mr-st of them had any conceptiontof. He thought they were greatly in,iebted to him for his ser- vices.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Garaide with cheers Mr. GARStDB ia rerpondinz, said he thanked them heartily tor the vote of thanks. It had been a pleasure to him to do the wrk and he was sorry that pressure of buminers prevented him from continuing it. He had looked for no reward at all. The mere pleasure of doing the work had been quite sufficient reward to him for what Iv-, had 'done in the par,. (Applause )</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>TRANSVAAL CAMPAIGN -RELIEF FUND</head>
        <p>THE TRANSVAAL CAMPAIGN -RELIEF FUND</p>
        <p>The CHATRIII&amp;N said it had been auggested that they should make a donation of one guin.a to the local fund flr the relief nf ths uriv, s and families of the te4ervists. II e thought thy would all approve of th4t. It w.tg unanimously decided to contribute a guinea to the fund. The proceeiings then terminated.</p>
