      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>EXAIHNER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAIHNER.</p>
        <p>DEC EMBER Ist 1899.</p>
        <p>WHEN the stress was The Navy and the greatest upon our forces in War. - -</p>
        <p>South Africa it was said that there was abundant material of the very first quality lying unused in our marines and seamen in the Cape Spadron. They were regarded as emergency men, not to be called upon unless their services were absolutely required. The despatch of a naval contingent with ships' guns to Lady- smith was a brilliant suggestion, and it was carried out with the results we all appreciate. The naval guns saved General White from being over- whelmed by superior artillery, strengthened his force, and enabled him to make the splendid fight of it which coming generations will admire. What would Colonel BADEN-POWELL have given for a similar contingent with a few ships' guns ? The ; formation of a naval contingent to ;co-operate with Lord METHUEN in his advance upon Kimberley was another brilliant idea. The value of its services is acknowledged in the battle of Graspan, where there have been large losses. Bat now the turn of the tide has come, and we have the " Times " military commentator saying : " We may well doubt whether it is desirable that the personnel of the navy should be drained away in military operations hundreds uf miles from the sea." We regard this as an unkind comment on the navy, which has ever been ready to do its part when there has been serious work to be done. During the siege of Sebastopol the navy was able to send ashore guns and men that were of the utmost assistance. Again, during the Indian Mutiny there was a Naval Brigade, which was found to be useful and achieved remarkable things. Why is the navy to be kept out of its share in the S mth African War ? The ships were lying idle, there was nothing for the men to do, guns and men were wanted, and the force has done magnificent work. It may not I be" desirable" to turn sailors into soldiera in a general way. They have special training, awl there are not too many of them. But the mon " were at hand, they were eager to have a share in the war, and it would have been the height of folly.to have allowed our soldiers to be outnumbered and outmatched whilst there were men and guns available. Red-tape,in its thine, plays many parts. Our marines, for instance, are being kept at home when they would gladly have:contributed a batta- lion or two to the force now operating in South Africa. Red-tape decided otherwise. Red-tape would now measure the wisdom of employing marines and seamen by the n umber of miles they are from the sea and from their ships. It is not in this way that patriotic people look at the matter. They rejoice to see the navy doing its</p>
        <p>part, and doing it so well and the believe the navy, as a whole, will be the better for it. Natur- ally, we regret all the losses that the fortunes of war inflict upon us. We should prefer to see posi- tions assailed and battles wotiwith fewer losses of all kinds. But the drain on the navy is not likely to be large, and we feel sure that very few naval men will wish for such comments to be made as have appeared in the "Times."</p>
        <p>THE theory of our POST- A Reform of the Law is excellent, but the Poor Law. system is open to many objections, and, like so many other parts of our constitutional and administrative institutions, it requires amendment and bringing up to date. Hence we are not surprised that the Conservative Union preferred to pass a resolution tinkering with the Irish Union rather than a resolution suggesting reform in the working of the poor- law. The former might give political gains, the latter would meet a good many social evils; and se it is much less captivating to mere party poli- ticians. The direction reform should take is a very delicate matter. What is wanted is an ex- tension in two directions, giving better provision for the well-deserving poor, and embracing in the scope of poor-law operations such changes as would sweep our streets of mendicants, and make it impossible for anybody to plead that work of some kind was unobtainable. It is surely not impossible to make these changes without constituting a large class of paupers, and destroying the independence of the Wage- earning classes. Outdoor and indoor relief, as .at present administered, do fail shorl, of what We have a right to expect in a wealthy af:d Christian country. Some division is to be attenped in the way of separating the deserving from oceer classes, and whenever a scheme of old -age peoQions is inaugurated it must tonch many 4,f the people who now hang about the borderland which divides the poor who half-starve rather thla receive aid from the rates from the poor who do not hesitate to receive outdoor relief WllBll they ctii get it, or enter the union workhouses when taey *tie help- less and worn out. A very difficult question was raised about men who are deprived of work arid the means of Sustenance, because of trade troubles in which they have no direct interest, or which they have done nothing whatever to bring about. We do not desire to see the poor-law an aid to the war between labour and capital, but it is still pain- ful -that when assistance becomes imperative it is branded with the political disability attaching to aid of the ordinary kind. Hew this can be met without some system of public wezks we do not know. But if there were a plan devisable by which poor men could obtain some kind of work in times of stres?, at wages upon which they could hope to subsist, we might cure vagrancy and pro- vide for emergencies as well. The poor-law system breaks down or its work side. The Workhouse has ceased to be a workhouse, except for casuals, and we think it might be restored to its older form and become a part of our social system. Continental schemes are in existence by which the vagrant and the mendicant are put to work upon farm colonies and what is done abroad can be done at home. Some part of the scheme would have to be com- pulsory, ea:l other parts of it voluntary. The lazy, good-for-nothing prowlers and loafers would have to be dealt with openly for what they are really—an injury to the whole community. The unemployed generally would have to do some kind of work, not so well paid as ordinary labour, and yet of a kind remunerative to the local body which instituted it. Economy in general administration would assist in providing the means for much Of this extra expense, aril the discipline of work would be found effective in etrabling men and women to become respectable members of society. These suggestions involve the recasting of our poor-law system. We are getting ready for some of these changes, and probably, in the first in- stance, they would need to be permissive and ten- tative, much liberty being left to local Boards of Guardians. At present the system is too rigid, • and the Local Government Board is without the power of permitting experiments. A change here would gradually lead to a much more effective and far-reaching system.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sidney Low has et3setl a very happy mo- ment for cautioning England against being puffed up over her own calm in the moment of disaster, or over her own feats in the moment of victory. This caution was not unnecessary. The other day we were superbly self-complacent when the news of the disaster at Nicholsons's Nek arrived, and foreign comment of an admiring kind was re- ceived as something which wo were quite aware was due to us. Now,this spirit has its advantages in moments of very real danger, but there is no real danger to the Ropire in connection with the war,and our complacent heroics are both undigni- fied and ridiculous. As Mr. Low points out, the more dignified attitude for the greatest Power in the world, engaged in chastis- ing two of the smallest, would bel to re- gard the whole business as a disagreeable though necessary duty. We fear, moreover, that there is a serious lesson to be culled from the in- cident. Our calmness is so superb in its propor- tions that it begets the suspicion that it cannot be sound. How should we behave if the dark and miserable hours of 1797 were to be repeated ? In the spring of that year the fleets at Spithead and the Nore had mutinied, our coalitions had fallen to.pieces, our armies had been defeated, the Bank of England had suspended, payment, Consols were down to 45, foreign foes had landed on British soil in Pembrokeshire, foreign fleets were prepar- ing to set sail for the Thames and for Ireland, and we could not raise money to buy them off! We may rightly congratulate our grandfathers upon their confidence in that black era. But can we truthfully declare that we should exhibit it with equal strength if so man y dangers were repeated to-day?</p>
        <p>The serious small-pox epidemic which is at pre- sent raging in Hull will recall to the minds of many Warrington pe ipie the last visitation of that dire; disease to this neighbourhood an its terrible consequences, the like of which it is to be hoped Warrington will never experience again. But to be forewatned is to be forearmed and the: present is perhaps a fitting opportunity to ask Are we any better prepared for the isolation ef small-pox patiehts than we were a few years ago. If not, then we have failed to profit by the severe lesson we received. We were found in a state of unpreparedness when the small-pox fiend last visited us and a grave responsibility will rest upon the shoulders of the meiebers of tiio Health Com- mittee if they are caught napping a second time. We are aware that something has been done to- wards providing an isolation hospital at Pear Tree Farm, if the passing of. building plans may be con- sidered as such: This, however, is not enough,snd it is time building . operations were seriously con- templated.. Even if the hospital :yore built and left unfurnished we should feel more secure than at the present time.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL VISIT TO CREW-!r. Tllo2laa Don, Oculist and Aurist (of IsJn's Eye and Ear DispenFary, Leeds), established 1871, will visit rhe R iyal 116 tel, Crewe, Wednesday D,scembor 611. h iurs 11 to 1, and the Royal a-itel, Wigan. Thurs lay I:o2cimber 21s1-• flours 2to 6 and may be cNisulted oa all eases of failing sight and hearing. New tr:?atmont without opz!r.tion. 'sufferers should avail themselves et tie present opplituoity of consultation. Spectacles adaptei for weak or defcctive sight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE AS3OCIATION. The quarterly meeting of members and friendi of the Runcorn branch was held on Thursday, November 23rd, in the Guild Parlour, Brunswick School._ Mrs. Christieri presided. and there was a large attendance of ladies. .Miss C.T. Tinliag,of London,gave an account of various departmeuti of work carried ma by some of the oth,r branches ot the N. B.W.T. A. At the close of the address! a pleasant bcur was spent in discussion and conversa- tion.</p>
        <p>LECTURE AT THE NATIONAL _SCHO-3LS.</p>
        <p>A large audience ..ssetiibled at the Rano ra National Schools, on Friday night under the chairmanship of the Rev. H. B. Firth, and listened attentively to an admir- able lecture delivered by the Rev. C W Sherrard, formerly vice-president of St Aidan's Theological College, and for twiny years a missionsry at Grahanr•town,Smth Africa. At the close of the of the lecture a collection was tsken. ACCIDENTS TO LABOURERS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>John Oliv(r Johnson, aged 18, 23, Beaumont-street, a labourer at Tod's drum works, was admitted to the Accidesit Hospital on Saturday, suffering from a crushed right hand, it being caught in an iron rolter.—On Mon- day, Thwnas Eloward, aged 25, Mersey View-road, Hale- bank, whilst following his employment at Bolton's copper works, six copper plates fell on his foot and crushed it. Be was removed to the Accident Hospital. Both men were attended to by Dr. Hutchinson. NARROW ESCAPE OF MR. H. H. POSNETT.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. H. Posnett,the respected manager of the High- Seld Tannery, is said to have bed a narrow escape on Monday. As he was cycling along the canal bank to the Tannery he met a Telegraph boy, who proferred him a telegram. In attempting to take the rnecsage out of the boy's hands whilst riding past, Mr. Posnett sreglectld his steering and rode into the canal. He was successful in getting out, however, with nothing less than a drenching, and his machine w,03 rescued later by a number of workmen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>MILTON WESLEYAN CHURCH. WIDNES</head>
        <p>MILTON WESLEYAN CHURCH. WIDNES</p>
        <p>Foreign missionary services in connection with tlis church were held on Sunday. The pu'pit was occupied in the morning by Rev. E. Hawker, of South Africa,and in the evening by the pastor, the Rev. J. Pedlar. Ap- propriate discourses were given ta good congregations. In the afternoon the P.S. A. held an 'open meeting. The church choir rendered the sacred cantata, " Harvest Some in the Temple days," in an admirable mance:. Mr. F.Lawringon presided over a large and appreciative gathering. 01 Monday evening the rnis3ioeary services were continued, and a gaol meeting was held, when ad- dresses were given by tue Revs. E. Hawker and W. C: Willoughby, two missionaries from South Al.rica.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>SU-DDE3H- DEATH OF A RUNCORN LOCK KEEPER</head>
        <p>SU-DDE3H- DEATH OF A RUNCORN LOCK KEEPER</p>
        <p>Last Friday morning William Wright. aged 76, lock- keeper, of 25, Ratland-street, died suddenly. It trans- pires Wright came home from work on Thursday even- ing, apparently in his usual health. After tea he occu- pied himself with reading until 8 30. as then partook of a hearty supper, and subsequently retired for the night. About two o'clock on Friday morning his daughter heard him moaning, and on going to his room inquired what was the matter. Deceased put his hands on his chest, and finding he was ill the daughter sought the assistance of a neighbour. The man, however, expired shortly afterwards. Death was medically certi- fied to be due to senile decay. WIDNES BOROTJGH MINSTRELS AT THE MERSEY MISSION.</p>
        <p>The Widnes Blrriugh Minstrels attracted a full room, to the Mersey Mission, lait Tu, sday night. The Chair- man was Mr. G. Surnmerfield. A capital programme was submitt, d, inoluling songs, and sketches, all of which were highly appreciated. The troupe opened with " Let the hills rps34nl with acne" and created a good impt•es.ion which they maintained to the finish. The °trier i'efrE of th? first part were songs, cmitc 'and Sentimental, contributed by Messrs J Ryan, J Walker, T Ifurstfield, J Rutter, G Outram, E Taylor, G R Jonas, G Cartwright, W Witter, W Abbott, and J Jones. The second part coniiisted of two sketches : the first illustrative of the " Troubles of a Doctor's Shop," and the second entitled " Have you seen her" which were supported by the whole streneth of the troupe. A bone solo by Mr. W. Baskerville •, a duet "The Pilot Brave," Messrs Abbott and Walker; and a song " Clever Mr. Green " from Mr. J. Hurstfield followed. The efficiEnt accompanist was Mr. T. Edwards. The usual votes of thanks closed the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PICKWICK LITERARY AND DEBATING SOCIETY</head>
        <p>PICKWICK LITERARY AND DEBATING SOCIETY</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, at the meting of the above a paper was read by Mr. R. H. Bridges on " Thrift." This subject to him, he said, was one ot great intereit, and Should be t all young men. He. was a devoted disciple of Samuel Miles, and had digested to a large degree the writino of that great economist: Thrift was the handmaid of piety, and if it took its proper place in the astimaticn of the working classes, a great social revolution would take place. Could the workingman be inada to see the importance of taking hold and practising this great virtue, then legislature would be relegated to a backward position' and old ago pensions and Ouch like would be thought of only as a dream of some would-be politican. "Self help," in the style adopted by Co-operators, was the lever, in his opinion, for the uplifting of the working classes, and could he have succeeded in converting his hearers to adopt that method, then his efforts wqu'd not have been in vain. Ele had seen to a large extent great irt- provideace on the part of ;he working classes, and this had caused him to look at the subject with greater interest. He hoped sincerely that the day would not be far distant when thrift would be among the things practised by the working man. Discussion tollowed,Mr Nillington presided and a good attendance of members listened attentively to the paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>FALL FROM A RAIL- WA.Y CARRIAGE.</head>
        <p>FALL FROM A RAIL- WA.Y CARRIAGE.</p>
        <p>As the 5.20 p.m. train frem Warrington drew in the Widnes London and North-Western Statism, on Monday, the occupants of one of the carriages were seen to be in the wildest commotion. They at once infortmei the officials that William Kelly, aged 4, son of Mrs. Kelly, 80 James-street, Widnes, who was also in the carriage, had fallen out of the train by Carter House Junction. Several of the station staff immediately ran up the line and found the child lyieg unconscious on the metals of the up-line. A light engine was also despatched to the scene and returned with the child, who was at once conveyed to the Accident Hospital. Afzer examination he yea* found to be suffering from concussion of the brain, and the Doctor held out ino hopes of his recovery. The occupants of the carriage were a portion of a weddine party, and were returning from the festivities. It is supposed that as they were busily engrossed in their conversation :he child had been playing with the handle of the door. At the junctien the railroad takes a sharp bend and the sudden jolt flung open the door and twee the boy out. THE, LATE MR. W. GITTENS.--.PULPIT REFERENCES. A large congregation assembled in the Greenway-road Chapel on Sunday night, when reference was made to the loss the church had sustained through the death of the late Mr. William Gittens. Tbe Rev. W. Firloe, aftn- delivering an impressive discourse, based on John xi. 21. said Mr. tiittens wee teem in the village of Welsh Frank- ton, Shropshire. Be was a Primitive Methodist of the third oeneration. The house of his grandparents was a preachine heuse. nig parents were also godly people. After the death of his mother, which occurred when he was ahoet 18 years of age. he and his brot!..:er decided to join the army. They went to Shrews- bury to enlist, when, to his surprise and grief, he was acceptel and his brother refuesd. He felt the separation keenly. He served in India, five years with the Royal Horse Artillery, fought in several eneagements. and, being seriously wounded, relearned to his netive village. Shortly after the Runcorn Brill::o was in course of construction, and he,with many others, sought and obtained employment. He attended the Primitive Methodist place of worship, and became a member about 20 yams am. He held several important positions in the church. For severe, years he WAS one of the treasurers for the truq and senior society steward. Be waq a trustee for the Greenwaseroad buildings and Aebbrides-street Mission Hall. He was else an assist- ant class leader, and treasurer of the building fund dur- ing the erection of the new schools in Greenway-read. He loved the sanctuary and esteemed the ministers and local preachers. Nothing gave him greater pleasure than the prssioerity of the church. He was a man or character, purpose, and fidelity. His illners wag borne with patience and fortitude, and on November 6:h he fell into a peaceful sleep and passed away. THE BRI7ASWICIE MEN'S. BIBLE CLASS RE•UNTION.</p>
        <p>A gathering of a pleasing and happy ?allure waz that assembled in the Guild Parlour of the Brunswick .schools. on Monday evening, when at the invitation of Mr. T. Simister, the past and present mernners of his select Bible °Nee, with their wives and friends enjoyed themselves by the holding of a re-union .—There was a very large at....ndance. After devotional exucises, Mr. Simister btisfly explained the reason why he had called the meeting together. He wished to cement the bond of sympathy which already existed be';weeN the momb n•s. and he also extended a very hearty invitation to all those who had at one time been members of his 0.19,, and Who at present did not attend any place of worship on a Sunday afternoon. He was very plen4ed to see so large an attendance. There was a very interesting pro- gramme to be gone through, and he hoped al ths per- 'VMS present would enjoy a very pleasant evening. Mr. Hughes sang in a very effective manner " The Storm Fiend," and secured a well-merited round of applause. Mr. Johnson's voice, wag heard at its host in 'the "Flight of AE,109." " Soldirrs of the Queen," by Mr. F Ellison, evoked an enthusiastic buist, while the humorous side of the audienne was Roweled to in a very able manner by Mr. W. Milington's reading, en- titled "Mark Twain's Account of an inrvis.„er.,, Mr. Hughes again delighted the audience with "In Friend- /hires Name "Going to Carnarvon," a humorous sketch, recited with much dramatic effelt hr Mr. W. Jones, was the source of considerable mirth. His recall, "Going to the Isle of Man,"was also well received, Mr. Birkett's reading, "His Best Girl." and Mr. J Leath- wood's song, "Jolly Jack,"were loudly applauded. After Mr. Millington had addressed the audience, refreshments were passed round, and the meeting eventirslly hrolTe up,everyone having thoroughly enjoyed the proceedings Is is interesting to note the elass has been established 42 years last October. It was established b..- Mr. Simiater nd from its very inception he has been its leader.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>STREET BETTING AT WESTON.</head>
        <p>STREET BETTING AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>Another Successful Prose-</p>
        <p>cution.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Se6sions, on Monday, before Messrs. A. Thomas (pre,i ling), and A. R. Norman another interesting prosecution of the "betting fraternity" came on for hearing, and caused considerable interest.—Walter Anderton, described as a weigher in the employment of the Ship Canal Company, at Weston Point, was sum- moned at the instance of Superintendent D. Pear- son, of the Cheshire Constabulary, for frequenting and using Post Office-lane, Weston Point, for the purpose of bookmaking, on the 17th of November, 1899, and divers other dates.—Thomas Bloor, George Kirkham, Joseph Henderson, William Beckett and Albert Ranklin, all worxing men, employed in or about Weston Point, were also implicated, and summoned for using the same place for the purpose of agreeing to bet, on the same dates Mr. Knowles, solicitor,Widnes, defended and Superin- tendent D. Pearson conducted the case for the police. At the outset, Mr. Knowles intimated, he did not wish to take any mean advantage, by saying nothing until the conclusion of the case, and then getting up to say that the case was wrong ; but he wished to point out that the information charging them with the offsnce, stated "On the 17th of November and divers other dates." He contended, and quoted cases to show, that they could only be proceeded against for the one specific date "the 17th," and that if the police were desirous of bringing other charges on different dates they should all have been entered as separate inditements. floe Clerk pointed out that Mr. Knowles' remarks re- ferred to separate offences, The present was one con- tinuous offence that of betting in the streets,aud advised the Bench that the information was good. Mr. Knowl(s asked that a note of his objection should be made, as in the event of a conviction he would ask the Bench te coots a case. He further characterised the whole procedure as an unscrupulous attempt on the part of the police to get a conviction. Superintendent Pearson stated that if the Bench held with Mr. Knowles he would ask for an adjournment,but if not he was willing to proceed with the case. Mr. Knowles' remarks had not frightened him in the least. (Laughter) The Bench ruled the information correct [and the case proceeded. Constable William Wylie, stationed at Weston,in con- sequeece of information received. said ho concealed himself in company with Constable Bancroft, of Weston Point, in the latter's st ttien. on the 10th of November. Whilst there, he saw a number of men assemble in Pest Office-lane. The men were in the habit of astemteing there reeulasly about noon. About 12 40 on that particular date he saw Anderton come up, and enter into cseyers tti nt with the noon. Afterwards he went along South-road. On Saturday the 11th, he was again concealed at Bancroft's Station and saw three men whom ho knew and two other men, unknown to him, standing at the end of Past Office-lane. The men were mostly conselting a newspsper. At one p.m, the defendant came up, and -taking a book out of his pocket started writing something on a slip of paper placed on the back of the book. He saw money Pass between the five men and tbe defendant. Andertm made entries each time money passed. It commenced to rain and the men dispersed. On the 16:h he was con- cealed in Dunbevin's shop. On that occasion he saw eorg( Kirkham give Joseph Henderson some money.— Mr. Knowles here ohj cted saying such statements could not be evidence. It was simply done to prejudice the case.--The objection was recorded, and witness pro- c 'edit* said teat mbsequently a man named Lamb tame up and had a conversation with the defendant, alter which they adjourned to the Dock Hotel Weston On the 17th he was again in Danbavin's shop, and saw men assemble again. At 12-45 defendent came up the Canal Bank, and went to the men standing in the street. Anderton took the slip of paper out of his pocket. Thomas Bloor gave him a Angle coin, and the defendant entereo, something on the lower. He then went to Joseph Anderton and George Kirkham, who were read- ing newspapers, and bath men passed coins to the de- fendant, after which he mad t entries in a book. Ander- ton then went on to the Canal Bank with three other men. He and Baneroft cams out of the shop by the back way. When the defendant and the other men saw them they ran away. They gavechase, hut lost sight of them. About 1.30 the same cTa.y, they wentte the office weere Anderton was employed, and he (witness) said to him "I saw you take money and make entries on a slip of paper in Post Office-lane." Defendant replied "I didn't take a bet to-day. I was just going to do se when I saw you coming," Witness then asised for his book and papers.—Mr. Knowles interposing: As it would be illegal to ask for the book and papers, it is as illegal to submit that as evidence. I again object.—Superintendent Pearson: I am simply getting at the facts for you Mt. Knowles.— To witness: As a matter of fact he did not give you any en-Witness : No.—Superintendent Pearson : Did defendaet say anything further P—Witness : He s ild " For God's sake let me off this time ; it will be the sack for me, and I will promise you never to take a bet from anyone again." Witness told him "It was entirely out of his province and he (defendant) had better see the Superintendent." Afterwards he did go and see the Superintenlent at the Runcorn Police Station. He and Bancroft then went hack to the Canal Bank, and picked up several pieces of torn paper which the defendant had presumably thrown away. Those pieces of paper he took borne and pasted together so that they forested the document (produced). Witness was proceeding to read the document which recorded is. 61. " Oppressor," is. " Oppressor " (any back), is. " Diana Ken" and other .bets when Mr. Knowles again objected.— Witness continuing went on to say that he looked up the "Liverpool Echo" and found the names recorded on the document (produced) were the names of horses down to run at Manchester.—Mr. Knowles in cross-examination did not shake witness the least as he referred to his notes (taken at the time) for his replies a procedure that Mr. Knowles again objected to.—ln answer to the Superin- tendent,witness said he wished to refer to his notes con- tinually because the case cevered so many dates he could not remember without them. On the 1041 the men would be in the street for a minute or two ; on the 11th about 4 or 5 minutes ; on the 16th for about 5 minutes, and on the 17th for about five minutes. Constable Bancroft, stationed at 'Wes'on Point cor- roberated the first witness ; 'and, in addition testified to seeing a similar accurrence on the 154.—8 y Mr. Knowles: They would be there for about 8 minutes on that occesion. William Pye, aged 13 years, said he resided in Regent- street, and was employed by Mr. Broder, oyster mer- chant, a neighbour. He had been with him about 9 months. His duty was to go to Weston Point every day. except Sunday, and meet Walter Anderton, who nave him a bag to take to Mr. Broder. He did not know what the hag contained.—Cautioned by the Superinten- dent ; Witness reiterated he could not tell what was in the bag.—Mr. Knowles: We will tell you if it will help. We will admit that there was somethine ta put on" in it —Continuing: Pye said he had seen Bsclrett land Blom give Anderton money, toot did not know why they did se. He went up on the 17th the same as usual, ; hut did not receive the bag from Anderton until he saw him afterwards. Frank Fox. labourer, lodging in Post °Mee-lane, Wes- ten Point, said he knew defenaant, and had seen him in Post Office-lane on the 15th sad 17th of November, sod other dates Having given evidence of a corroboratory nature to that of the police, he stated ha hod ofren made bets with Anderton. The last date would be the 15th of November. Sotre`imes he put a shilline and sernerimes 61. On one, occasion he put 4s. on" Flying Fox "and ehnuld have drawn £2 45,, but was not paid. This cencluded tee case for the prosecution. Mr. Knowles for the defence held that the" frquent- ing and using" had not been proved. Their worships would kn ow from their experience on the Bench—they would not know it otherwise—'hat men did earn their living as bookmakers and bookmaker assistants, hut these mtn he contended devoted their whole time to the work. The defendant he contended had employment and was reenter:l7 engaged. The prosecution had simply proved that the defendant had stayed for a few minutes a day on the route to his dinner and entered into conversation with his friends. He did not I:aild there for hours. Further he argued that lo,tting in EnOand was legal ; but it was only the reUy money nesting that was illegal. He would admit that his client and a few of h's frier&amp; were sending as they did to a regular bookmaker but Anderton had not made a bet and was not a bookmaker in any sense. Thomas Bloor, Weston Point. swore he never made a bst in h's life. Re did not care who said anything to the contrary. He had beard what the consteblee had sail, but h' did not care if 50 more came end tried to say he had made a bet in all his life with Anderton ort any other perenn.—Cross-sxamined by Sunerintenden. Pearson repeatedly, defendant adhered to his state merit. waiter Anderton, the defendant on oath. aid he was not a bookmaker and had never made a book in his life. It was a fact he sent money and stips in a bag to some- one in Runcorn who made a "book." He simply sent on the money of himself and his friends.—Cross-examined by Superintendent Pearson : Witness reiterated his statement. He was not paid a salary or commission by Broder That he swore to. It was not true that he had quarrelled with his wife and that she had threatened to go to the police and " give the show away." William Beckett, a cooper by trade. but no as following the occupation of a timber clerk, swore to being confident that Anderton was not a bookmaker. He simply sent money on for them, and they would not in any way hold Anderton reetonnsible for any bets they asked him to send over to Runcorn for them—By Superintendent Pearson. He knew the witness Fox but did not know that he was comine there as a witness. He had heard him swear that he (Fox) had made bets with Anderton and had heard him sac that I 6'. was laid by him and that he was not paid by Ander- ton. Yet he witness would swear he was positive that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 1, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 1, 1899.</p>
        <p>Anderton did not receive bet, He (witnesq) had made a bet about sor 6 years ago. He had :olio) I Andert(sa to pass money on for him as a friend. In furtiter exami- nation witness remained fir:n</p>
        <p>The Chairman to witness !, If you want to moio a bet what procodnre do you follow to get about Lt? Witness : I have sent him—(Broder)—a wire. The Chairnaan : But the lad received the money from you. How did you get it on? Witness: If ever I had aVA it was given to Allier- ton to give to the bov. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles conch-Wing his cas2 said the word"book- maker" had an English definition. A bookmaker was man who laid odds against racehorses. He was clearly a man who made a "book" —a "book" was a document. There was no book, be contended, produced to that Court. There had simply been a few slips sent in a bag, and in addition to that the defendant himself denied be was a bookmaker. After a brief retirement,the Chsirman stated they had decided to convict, and fined And.ertan lOs and cosp or 14 days in default. Ndr. Knowles then referred to the other cases, and without yorejulice, offered to advise the men to pay the costs 44 61 if the police would witndraw the case.</p>
        <p>The Superiitenthnt conswited saying he had no desire tp prosecute the men. The m3n agreed and willingly paid 4; 6d eazh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>On good authority I hear that there are signs of a decided improvement in the chemical trade so far as Widßes is affected. On Monday last,"Mathiesons" took on some 20 fresh hands, and now evidences abound of the plant at the "Atlas" works being over-hauled with a view to its recommencing. There is some talk, however, of one of the Widnes works being transferred to Northwich; but, even if , that does come about, the number of" hands" affected will in all probability follow the "job." All this is extremely gratifying nays, especially,during winter time, when so many laffourers a; essentially summer employment have to look round for some- thing to keep away the "wolf" from the door.</p>
        <p>This week there has been a further holding of meetings, miscellaneous in character, throughout the district with the object of raising further sums to swell the "soldiers portion ;" and it is pleasing to notice a more practical line has been taken by the holding of concerts, the proceeds of which have been devoted to this laudable object. Last Saturday a successful collection was also made at the football match,at which the Band of the 2nd Earl Cheshire (Runcorn Detachment) gave their services gratis. The Volunteer Band have also announced the hoiding of a series of Saturday Night concerts, the first of which comes off at the Drill Hall to-morrow.</p>
        <p>The St. Paul's Wesleyan choir as I announced some weeks ago have a movement on foot which culminates in that most praiseworthy and philan- thropic work—the Charity Concert. The object is to raise some substantial benefit for Mr. Robert fiarrop, who, it will be remembered, is devoid of the perfect use of his eyesight, and who only the other week suffered a severe blow in the tragic loss of his wife. An opp3rtunity for practical sym- pathy is here offered. Independent of the philan- thropic character of the concert, from a pernsual of the programme submitted, I gather it is well worth patronage.</p>
        <p>The air is now full of rumour, and on the horiz9n of the Runcora ratepayers giZ3 there looms largely the word "Association." This I understand is a</p>
        <p>resultant of the Park and Pleasure Grounds policy of the Urban Distric:; C)uncil.—A. policy to which several of the pronalters of the" AssJciation a fancied fanacea—are somewhat hostile. Why those people lest their opprtunity of objecting at the inquiry is a mystery? But now they advocate the " Association" and show a spirit that is entirely unique.</p>
        <p>Doubtless something good would come out of the establishment of a "Tradesmen's Association " for our town, and one wonders why there is no such institutions. A body: of men gathered together for the object of protecting the trade and commerce of the poet of Runcorn would indee I be welcome as a power for good. But a Ratepayers' Association interested only in party politics, or, worse, local politics of a limited character, would be something deplorable and devoid of anything approaching beneficial results.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Liberal Club has been temporary transferred to the Forrester's flail in Devonshire- square, whilst the old buildinq in Regent-street is about to undergo transmogrification. Owing to an alleged insurmountable difficulty in the nature of light ob4truction the building cannot be elevated. Tilst difficulty is however, being oveicome by the lowering of the basement by excavation. The building will ultimately be converted into a two storey one, with the billiard-rocm above the read- ing rooms. Hearn the change has been rendered poksible through the unbounded generosity of Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>THEFTS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THEFTS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Prisoners Leniently Dealt With. M3ssrs A. Taornas, and A. R. Nonnau presided a% the Runcorn Sessions, last Monday, when two interesting cases of larceny were. dealt with. Ann Jane Trelfer, a boat woman, of Gas-street, Run- corn, was charged with stealing a pair of trousers of the value of 5111 d the property of Samuel Row lends. on Saturday last.—Fro:n the evidence, it appeared that Rowlands purchased the trousers for 54 11d and on his way home called at the "'Wheat Sheaf " public house. Ele put down his parcel on a bench in the taproom, and indulge.' in a game ef dominoes. Prisoner took up the parcel and went out of the room; but her action was %Mussed by another woman (Elizabeth Niekolls). VVhen Rowlands rose to leave, he missed his parcel, and subsequently gave information to the police. Constable Carrington went to Gas- street straight way, and found prisoner's husband and another man corning crut of the house. Trelfer had the parcel under his arm, and c mseqaently Carrington took him in charge. From further inquiries ho went to the "Wheat Sheaf" and arrested the prisoner. He chargel her with th 3 offence and she answered "Yes I saw them there, and took them to my house thinking someone would come for them sometime or other." .41.171 also told her husband when first brought t ) the Station in the presence of Constables Carrington and Fry that she "had come to taka his place."—For the defence, the husband said the police nev,sr met him, and prisoner said she tosk the trousers but never intended to steal them. 9ne hoped the Beach would be merciful.—Fined 10a ineuding costs.</p>
        <p>An Ungrateful Girl. Elizabeth Ann Martin, (a girl aged 18), who said she resided with her parents in Gilbert-street. was charged with stealing a keyless silver watch and chain valued at £2, and a pair of corsehs, 21 , the property of Winifred Williamson, en the 25Ve of November, and also with stealing a saucepan, valul 2s. 61,, the property of Emily Finney, Waterloo-road, on the 21st November.— From the evidence of Catharine Williamson and her daughter Winifred Williamson, it transpired that the prisoner WAS the d auehter of an old neighbour of theirs. She came to the house on the night of the 24th arid told Mrs. Williamson that she had had a row with her parents and wanted shelter. This was afforded her.and in the morning she assisted in a little housework. She W 31,3 providad with a sub tantial breakfast, and left later in the day. In consequence of what witoesys heard, they went upstairs and missed a watch awl strain (produced.) The daughter subsequently missed the corsets (also prniuced) which ewe now identifie 1 as hers by a private mark.—Conetehle Fry proved arrestisg the girl with the watch and chain in her p is. session. She admitted taking it, on being charged with the offence, but said she" bought the corsets in' a place WI ere she could show him if she had in aney eaotreli to go there "—E nily Finney, wife of Mr. Ge aree Finney, 'said she was the'keeper of a small shop in Watsrloo-reae. ' About half past eleven on Tuesday night the 21st of November she wont to the yard t fetch a saticep en but it was not tlvre. She gave information to the polies.— ' Charlotte Marshall testifi,d to meeting prison rin the street on tin same night with a swcspaa.—Elizi Pille• line, wife of E twirl Pidtine, 3. Cartwrieht-stre st, de- posal to prisoner asking her to buy a saucepan (ore- ducel) —P.C. Fry said he arrested the prisoner and took posssseion of the saucepan. She admitted stealine it.—Prisoner pleaded guilty to both thefts. From numerous questions put to her b; tie Beach it would appear her parents were boat people and had gone to Liverpool. 'See was in the habit of ac lompanying them, but had cperrelled with them over s meething and had not been with them for three weeks —She r 4uled how- ever, to s atiefactorily ac:nunt for the way he which she had spent the three weeks.—The Chairmen, said if prisoner was willing to reform, the pollee would get her into some inetitution.—Prisoner exori red her willing- ness to go to such a place, and was consequently reman- ded for 7 days to see what could be done.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Miffs Cricket Club.</p>
        <p>• The annual meeting of the Sankey Wire Mills Cricket Club was held on Monday evening. There was a good attendance of members, Mr. Nathan; Cook presiding. The following were elected officers for next season :—President, Mr Ernest Houghton; secretary, Mr R W Clok, 47, Wellfield-street, War- rington; assistant secretary,Me J J Pritchard; treas- urer Mr James Evans ;captain Ist Eleven, sir A Mar- shall; sub-cinrain, Mr W Woodcock ; captain 2nd Eleven, Mr G Shaw; sub-caotain,Mr Joseph Hamer committee, Messrs F Williams, W Woodcock, W Backhouse,A Clarke,J L Hawthorn and W Evans. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Presi- dent. Patrons, and Vice-Presidents of the club for the help they rendered last year and they were all cordially re-elected. The balance sheet shows a good sum in hand and the club is now in a very_ prosper- ous condition. Mr.W.Woodcock then presented the secretary, Mr. 1?. W. Cook, with a beautiful gold medal as a token of respect for the services he had rendered !..o the club during the past years. The Secretary said that he thankedthe members for the kindness they had shown to him and that he would do his utmost to make the next season as success- ful as the past years have been. A. vote of thanks to the chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>When asking for 0003 a, insist on having C &amp;DBURYS.—soId only in Packets and Tins as other Cocoas me often substi tutel fcr the make of extra profit..—Advt (1) MASTOID comrouNn. for Coughs and Colds, Asthma and bronchitis. Of Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
        <p>"LAMPS. 011 Stoves. Coke Stoves, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53. and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.— Advt. BEST BOOTS rni ALL, UNDBIi VIE CLOCK.— eldv</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>PROPOSED COTTAGE HOSPITAL FOR RUNCORN. Provisional Committee Formed.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED COTTAGE HOSPITAL FOR RUNCORN. Provisional Committee Formed.</p>
        <p>A meeting convened by Mr. F. Wiswall, chairm in of the Urban District Connell, was held in the Technical Institute, Runcorn, on Monday evening, to consider the advisability of establishing a cot- tage hospital for the town. Mr. Wiswall occupied the chair, and he was supported by the Rev. Canon daitland Wool, Messrs. S. Taylor, A. R. Norman, E. Imison, and E. Marshall. while in the bady of the hall were the Rev. J. Chambers, Rev. W. Fiche, Messrs. R. H. Posnett, D Bisbrowa, several members of the Runcorn Cycle Club, and others. Mr. Marshall read letters of apology for absence from Mr. F J Norman and Mr H Congreve. Mr Norman said he would he pleased to subscribe £5O towards the building fund on the distinct u-Alqstanding that the scheme shou'd bo on public and unascrarian lines, and controlled by the Urban Council. Mr Conveys stated that he would give £lOO t )wards the sch-me and the Ship Caaal Company would contribute £5O per annum towards the maintenance expenses for three years. Dr. Robinson wrote stating that as he had taken no nart in the collection of the funds, he was unable to offer any suggestion as to the disposal of it. He expressed his approval of a scheme which wouki provide for boat peoili and Irish farm labourers and strange s. His experience was that his own patients preferred to be treated in their own homes. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman explained that the question of a otters hospital had been discussed by west he might call a scratch committee, and that committee had duetsg the ls few weeks, raised the earn at their disposal from £3OO to more than double that amount. He thought that was a matter for congratulation. That meeting had been called at the ins 'acre of the committee with the ohjsct of forming% Prov sional Committee to formu- late a seheme. The fiast resoltrion on the paper they had received road as follows :—" That this meeting is of opinion that there is need of a cottage hospital for surgical cases in Runcorn." If they went beyond surgical cas,S they entered upon debetable grounl. He would prefer to take the resolution as it stood. As regarded private cases, any person or iadividual company requir- ing private eatients treated in the hospital shriuld have a fund to cover the cost. If the resolution was to be altered to cover private cases then their estimates would be affected. The Ship Canal Company said they would provide £5O a year for three years pro- viding a Rum of £l5O per annum for maintenance was guaranteed. He (the chairman) thought there would be no difficulty about that. The Rev. J Chambers said he came there with great interest in that movement. Although he was a stranger in the town he had found the neceadtv of a cottage hospital. Ac aeon as he arrived in the town he had to deal with two cases, and he was very glad to find that that movement was going on,. He .had come to give all the support he could to the resolution. He suggested further that they should restrict the use of the ins;itu- tion to needy cases, where the patient could receive hatter treatment in the heenitai teat he could po2sibly receive at home. He moved the fizat reso:utien,already read by the Caairman• This having hen seenneei, The Vicar peinted oat that if they reltrict .d them- selves to surgical cases it would be a difficul: matter to alter the scheme hereafter. If th W pas3ed toe resolution in a general form they ciuld alter it as tliAy thmzlit necessary. If they could raise £3oos year thy could maintain a very de lent homitel for all kinds of cases. He knew of a case where there was a hospital of some sie or eight beds, and tha maintenance did not cost £3OO in all. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Unison said he thonght if the words " surgical cages" were retained, it would deor!ase the popularity of the movement. They had already a hospital which might be more useful, but the bugbear of increasing the rates prevented its use. If they in Raneorn compared themselves with neighbouring towns, they would find that in those times cases of typhoid fever were removed to the hospital and received proper melical attendance and food. but in Rai:acorn they did noting of the kind, not because they did not see the necessity of it, but because they were afraid of increasing, the rates. If they confined the cottage hospital to surgical owns, with a probability of it falling on the rates, then he thouzht the movement yr:mid not ree ive the support it other. wise would.. If the movement was to be popular the definition of the hospital should be as wide as possible. Mr Taylor said he thought it would be a mistake to leave in the words " surgical cases." Toe hospital had always been spoken of as a cottage hospital, and they need not go into the details at all at the present time. Mr. R H. Posnett said if there were not some restric- tion placed upon the hnspital,the doctors would flood it with cases he would like to deal with there, and the minister of religion, in his rounds. stw 101 cases he would like to have dealt with in the hospital ; but the hospital they desired to found WAS not meant for such cases. If the word surgical was read in a large and liberal way it would cover all that was neces:ary..</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman said the estimate for the e.stablishment and maintenance of the hospital was basei upon it be- ing a surgical hospital. In reply to Mr. Imison, he slid the estimate was hosed upon the number of beds. Mr. Irnivn : The ait &amp;es not matter how the beds are occupied, the expense would be the same. The Chairman said the Ship Canal Company had madt their promise condidonal upon provisions being made for accident cases.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Norman add the qreat company bad neen the nee] in,the town of a hospital for accident ()tan. One or two beds might always be kept for emergency cases of that kind.</p>
        <p>Mr. Posnett ga'.d it wag generally under4tood that the first object of the hospital was f)r emergency cases of accident. _ _</p>
        <p>Th. resolutinn wls carried ancl the words " surgical cases " were deleted.</p>
        <p>„ Mr. Taylor moved that the sum of at least £l,OOO he raised for the e.tablishment of the hospital by public contribution. Mr. Taylor mentioned that pr,vious glees to establish a hospital hal failed for lack of way. and means, but thremgh the energy mod practical work of the members of the Cycle Club.£3oo hal been raised, not train wealthy people, but from working neonle. _ _</p>
        <p>That fact alone convinced him that the pelple of Run- corn wanted a cottage hospital. (Apnla,ase.) Therefo-e, he would cry" shame" upon hims-lf or awn any indi- vidual in Runcorn who did not encourage the work- p,ople to establish such a valuable and much-needed institution. With the example of the Cycle Clab h.for. them there aught to be no difficulty in raising, £l,OOO, and he was confident there would be no fear of raising £3OO a vPar for its maintenance. 13 shoul.l do his utter- most to keep it off the rates. (Applause.) Mr. W. Ilopth, socre`ary to the Cycle Club. seconded the rrsalution, and said the msmbers of that club who had worked so hard in the past would do the same in the future. The rPsolution TVIFI carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Posnett moved that on !I'm estahlis'imeut of sich hospital the Urban District Council he asked tn take it over, and to Appoint a committee cnnisting partly of non-membnrs of the, Council to manage the same. He believed there would be no difficulty in maintaining the hospital, and was cnnfiient the example of ths S'nin Canal Company would he followed by other works. He would have pleas lre in handing over to the fund £25 from his firm an.l would consult them as to furt'ser help. Mr. Impton seconded the motion, which was carried. The Chairman announced that Mr. A. R. Norgnan bad promised £2O. On the proposition of the Chairman, ths following were appointed a Provisional Cimmittee to deal with the matter :—Messrs P R Vir Booth, A Congreve, W Dudley, Dr F IVlcDougall. James Frith, E Imiion, F .1 Norman, A R Norman. R H Posnett, S Taylor. W Timmins, Rev Canon Word, Me3srs El Wright, and F Wiswall. Rnsnonding to a vnt,a 0? thqnki, th" ¶ salrman said they meant to carry this thing throne!), and mentioned that a deputation had 13N.n aonnintei to wait upon Sir John Brnnner, who wnuld n, &amp;mint givn a donation. The meeting concluded. The donations up to the present are f.—Mr S Tavlor, £100; Mr H Cosgrpv.. £lOO ; Mr F J Norman. £5O; Mr A R Norman. £2O ; Mr F A Lk', £10; Dr Steele, £10; and Miss H C han, £5.</p>
        <p>NATTONAL TOA.ST.—" Here's to John Bull, Pat, and Sandy in Grant's Morella Cherry Brandy, I" Most delicious and comforting (la a damp or frosty day. Try a 3d " nip" of it and yo' will be pleasel. Sold at all bars, also in bottle by Wine Merchants. Don't be nut off with Inferior makes.—THos GRANT Awn SONS, Mailstine, Makers o Cherry Whiskey, Sloe Qin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [By.:o-valist."l • __</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [By.:o-valist."l • __</p>
        <p>The rare pleasure eli to Wsrringion on Saturday of winning by 25 points to nil ; but as the score was com- piled against a second class team playiag only 14 men there was not much glory in it. Moreover the margin was not so great as it should have been, and I cannot compliment V. arriugton on their display of football, a 3 it was of the most mndiecre description. But there is some excuse for this in the fact that only three of the first team backs were playing, and combination there- fore could hardly be expected. The supporters of the club roads a good estimate of the sort of gauze there was likely to he, and the gate was the smallest this season. The " A" team has on one or two occasions attracted a bigger crowd.</p>
        <p>It was j,.st as well for Warrington that Saturday's fixture was of an unimportant character, for had the opponents been in the first division the same team would have had to do duty, and dos result could only have been disastrous. From one cause or anoth-r, principally injuries and infringements of the working clause Warrington had to take the field minus Bate,Fish, Harris, Jones, and Morrison, a quintette of players whose presence means a lot. With the excAption of Harris, whose dislocated thumb will probably keep him out of the field for another fortnight, they will probably all don the jersey for toPday's match with Leigh.</p>
        <p>Birkenhead Wanderers are nothing like sogool a team as they were a season ago. Several of their lo,st players have been poached, and the result is that even in the second division they have a difficulty in holding their own. Watson, Dillon, Glover and Finlan of the older men are still able to give a decent account of themselves, bat the newer blood seems of inferior qaality. The game with Warrington was little more than a farce, though the Birkenhead men played hard to keep the scare down. In the first half.l Warrington played with some smartness, Moores and Isherwood in particular showing good combination. But after the interval there was disgracelul scrambling, and for two thirds of the time there was no play at all worth turn- ing round to see. A few extra tries were put on in demot fashion towards the close, when the score had mounted up to 2 goals, 7 tries.</p>
        <p>Indiv'daally, Moores, who obtained three of the tries and showed some of kis old cleverness, was the best man on the homo side, Isherwood and Duckworth coming next, while Wood and Boscow both shaped well. In the forwards Bien and Merryweather were very conspicu- ous, and the Bristol man gives promise of 'being a very serviceable man in the front rank. Mien. Edmund, and Clayton were the most reliable exponents of the game on the Wanderers' side. Eimunds, the little half hack, is a Mountain Asia man and showed that h's Welsh training had not left him short of cleverness. The s.ven forwards all worked bard aud did not once shirk their duty.</p>
        <p>But, what use is it criticising such an encounter as Saturday's? Nobody took it seriously, and weishall have to wait till this afternoon at Leigh to see what the men can do. They are very anxious to take the Mather- laners down, as the latter have had far too many points at 'Warrington's expense during the past few seasons. It is high time they were taught a lesson, and I hope they will get it this time. Warrington were very near to success last year, and if they try bard they ought to get the better of the match to-day with a full team on. Bate is resuming the captaincy and I hope his rest will have restored him to form. He ought to be far from played cut yet.</p>
        <p>Toe course of events on Saturday caused Warrington to fall c3nsidtrably in the League table, and they are now seventh in the list. A fortnight ago they were third St. Helens won at Stockport, and will now take a lot of overhauling. There shou'd b 3 no fear of Salford however, who seem to go from bad to worse, and got a severe trouncing at Runcorn. Oldham triumphed again at Wigan's expense, and ran up 7 tries to one. Their plor goal kicking may yet cost them dear however. Up to date the League table is as follows t. : For. Against. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P Oldham 11...10... 1... 0...14 39-145- 5 6... 28...20 Runcorn 11-10. 1. 0...18 27-117.- 4 4... 20...23 Swinton l- 8... 3... 0...11 21- 85...14 9... 65...16 St. Helens 5.11... 7... 3... 1... 8 21- 79... 3 9... 33...15 Widnes 12.. 7. 4. 1.. 8 21- 79... 4 12... 44...15 Leigh lO.. 7. 3. 0...16 18- 86...10 12... 55...14 Warrington 12 6. 5. 1.. 9 22- 84.. 8 12... 52...13 Salford 12.. 6. 6. 0...17 28-118-13 19... 83...12 *Broughen Raer'sll... 5... 6... 0... 6 14- 54...10 16... 68... 8 Stockport l2 4.. 8. 0. 7 12- 50...10 22... 86... 8 *Roehdale Hornets 9... 4... 5... 0... 9 6 - 36... 6 19... 69... 6 =ions 10.. 1. 8. 1.. 5 9 - 37...10 26... 98... 3 Tyldesley 13. 1...12... 0... 6 6- 33 ..25 48...194... 2 Wigan 11. 0..11.. 0. 2 4- 16 ..14 34...130.. 0 *Broughton Rangers and Rsehdile Hornets hive each 1;0 two points deducted for breach of rules.</p>
        <p>lam glad to be abl3 to state that the committee's persistent endeavours to secure a centre three-quarter have at last been successful. On Wednesday evening negotiations were concluded with the Wigan club which secured the transfer of W. Smith (late of Penarth), to Warrington, in exchange for C Wood and J Wilcock, the two Welshmen Warringtm imported last season. Smith has played regularly for Wigan, but in such poor company has had little opportunity of distinguishing himself. He is said tn be a vary clever player, and it is</p>
        <p>. . hoped he will prove the on man wanted in Warrington's threc-quarter line- The team to cpupse Leigh was selected on Wednesday ani includes Smith, who will partner Fish on the left wing. The list is as follows : Boscow, full back •, Fish, Smith. Isherwood and Jones, three quarters ; J T Bate and Duckworth. half 13 ieks Taylor, Morrhon, Merryweather, Boardman, Swift, Fairhurst, Eden, and Scholtze forwards.</p>
        <p>This is a strong combination and if all arelable to turn out, as expected, I see no reason why Lsi,gh should not be defeated. Of course Leigh may supply that reason, but they are hardly as strong as they were last season, while Warrington are undoubtedly stronger,and burning to avenge themselves. When Harris is fit for duty once mere the club ought to he able to climb a few places higher in the League, and there will then be an oppor- tunity, ton, of trying Jones at full back, which is, by the way, his real position,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>DOUBLE DROWNING_ FATALITY</head>
        <p>DOUBLE DROWNING_ FATALITY</p>
        <p>AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A DANGEROUS PIT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquiry on Friday afternoon last week at the Black gorse. Farnworth, touching the circumstances attending the deaths of John Dwe.rryhouse and Nathan Atibott.—Mr. Lewin watched the case on behelf of Mr. Brockleb ink, landlord of the Black Horse, and Superintendent ge ghley on behalf of the police.—Joseph Dwerryhnuse. jun., Liverpool-road, Cronton, said that his father was 70 years of age, and he last saw dsim alive about 7.20 on We:A:earthy. night. Ten in,nutee later a man named Platt ca'led them out of the Black Horse to help to get a man out of the pit. Witeese did not know the man was his father, Nathan Abbtt plugged into the water, and as soon as he got in, witness thoight to himself th at he was " a dead one." Witness went into the water up to his waist, threw a rope several Cruse, and called to them, but he got no answer. Wnen they got the bodies out witness went home and changed him- self, and finding that his father had not returned went rraight back te the pond, and found that one of the bodies 'as that of his father. His father was vary fond of snuff, anti he bad gone nut to get some from Elisen'e. He must have lost his way, and gone back by Swift's farm by the fencing. He hal very weak eyesight, eapecially in the dark.—Nethan Abbott, the Lodge, Birchfield-road, deposed to identification of his son's body.—Deceased went out before they heard that a man was in the pit. and witness was told afterwards by a woman that there had been an accident, and he might know who it was. His son had hen of saber and steady habits. Witness had heard that he was a good swimmsr, and had saved :three lives, two children and a man. Mr. Lewin said he heartily sympathized with him, for he knew what decease was.—James Platt said that on the night named he was going along the road with his horse and lurry and thought he could see someone in the part Then the man shouted for help. Wituets ran for assiratice. In reply to the Coroner witness said if it had net been for th lamp on his lurry ha would not have seen the pond at all. It was a very dangerous place.---John Swift, tenant of the farm on which the pond is situated, said that it belonged to Mrs. Wright., of Sussex. Messrs. R Davies and Co., of Warrington, solicitors, were the agents. Witness had been on the farm five or six years. He corroborated the evidence already given. Replying to the Coroner, witness said that the fence was put up when he took the farm. He made use of the pond for watering the cattle. The steps were just as they were at present when he took the farm. Witness admisted that it was a very dangerous place. On a dark night there was every possibility of getting in.—The Coroner in summing no, paid a high tribute to the conduct of the deceased man Abbott. There was no suggestion that Dwerryhouse was drunk. He understocd that the pit had been caused by a plea being excavated for clay. If ' that was so it wonld come within the meaning of the Quarry Act. The fencing %%Ns Oefective, and the para- pet at one point was extremely dangerous, and the lighting of the place was bad. To his mied the roadway ought to have been reconstructed.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and suggested that the pend should either be filled up or adequately fenced, and L lighted.</p>
        <p>TO THE DEAF.—A gentltiman who cured himself o Deafnels and Noises in the Head after fourteen years suffer ng will 'gladly send full particular s of the remedy, on reeeip stampedl. addressed envelope.—F. CLIFTON, Amberle House, strand, London, W.C.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>TtiE LAUREATE'S LATEST,</head>
        <p>TtiE LAUREATE'S LATEST,</p>
        <p>What 7:9 it makes the whole world kin? What is it each of us ought to take What is it heals the broken skin? When starting out on bike or brake? Is it not Homocea? Surely'tis Homocea. (Cuoitus) °mauls : Then surely we'll stick to what we know, What is it "always touches the spot," And take Homocea wherever we go: 1 And heals whatever sores we've got? Hurrah! Homocea! Hurrah! I Nothing but Homocea. (Caoßus.) *N.B.—Not the Poet Laureate, he couldn't have done it; but the Spot-touching Laureate, your friend, HOMOCEA.. For BURNS, BOILS, BRUISES, BITES, and BROKENI SKIN; i USE For CHAPPED HANDS, CHILBLAINS, CUTS and " ..: COLDS; } HOMOCEA For STINGS, SCALDS, SCRATCHES, SWELLINGS SORES; FREELY. For PILES and ALL PECULIAR PAINS WITHIN :j</p>
        <p>hindered the spread of the Gospel. There were class distinctions in England but they did net compare With the caste system in India. In India tnere were bard and fast lines which nothing could get over. If a man were horn in a certain caste he remained there all his life. The principles of Christianity and caste were diametri- cally opposed to one another. Christiarety taught that all men were equal ; caste said that God was a respecter of persons—and cared more for some than for others. Caste separated man from man arid toek away all the humanity out of man's heart. He instanced a terrible railroad accident, in which the spectators would not render the sufferers any • assistance because they (the epectators) were ef-a higher caste and would pollute themselves if they touched people of lower caste than theineelvee. It was very hard for one of these men to become a Christne. It as a terrible struggle. Mere was the terrible sense cf pollution ; he was persecuted be his fellows, he had to !wave his horn?, and his ptrents and ht. ethers and sisters cursed him and spat upon him. It took a great amount of cenyictien to bring a man to 'ake such a step. Many men, however, did c me out; thank God for it. Perhaps more often there were many men, who though persuadel, dared not take the rtep. They were secret worshippers. Teets the religion of Hincloriism was a. great obstacle. It was a conglomeration of religions of all sees. New sects serene, up with new doctrines, and it was considered most expedient to take aunt in. They therefore found contradictory doctrines in Hin- dooism, incoherent and inconsistent. Tonna were truths on one hand, and on the other doctrines vile beyond description. It divided its into two paws : there was popular Hindeoism and philosophictl Elindeoiern They were widely different. tlot of the people were idolatsrs, and in their religion there was everything to debase thew. All the vices of humanity were attributable to their gods, and on the principle " that as be gods are, so are the worshippers " the people lived the worst possible liven Tnei: metal sense was darkened and they had no spiritual apprehension of things. They had no cerscierrce,ancl that was the great difficulty the missienariee met vrith. Tiny talked to the people about spiritual things, but they did not compre- hend. The speaker then entered into an expoeition of philosophical Hindooism. Cheistiarsity was opposed by these cultured Hincloos hecarwe they knew that it would break down caste and that they were determined to up- hold. But despite all Oppositien Christianity was win- ning its way. Hindooisim must fall to plecea by reason of its own rottenness. All over India thel) was evidence of the progress of Christianity. The people were begin- nine te realise that their own gale were false, and in all conditions of societe thry were beeineing to have faith in the new rolieion. Whole villages were crying out for' teachers, but they could not receive attention in many cases for lack of sufficient fun is. In time he hoped the people theinselYes would becuine their own teachers. The time must come when the peoplo. would turn to the only true religioe, the religion of Jesus Christ and when our posterity and their posterity would b , worshipping the same God, our Father in H ,even. (Applauee ) The Rev. W. C. WILLOUGIIBY in speaking of the diffi- culties experienced in Smarr Alrela referred to the rela- tions between !he white and the black people. If they asked any South Aftec Lie politicien at the present time, eupnesing he could get the war out of his mind for a moment, whet the great polite-ell protein's.% there was,be would answer the native question. It was a question which was always with them, and one which sornetimee frightened their politicians. What were they pine to do with the censtantly increasing native; of Africa ? The great fact was this ; that for mane years to come, per. hap; for a century, tee brain of Snell Africa was going to be covered by white skin and the muscle of South Africa by black skin, and the preteens was how were they . going t arrange things 'shit the brain and the reeeele would work beautifully, harmoniously and satisfactorily together? Hew were they ping to guide these two people into paths which tended towards human prosperity ? The white man tcok fireedom wherever he went. Tee black man working alongside him breathed thet freedom eferond-hione arid cried out for more. Freedom was bad without an accompanying sense of responsibility, arid the 1 eiter trAi wnat they had to teach the black man. Theitnim was to do that induetrialty and spiritually. (Hear, hear.) Unless we uplifted the natives we steeled pay the penalte. Oar sons and brothers who were being cent out to SouthAtrics would he pulled down to the depths of the natives, and the depths of African heateeniene were vere deep indeed. If England with her hands in almrst evere part of the world did net recognise her duty to the netveti of those lands by uplifting diem she would pee the penalty, and the penalty was a very heavyone. „ (Applauce ) A collection was taken in aid of tee London Missionary Society. During the meeting several hcmns were sung. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mug. Bac., presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
