        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. R. Tarbuck and Local Reservists. The Rev. E R. Tarbuck, at till close of his sermon at St. Paul's Church, on Sunday evening,alluded to the war in South Africa, and warned his hearers against a false kind of patriotism that existed now-a-days. He spoke of the recent departure of Resservis:s from Warrington, and said they ought not to feel proud of sending their soldiers off to the station in such a condition that some of them were not ala!e to walk straight. Had they for- gotten the object for which the war was begun ? Was public opinion in the 'tewn so low :hat they were satisfied to show their loyalty in the way some did last Monday ? There was a reason f r tilking about thfss matters, and he most earneatlr asked them with regard to th,.rsilts of the war made known from day to day to pray for the real object in view—the extnqion of Chris' a kingdom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Gospel Hall, Forster-Street.</head>
        <p>Gospel Hall, Forster-Street.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, Mr. G. Barton of alancls.sler preached the •Gi-spel from the cleanlie4 of the Leper (see Lev. 13 and 14). Leprosy was a well known type of sin, there was no known cure for it apart from God. It was contagious and therefore a leper had to go away, from home and friends and if anyone came near him he must cry unclean. Ho would not like to admit he was a leper at first, and his dear friends would not lk to admit it, but it mattered not what his friends or him self thought, what did Gael's representative the Pries- think ? No man will single himself out as a sinner, not do his friends like to admit it. Friend Unitarian tellr him he has no need of the blood and Friend Socialist says he wont go to Heaven on the back of another. Ho will use his own efforts, but what says God? God tries man without law, he provss a failure. Neat with law, still a failure. Now man is beieg tried under Grace which spurns and prov-,s hims.,lf to be at enmity with God. The way in which the Lep-ir was cleaus,cl ehowa bow Christ died for our sins and was raised for our jestifica- ti on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Position and Prospects of the English Catholic Church.</head>
        <p>The Position and Prospects of the English Catholic Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. E. Powell, Vicar of St.i3rl3o3'. Latchford, preached the last of a cours of sermons on the crisis in the church. on Sunday evening, when he took for his subject " The position and prospects of the English Catholic Church." During the course of his sermon he obser,ecl that the English Churchman conceived of the Church not as a society formed by men who held to particular views of religion, but as a society founded by Christ whose members had been initiated into it by baptism. Just as by natural birth they were mem- bers of' the great natural family of man, so by spiritual leh.th they were members of ,he ereat spiritual family. Some of these members conformed to the principles and usages of the historic church ; some did not conform. But both Conformists and Nonconformists alike were members of the one Holy Catholic and Apostolic Chorale by baptism. Membership did not depend on bow a man grew up, but on the fact of his initiation. 'raja being so, the only diffrrenne, so far as organisetion went, be- tween Chris:iaos should be one of geography: 'roe Roman conception of the Church was imperial—the eathnlic Church was to be subject to the Roman Curia, which was the chief enemy of Christian unity. The Protestant conception of the Church was opinionative-- all who agreed with certain opinions were asked to come together and unite to form independent churches. Tire Anglican conception of the Ceurch was neither imperial nor opinionheive. The English Church did not want to interfere with the Church ire Prance, Italy, or America, but she was equally determined that no foreign bishop should interfere in England's Church. On the other band, the English Church should be prepared to tolerate a very wide divergence in oeinion and praetice, for her members were not asked to accept any one men's views —whether high, low, or bro ei—of the Scriptures, the ministry, the sacraments and the creeds—hut simply those great cardinal facts themselves as they had come down to them through two millenniuzns They should aim at no blind uniformity but at a rational :Intl sober unity. They longed for reunion with the great Apcstelic See of Rome, with its splendid traditions, with its long list of martyrs and saints, with its world-wide privileges of Christian communion. They longed for what seemel likely to be shortly accomplished—reunion with the great Eastern Church which sturdily resisted with them the encroachment of the eepaly. They longed too for re- union with their Nonconformist brethren at home with their magnificent zeal and earnestness, their splendid stand for liberty of conecience and freedom of thought. But while they prayed and longed for reunioe with these their fellow Christians they could not accept terms from Rome which they did not believe to correspond to the original deposit of revealed truth,nor could they abendon their hold on Apostolic order for the sake of mere temporary gain among their Nenconformist friends. The position and opportunity of the Anglican Church were unique and hopeful. She alone of the reformed Christian bodies of the 167 h century retained her hold on and the continuity with the Caeheile past. A well-known leader of Methodism in the United Sat(l3 gave recently as his firm conviction that Me'llediste,Presbyterians,and Congregationalists, were being drawn with greater and greater attraction to the Anglican communion. Let English Churchmen but rise to the height of their glorious opportunity, standing firm on the rock of principle while extending their arms wide enough to embrace all sorts and conditions of men.</p>
        <p>Foreign Missionary Anniversary at Wycliffe Church. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The -amnia! Foreign Missiodary S ,rvices in concec- E'en with Wycliffe Congregational Church were held on Sunday. At the morning service the preacher was the Rev. J. Yonge, who based a missionarY address upon the text Romans, 10 chapter, 12, 13, 14, and 15 verses, "There is no difference botween the Jaw and the Greek" &amp;c. In the f!our;e of his address he stated that En,gland owed her position te-day to the fact that cen- turies ago missionaries came to our island home and preached and taught our Prefathers the ?ell -ion, which was such a source of comfort to us to-day. The sepreme wealth of this country was not its millions of money ; not in its thriving populatien ; not in its army and navy ; not in its loyal colonies all empire, but in its Christian faith. England's greatness rested upoe the gospel of the Lord Jesus Christ. What had blessed and benefitted us would be of Ferylee to other nations. The obligation was laid upon le, to do our share towaris the evangelisation of the wreld.—ln the afternoon the Rev. w. C. Willeughby. from Smith Africa, addressed the Sunday school scholars and friends i a the sehordrnom. Mr. George Wallineten, the superintendent, conducted the meeting. The Missionary gave a most interesting address on his work in the Khama's country. He also delivered an address at the evening service in Wycliffe Church, referring to tha beneficial effects which followed the introduction of Christianity into Bechuanaland Collections were taken at each service on behalf of the London Missionary Society.</p>
        <p>The Missionary Meeting. The Rev. J YONGE pre,ided over a we:-Ittended mig- sionary meeting on Tuesday evening. held in Wycliffe Church. Re was acoomnaniei within the communion mill by the Rev. E flawker BA. missionary at Coimbaoor, India and the Rev. W C Willonghby of South Africa. •</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that while th-i, missionary gather- ings were not sn large as they were 30 or 40 years leo the evidence of their missiolary funds in general clearly in- dicated that the spirit of mission rnileav •ur had tiT. no means died away,but that their churches did feel the re- sohnsibility of the great cue and that the people were willing to give of waat God had givna them, in or ler that the glad tidings which were such a source of joy and peace to them might go throughout the world. (Ap- plause.) The Landon Missionary Society was one in which most of them took a very particular interest, an -1 the mere they gist to know of the society the more interest they would take in it. It fell to his lot to have somethincr to do very freq tangy during the coot's() of , twelve months with the Missionary Society in some of li its important committess,and he could assure them that their meetings were always of the greatest nossible inter- est. They heard many things which made their hearts glad, and sometimes they had to listen to things which made them anxious and full of care. The society never stood so well with the churches as it did at the pv.s,--nt time, and evidence of God's word by this means was never 5) plentiful nor so wonderful as in many cases they were now. He hope I what they would hear from the two missionaries would stimulate them to fresh exer- tions on b^half of the work, and that their coltrihn tion to the parent society would be even larger than it was last year. (Applause). The 13,1 r. E. HAWKER in a most interesting address reviewed the position of Christianity in India fle said that India was great in many resnects. It was a great lard, i- had a great history, great sciences, and great philosophers, while its people were in many respects a great people. They professed to regard Christians as oiecasts, and sometimes when spoken to of the .religion of Jesus Christ they said it was a new story and that long before that religion existed they werea great nation land had their own philosophy and religion. Theirs.thev said, was the older religion and therefore the better. In referring to the caste sys'em in India, he sail it would Ibe very hard to convey to his hearers' minds any 1 alequate conception of whit that was, and how it</p>
