      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEFORE the NI AGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the NI AGISTRATES</p>
        <p>SERIOUS ASSAULT ON A BUILDER. At the Warrineton Police Court. on Friday, William Mc Cue, 26. Gnlhorne-street was summoned for assault. ing Frederick William Wright, builder, 27, St. Mary's— street, on the 14th inst. Mr. F. R. Roberts appeared for the prosecution. He said that on the date in question defendant entered the employ of Mr. Wright, and ve-s put on a jib in Padgatc-late. At 11 o'clock Mr. Wright sent to see how he was cling his work and not being satisfied with it, told him to leave off working at dinner time. It appeared that the man had been drinking at the "King and Queen" Inn instead of doing his woik. When told to leave his work he hit Mr. Wright a violent blow behind the ear with his fist. He then took a staff beam that Mr. Wright had in his hand and belaboured him with it for several minutes. The assault was a very serious one.—Defendant who did not appear was Com- mitted to gaol for a month with hard labour. ATTEMPTED SUICIDE. —A FORTUNATE MISTAKE. At the Warrington Police Court, on Saturday, bafere Messrs. Birchen, (in the chair), J. W. Hayward and R. T. Fairclnugh, William Miliington was brought up on a charge of attempting to elmmit suicide at the house of his daughter, 77. Laira-street, by drinking a quantity of carbolic oil.—The Chief Constable said the man was a. general labourer. He went to his daughter's house be- tween six and seven o'clock on Friday night. It appeared that twelve months ago he was in a state of unsound mind and his tendency was said te be suicidal,so he was sent to Raintill Asylum and remained there three or four months when he was discharged as cured. He went to his daughter's house in a very despondent state, eat down, and said to his daughter," I have come to see you and say geod-bye, lam tired of my life. I have that in my pocket whieh will end it all." The daughter became frightened and ran out to look for as- sistance. A pers ,u named Buckly was in the neieh- bourhood and they went to the house. They found the man was still there, but he said "It is too late I have drunk it now." He further said •6 If this does not do it you will find me in the eranal at Buttermilk Bridge. Tile bottle produced was found, and contained a fluid im- pregnated with carbolic acid. The label had been scratched off. Inquiries were made and it was found that the man had gene to Mr. Ashton's in Rankers -st and askod for three pennyworth of carbolic oil. Ile meant carbolic aeld but fortunately got what he asked for, as carbolic oil contained 20 per cent of carbolic acid, and 80 per cent. of olive oil. The police were sent for, and P.S. Evans arrived and administered an eenetic and castor 01. The man was then taken to the Infirmary and a sten:tech pump was applied, but it was an hour and twenty minutes before be was pronounced to be out of danger. He was thee removed to the Police station, and wag watched all night. Taking into consideration that the men was in a despondent mato over real or imaginary trouble he would withdraw the charge, and ask the maeistretes to make an crier for prisoner's detention in the Work- house, where his stets of mind could be properly inquired into.—The magistrates made an order fee.. prisoner to go to the Workhouse fer 14 days. SCENES AT WARRINGTON PUBLIC HOUSES—A BARMAN BADLY USED. On Monda.e, at the Warrington Police Court, before the Mayor (in the chair), and other magistrates, Michael Lyons, wheee address was elven as Plinston's lodging house, Rose and Crown-street, was cha-ged with being, drunk and discrderly in Mersey-street at 9.5 on Satur- day night, and with breaking a quantity of glass at the Leigh Arms, Mersey-street, doing damage to the amount of £3, and also with assaulting David Wootton, and Austin Connolley Broe.kee at the gams time.—Austin Con- nolley Brookes, a barman, in the emploe of Messrs Peter Walker and Sons, at the Leigh Arms, Mersey-street, said that at nine 'clock on Saturday night he was at the bar, when prisoner came in and asked for a pint of beer. He was under the influence of driek, FO witness refused to serve him. Prisoner then made use of filthy language and tried to drink anotker man's bee:. Wit- ness asked him to go out several theses and tried to eject him. Prisoner dragged him into the street, threw him on the ground, and kicked him on the shins and forehead. He also bit his lip. Witness got away arid went back to the hat and closed the door. Prisoner then put his hand through one nane of glass and his head through another. The panels of glass were rather expensive. Prisoner then ceme into the vault again. Witness was it the act of pickine his apron no when he put him on the fleor and kicked him again. They had a struggle on the flier and prisoner nut his fingers in his (witnesi's) mouth, He then picked Rotw glasses up and threw them across the counter at him, but he dodged them.—David Wootton, the licensee, said that he heard the last witness refnee to serve prisener. He saw the man put his head anti hand threugh the window. He also picktd up some glasses and threw them about. The panes of glass were worth abeut 50s. and the glasses, 10s.—Detectiv0 R ,Ofr spoke to arresting the prisoner.— The Chairman said the Bench were fully determined to protect any license who was fully determined to kern his house respectable. They had no sympathy with any breach of the licussing law but they were determined to protect thoseewho tee Ito keep th -rnselvei fihe from that sort of thing. They thought the prisoner was to blame for what occurree and they should give him a month with bard labour.—George Hopkine and James Hopkins were charged with heine &amp;unit and disorderly in Butterma-le t-s:reet at 10.33 on Saturday night end also with refusing to quit the " /Helier Angel " Inn when requested to d so—Robert Perdoe. licensee of the "Higher Angel" lun, said that at 10.25 the prisoner James Hopkir. s came in. He was drunk. He asked for beer but on account of hia condition be (witnees) refused to serve him. He then commenced to create a distur- bance with the other men who were in the pace, and in the scrirem age which ensued several glasses were broken George then came in also in a drunken condition, and asked for drink. Oa aeseuet of his condition he wae re- fused, Both pr:seners then became very abusive and he asktd them to leave the place. G sorge picked up a stool and threatened to hit him with it. Tae men we7e ejected and they coot inued itheir disor.lerly conduct out- side. They wanted to fight everyone. The police came up and hetmeyed them—PC..Miliingt on said the mee be- haved liketwe madmen. Toey were very violent at the Police Station and relived to he searched. TAPP had to pull the firet prisoner's beets off.—The Chid Con- stable said that was George's 46 h appearance. He had been before the Court three timea fer refesing se gu:r.</p>
