        <p>six times for datnage and assault on licensed ' premises, I and had also ben convicted for drunkenness and assaul- ting the police. James had made 22 appearances. The men were simply a terror to public house keepors.—The Chairman said the 13,mch would give prisoners the option of paying £5 and coats or going to gaol for six week; with hard lannut • Snocxma TintA.TMENT OF A FrOESE—A 111,1110LN FINED. At the Diresb iry Petty Seasions on Tuesday, Jos via flignett, a canal boatman. was au•nmoued for illcreating I horse at Moore, on th" 18;h November by working it I while it was in an u.,fit ceodNon.—fle plealed not I guiltv.—P.C. Ellis stated Ma; in consequence of infor-1 mation he received on the 18th Novembar fnm Mrs. Conerave at Moore, he proceeded to Runcorn, where he saw the defendant in charge of a fist (Wien with 53 tons nf s'ack).,hich crag being pullei up the canal by a none, which he examined. 'Ha found 6everai large raw sores on the shoulder. where the c)llar had been, and near the wither-he found atu-ther la.rge wound. The legs were also creaUv s molten . It seemed t' b. suff rlng great pain and irrespective of the wounds it was not fit for work. Ha te the defendant abut it, alai he said that he had complaiied to a o°l...keener abut the horse's collar and he had been in.tructe Ito get it altered. He had gone an eight hours journey, but it had taken them 11i hours—P.C. nerringtnn, stationed at Runcirn,.aid that on the 18th Nwomber the horse was brought to him at the Min* Station by the last witless. It was examined and the wounds described by the last witness were ford. some of tbrm were bleeding a great deal. The defendant had t..ld him that the horse belonged to a Mr. Abel. a boat proprietor and carrier, but he (witness) had learned on making inquiries that it had been bought off Mr. Abel by the defenlant.—Virs. Mary Congrave, of Moore corroborated. Dafendant was Rood 10s, and , costs.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. Warrington Borough Police Court. Fewee.y.—Before the Mayor (in the chair). Alderman Wright, Councillor Smethurst. Dr Gornall, and Mr. tlepherd. Recenearonv PAUPERS —James Mater,. George Evans and James Evans. inmates of the casual ward at the Workhouse, wece committed to gaol for a m snth with hard labour for refusing to perform toe task of work set them by the_inester. IMPUDENT TnF:rr. -George Garner. of no settled abode. was charged with stealing a bungle of braces from the shop door of William Clegg. drapes., Irlam-street, at 3.30 the pre- vious efteenoon.—P isoner was seen by a woman to take the braces, and ween he found out that he was detected he brought tbem be&amp; and wanted to shade hands with Mr. OltlitZ• Information was given to the police and the MAR I wee apprehended by P.C. Hodgkiss.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 days. 1 A SCANDAL.—WiIIiam Horton was charged with deserting his wife, Hannah Horton, on the '.sth October.—dr. W. B Fowler, solicitor, appeared for complainant. He stated that the case had been before the Court previously, and was adjourned. RS acts of infidelity were alleged against com- plainant. He would now call evidence to show that com- plainant had always been faithful to her husbend. He admit- ted the desertion, and compleinant asked for a separation order.—Evidence was then called, and it was stated that the parties; had only been married three months.—The Chairman said that to ask for a separation order after.three months mended life was a scandal. They could live together well enough if they tried His colleagues could make what order the- liked, but he would not consent to it.—An order wss granted, defendant to pay lie towards the maintenance of his wire and famity.—The Chairman : It is a decision I strongly dissent from. lam very sorry to disagree with my colleagues. PE -SISTENT CRUELTI TO A WIFE —Alfred Berry, 10, Canterbury-street, WsB summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife, Ada Mary B -rinr —Ai% Browne proeecuted and Mr. Forshaw appeared for the defence—After hearing the evi- dence, the magistrates granted a separatio a orler, defendant to pay its a week towards the maintenance of his • wife and child ASSAULTS. —John Riley. 26. Jockey-street, was fined 58 and costs for assaultine his wife on the 20th inst. ; Joseph Tom- linson, 20. Crossley-street, was fined 5s and costs for assault- ing his wife on the 20th inst. Meemay.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Councillors Bmethurst and Shave Green and Messrs. Hayward and Birchen. FCCXE iv WINWICS-STREET.—Ants Jane Finn, Wooton'a lodeing house. Bank-ES-red, was charged wizn being drunk and disorderly at 9,15 on Saturday nieht, and Thomas Llewellyn, 6 Bissel-atreet, was charged with obstructing P.C. Johnson whilst in the .xteution of his ditty at the i seine time.He was also charged with assaulting James Collins whilst assisting the police. -P.O. Johnson sad said that at 9 10 on S sturcley night lee WAS on duty in Winwiok-street when he saw the first eft sneer who was very drunk, in com- peres, with a man, creating a disturbance. There were 200 or 330 people standing round He adyisel her te.go borne. The man went awey, bet she said she had no home to go to, He was obliged o take her into custody. Wh,n he did so, she threw herself down on the ground. " threw her heels up" and screamed at the tee of her voice. He ha.l to get the assistance of a man named Collins to carry her. When they got noir Central Station. the senond prisoner came up and hit Collies in the face, kicked him on the shins. and rats away into the crowd. Witness caught him and handed him over to P.C. Metcalf. He had to get a handcart to tike the woman to the Police Station.—James Collins. corroborated. Llewellyn said that he w-nt to see who was being locked up and the poll:semen made a rush at him and knocked him dewn.—The magistrates exoreised their approv a of the action of Mr. Collins and committed Finn to prisonfor a month ant Llewellyn for two months with herd labour. MINOR OFFENCES Catharine Murray, 16 S7llver-street,was fined 5s and costs for niikinz use of obs en, language on the 21st November.—Patrio'c Gillick, Steckton Heath, was fined 5s and costs and costs for obstructing Market-street, on the 22nd November by allowing a horse ant cart to stand there loneer than necessary for loading and unloading. —aeorge Brown was fined 5s for beine drune auel disorderly in Gel- borne-street et 8.20 on Saturday ni /ht. TETEID i.Y.—BM'cre Mr. Biretta!! (in the chair), and Councillo</p>
        <p>Shaw Green. THe FLoSTING POPULATION. A middle aged woman named Mary San- Corney was charged with having b.ea drunk and disorderly in Brid e-street on Monday afternoon- -P.C. Dennett Blotted that the prisoner stopped a large number of people and Raked for money as. she seld, she " wanted something to eat."—The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner had beers t Klee previously brought before the Bench oo charges of drunk rtness. She was one of the floating population.--ehned se. and costs. WANTED T • 01T . Tan WAR. —&amp; y one man nettled Robert Huston was charged with being a desert -r from the tat Battalion West Yorkshire Regiment stationed at York on December 24th. 1896 —A. Corporal from Orford Barracks stated that the prisoner had reported himself as being a deserter from the battalion. anal he s sbeeqenttly hanle I him over to the civil authorities. Dee of the Yorkshire regiments was now on day he South Africa. -Courseillor Shaw Green ae iced the nrisoner if it wee owing to present circumstances that he had given himself up. Prieoeee (evidently not under- standing the question) : No sir : but I want tsgo te south Africa. (Laughtter.) The Chief Constable: I aria afraid you will miss the boat. (Renewed laughter )—He was remanded to await the arrival of the military em ort WEDNEIDAY.—Befpre Alderman iintton (In the chair). tiro Alderman James Fkaelongh,Uessrs iGeddes and Brehall. UNDER THE SEAT.—William HammOndand Robert Bnaery. youths, were chergerl with travelling on the Cheshire Lines Railway from Liverpool to Warrington, without paving the fare on the previous morning,—Mr. Winters appeared on behalf of the Railway Cempener. le said that on the arrival of the 1).30 train from Liverpool the prisoaers were found underneath the seat In a co-npartment by the foreman. Ho got them (eat of the carriage and asked them for their ticiets. They said they had not got any tickets or money. He asked them what they had to say. and they replied thatthey w ere. gale