        <p>TEWPORARY AUTHoRIT-Y".::is:jri-e;-1.79,d trilde apelication for, ant was granted, temporary authority to sell at the C sadeley Arms, Vrodshern, null the next license to-anger de v. N EGLEC r OF VACCINATLON.—CIharIes William elyzAr, Fradsh im, for neglecting to have his child vaccineted within the prescribed time was fined El including costs.—Kvideuce Was given by Vaccination (Miler Riley, of the Runcorn Union.—Judgment went by default, DRUNICENNESS.—Pqm Williams and Joseph Wright. both of Frodsham, were fined se. for a first oft snee of drunken- ness.---iareh Garner, for being drunk on icaused premises was fined ss. and Bs. 6,4. costs. ALLEGED FFENCE UNDER THE FOID3 AND DRUGS anr ---.` BUTTER THAT WAS 11A.RGARINE "—Joseph G. Kydcl. grocer, Uhurch-street, Fradshana, was summon° i at the in- stance of air. W. J. Mallard, Inspector of weights and measures &amp;C.' to the Cheshire County Council, for selling by the hands of his servant George Bowers. " margarine" whioh was net of the nature, substance and quality of the article demanded by the purchaser. who demanded a "pound of butter" at Frodshem on the 6th of November last ;ani also with failing to deliver the same in or with a paper wrapper having printed thereon in capital letters " mar- garine." Mr. 11. N. Linaker, solicitor, Runcorn I who defended plead d not guilty on b half of his client.—Rvidence for the pros-cuti in was submittal I by Mr Holland and Miss Wien Set, of West Kirby, to the effect, that the latter went into the shop of the deteniant on the 6th ult., and asked the prices of butter. Rogers the assistant said Is. is Id, Is 2s and is 3d per lb. Miss Salt then purchased two pounds of butter at is and Is 2s respec- tively. Inspector Holland then entered ths shop and told the assistant he wanted the " butter" for purposes of public analysis, and the sample was divided and forwarded to the county analyst who certified that the • Is butter" wss mar- earine. Subsequently Mr Kyeld came into the shop and said to the Inspector his assistant had made a mistake.—For the defence Mr Linaker contended thst the Inspector did not use the words " public anvalyst '' which was essentiel---Vlr. Holland stated he did use the words, but even if he had not it would not have mattered —Mr. Linaker further contended that at the very most they were guilty of but a slight mis- take, which arose through the fact that the papers lit which they wrapped the " margarine" all hung together. and it had been found that though the msjority o them had the word" margarine" priaterl upon them, there were a few blank ones which had evidently been rills Antal He sub- mitted sample sheets of paper in support of this. Again, he point d out that people often asked for" butter " at as low a price as Bd. but that when they Weie told they could only have •• margarine" at that price they replied • But it's the same thiog." -G-m-ge Rogers. assistant. acid &amp;nest Collin- son, manager, of tic. K vdd's shop. were called, and swore that the Inspector did not use the words" public analyst." When asked the price of butter Rogers said he replied Is Id, Is 2d and Is 3,1 aid only gave ' three priess no four.—The Bench decided to dismiss the first charge, but held UlO A slight technical often es had been committed in the second, but tempted the probability of its being a mistake and therefore they could not impose a penalty.— A charge by Mr. Kychl. against his assistant Rogers for selling to his pre- judice was then withdrawn</p>
