      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Great Sankey School Board.</head>
        <p>Great Sankey School Board.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Great Sankey School Bard was held at tne Board Scaool, Great Sankey, on Tuesday evening. Mr. T. Halton presided, and there were also present—Uesers. Wright, and J K ilshaw ; W. D. Jeans, Jun. (clerk), George Clarke (schoolmaster), and B. shearer (school attendance offi2er). The Clerk real the scnooltnaster's momthly report, whien stated that the average attendance during the past month had been 238.6 and the fees received .£2 19s. It was also stated that during the past month measles and scarlet fever had been pre- valent at Sankey Bridges, and there were now 25 'children absent who are affected by these diseases. —Mr. Clarke said he had received a letter from Dr. Garnall, medical officer of health, asking if he took in children from houses in which there were dis- eases, and he had written him saying that they were very careful under such circumstances,and in so doing they had not had to close the schools. Mr. Clarke also stated that the children had collected £1 for the Mayor's fund in aid nt the army reserves.— Pie Clerk read the Inspector's annual report, as follows, which was termed by the Chairman as very satisfactory :—" Mixed school : The school is in very good order. The teachers are industrious and painstaking,and the children are well advanced in their work, .which is carefully planned and tested. Infants' school : The general condition of this small class continues satisfactorily. (loot order is kept, and the teacher employs suitable methods of instruction, with success."</p>
      </div>
