      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. Warrington v. Birkenhead Wanderers</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. Warrington v. Birkenhead Wanderers</p>
        <p>Wa,rrington—had an off day from Competition match'lli on Saturday, and the open date was filled up by Birken- head Wanderers, who arrived with only 13 men. Another turned up corm alter the start of the game which brought the visitors' strmegth up to 14 men. The home team had a large number or absentees, including Bae, Fish, Jones, Harris. and Morrison. There was only a small gate, about 2,000, when the teama Heel up in the following order Warrington: B -iecow, full balk; Moores. Isherwood, Ashurat, and W3ll, three-quarters; Doek worth and Age %, half backs; Taylor, Boardman. ochol ze, Flattop, M rrvweather, Swift. E len. and Fairhurst', forwards. Birkenheal Wanderers : Dillon, full back ; Clayton, Lewis. Phillips, arid Pumford, three-quarters ; Watson and Edmonls, half backs ; Glover, Finlan, Davies, Dattne, Kidd, Jones, and Clok, forwards. Birkenhead stated the game, Allen returning. Nice paesing by Warrington cwried play to the line where M vire; was grassed. Ai e IST attack was maintained, W ,ocl trying to drop a goal and failing nalserablv. W irriegton passed again, hut Ashurst mulled the ball. Wood was fortunately in ri•tmdance and smartly pick- iug up he ran over the line, Taylor failing at goal. Toe Wanderers made some headway after this.Ciavton ree'is- t ,rinr, a deal ball. Warrington were much the smarter b.hind the pack and soul mananed the line, Duckworth Isherwood, and Moores by a fine movement scored a second try from which Isherwnod kicked a goal. Taylor added another by forning himself over from a scrum near the line, lant there was no goal. Moores soon after scored another fine try and Iseerwood hit the posts with his g )al kick. The interval came v:rith Warrington lead- in' ny 14 points to nil. In the second half the play fell off coneiderably and it was a long time before Warring. ton eta led at all well. Elmende eeveral times oil smart work for the Wanderers, while Dillon was strong on the defence. Duckworth, Ashurst, awl Wood passed neatly, and the latter got in at the corner. Once started and the scoring cnntinued, Merryweather gain- ing the 6th try near the p icta b; good forward play. Duekworth was successful with the goal kick. Jost before the close Moores ant in very smartly at the cornet% and the final score read : Warrington. 2 goals 7 tries (25 points) ; Birkenhead, nil.</p>
        <p>COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP. Cumberland v. Yorkshire. NAither side anonami at full atrenvh f )r thi4 match, whilh was played at Millom. Yorkshire win by two o als and one try ti a goal and a try. LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Salford Defeated at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Salford Defeated at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The visit nt the S Ilford team tn Runcorn last Satur- day' prove] as interesting an elynit as Inn., and quite a laree coleours of sp,ctat )r5 assembled to witness the cent's. The teims which were th :roughlv represented were as follows : Smith, back ; Hadwen, T Williams (CAntain) Tnernas and Pe irson. threeqnsrters; Doran awl Griffiths. halves; Tunney. J Williams,Heath, Shaw, Uoweilyo, Fisher, Jones and Howarth. forwards. Rugcorn : flonzhtnn, back Wilkinrnn, Warder J Butterworth,aad C B itterworth.threequarters; Faulkner and Jolley, htivs; Walker. Firmer, F Gayter, Light- foot, Darlington, 11,ece, Tomlinson and Messenger, forwards.</p>
        <p>The game preyed a decidedly keen one throuzhout. At the commoneemeat a t Trifle straggle was main- tained iii mid-field, and for a. cenaiderable time neither aide secured any material advantage. The heeling out of Salrord was perfect and the ball was veered by them on almost (aattry occasion. Si k.oenly, however, did the " linnets" taelee that no hoed way could be made by the visitors; and slowly but steadily they were borne down en to to their owa lines. Runcorn forwards then secured advent ge by screwing the scrummages and lawful footwork gave them a further adventage. Smith was hard pressed but accounted fir his charge superbly. Butterworth next got an opening from a judicious pass from Faulkner. but he was grassed berme toe try was secured liadwen was pext especi- ally Rood and got grandly away. Griffitha. aed Thomas further improved the chance. Houghton re- plying with renerkab'e tact neutraii.ed and was deser- vedly applauded for superb work. Elia final kick, was usefully improved up VI by Walker and a scrummaga re- sulted from which Jolley secered and dropped a fine goat. Rencorn pressed stronely, the Salford defance being severely taxa I. Deran in stopoine a rush got his knee dislocated so badly as to necessit ite *Dr. 15dacLen- non's attention. H. was finally c arrie I off the field by members of the local ambulance corps. Jones took his , place behind the ecrimmaes. and though coesirlera.biy weakened Salford more than held their own to the inter- val. At that poriol the sore stood Rencorn 2 points; Salford Che aeronrimeiety saw the h ame team get off remaikablv well. Through the instrumentality of Butterworth, Faulkner and Wilkinson the inveninn was seen complete and Salford were kept to th‘ir 25 line* Wilkinson and Warder caused Smith same anxiety, but the seige was raised by Smith and Houghton was visited The veteran was thoroughly himself, and responded to all corners with alacrity. Thomas gave Pearson a chance which he took finely but Houghton collared him just in the nick of time, hut was forced to send into touch. Jolley then initiated a teevement in which F auntier and the two Batter worths were concerned and Clarke just managed to get in at the corner. Farmer took this kick but failed. Runcorn forwards now developed an amaz- ing tern of speed and so hampered the Salford b tcks, that it was not lone before J Butterworth was enabled to race through all opposition and score a eecond tre. Farmer again failed. Sa,lrord then firered at their best and Tunney, Pearson, Griffiths and Will'ams backed up by Smith made things considerably lively for Runcorn. Recovering the boat advantage Warder was given a chance and placed betvfeee the sticks, for Farmer to convert. .t.talford Waved well nn to the end, and on one occasion got over the Rnncorn line, C Butterworth only ?just deadening the hall by falling on it shortly before ' time. The end (tem. with Runcorn winners by 2 goals 3 tries (13 points) ; to nil.</p>
      </div>
