        <head>Stockport v. St Helens.</head>
        <p>Stockport v. St Helens.</p>
        <p>Th?e, tbon.andq np,c,ators witneivd a fine game at Stockport. where till " Sa;nta " ebirned the victory by two tries (Liversl“3) to one (Booth). YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Leeds v. Wakefield Trinity.</head>
        <p>Leeds v. Wakefield Trinity.</p>
        <p>Four thousard people witnessed this game at Leeds. The visitors woe. by two tries to nil. The points were reg'stered by Durham an i J,hnson.</p>
        <p>Huddersfield v. Hunslet.</p>
        <p>This maser' was fought under f tvourable conditions at Huddersfield. Graham and Williame vined tries for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>To MOTHERS.—Mrs Winslow' Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their Jhildren while teething. with perfect success. It will relieve the poorsufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain And the little cherub awakes " as bright as a button."Of all Chemists is Id per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Castleford v. Manning-ham.</head>
        <p>Castleford v. Manning-ham.</p>
        <p>A margin or three points separated the s3orm of these teams at Castlef )rd, the hme fifteen winning by two goals and two tries to two goals and a try. For the winners Speed and Dl:con got tries, Townend converting one, and Brady dropred a goal ; wile Rhodes aed Brown dropp d a couple of pals and Lockart gained a try for Manningbam. Bradford v, Halifax, The bomesters at Bradford defeated their visitors by one goat and three tries to nil. Robertson. Holdon and Cooper crossed the line, the last narned improving his try. . . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Holbeck v. Kingston Rovers.</head>
        <p>Holbeck v. Kingston Rovers.</p>
        <p>A eloge conceit resulted at flotbeek, the Roverß win- ning by two tries to a dropped goal. Guy and Watson obtained the ,ries. O'Bricm landed the goal.</p>
        <p>Liversedge v. Leeds Parish Church.</p>
        <p>On their own ground Liversedge gained a well-merited victory by a goal and a try to one try (Crampton). Stead registered the winners' try, which Todkill con- verted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Batley v. Hull.</head>
        <p>Batley v. Hull.</p>
        <p>The supporters of the honvi aid- at 13tt1ey had the satisfaction of vying their. filte.m win by three tries (Davies, Goodall and Rnhinson) to ono (Jacques),</p>
        <p>Bramley v. Brighouse Rangers.</p>
        <p>An interesting game was wttn, sied at Bramley. Lee dropped a fine goal, and the home fifteen proved victor - ions by this point to nit.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Junior League.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON Sr. MARY'S V. WIDNES WHITE STAR.— Played on the ground of the former on Saturday. The home team were poorly represented, having great diffi- culty in raising a team, whilst the visitors had their full strength. St. Mary's won the toss, and Widnes kicked off down the slope, and at once pressed. Lee cleared, and play was taken to centre. From a scrim- mage Leo go; away, but was not allowed to get far. Leadhetter got possessi ma at o?ntre, and after a good run scored for Widnes, the kick at goal failing. Shortly after Widnes again attacked, and Healey scored,the goal kick proving successful. Half-time : Widnes White Star, 8 points ; Warrington St. Mary's, nil. In the socond half the teams were mon even, play travelled rapidly from end to end, and though St. Mary's had chances of gaining points, they failed at finishing (ways. Final : Widnes White Star, one goal two tries, eight points ; Warrington St. Mary's, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WIGAN CUP.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. GOLBORNR.—This match was play ed on the ground of the former on Saturday. The band of the South Lancashires played selections, and a collection was taken in aid of the Mayor's fund for the relief of the families of the local reserviste. Both teams were fully represented, with the exception that Connor was absent from the home team. The visitors won the toss, and a forward rush was checked by Glover. Sinai% play by Golborne endangered the Warrington got!, and frtiitless corner resulted. Riley transferred play .into the visitors' qaarters, and Marsh was penalised, after whicn Riley missed a favourable opportunity by shooting wide. Warrington were next penalised twice in quick sueclssion. Aspinall saved his goal. Smart work by the home forwards saw Riley at his best, and that player opened the score after ten minutes Way. Warrington continued to attack, and Murray shot wide. De,perate efforts were made by Warrington to further augment their score, but Jenks and the hacks offered a s-übborn defence. Exchange kicking followed, and smart passing by the home for- wards gained them the advantage, and Murray shot over. Jones was apolauled for a smart sho, which went a Wile wide. Even pay took place for a time,and Glover relieved a dangerous attack oy Golborne. War- rington returned, but Houghtol saved in the nick of time. Pierpoint added a second goal with a beautiful shot. Golborne bad to act on toe defensive. Half- time—Warrin4ton two, Golt:orne nil. The second half went a4ain in favour of Warrington and Riley scrred a brilliantgoal in the first f.v, mlnates. The local side added another thraugh Pierpoint and finally won by four goals to nil,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE Senior Division.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PARK V. WARRINGTON T. MARY'S.—Played at Widnes on Sarmrd ty. Price commenced and immediately preaaed, Lambert scoring. Play now be- came very excit,nsr, and both sides strovo hard. War- rington now gave a li`tle trouble, but were met; with a stubborn defence. Park had hard lines several times. A fine csntre by Lambert was just clear,d by the War- rington backs. A splendid exhibition of football was now seen. Half-time—WAnes Park one, Warrington St. Mares PP. During the second half the home team added two goals and won by three goa!s to nil. Junior Division.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRINITY V. WARRINGTON ALBION.—At Warrington. The opening stiges seemed to gs in the, horae telm's favour, who scored in the first minute. They still held their own way, and were awarded a penalty, but the shot went outside. After fine passing by the home forwar Is, Ball sent .in a whizzing shot which the goalkeeper failed to at Ip. Ball struck the up- right afterwards. HAlf•time—Trinity two vats, War- rington Albion nil. Trinity added two soils during the second half and won by four goals to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON OLYMPIC V. ORFORD BARRACKS.—PIayed on the ground of the former. The soldiers kicked off, and the hornwters losaan to press, Sankey netting the ball, but offside nutlifiPdthe point. Tne visitors broke away, and good play by Garlick resultel in play settling in the home quarters, and for the mx:.; quarter of an hour Parry Lad a hot tim:i of it,bw he managed to keep his charge &amp;ear. El ilf.*..iine—No score. There was no score during the last half, and the game ended in a pointless draw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Newton-le-Willows v. Birkenhead Reserve.</head>
        <p>Newton-le-Willows v. Birkenhead Reserve.</p>
        <p>Played at Nursery Park on Saturday. Both elubs turnPd out indifferent teams, and matters were made wore() when the visitors arrived with only nine men, two of their players having missed their train. The visitors r.arted before a fair gate, and despite their being two men short they Lad quite as much of the play ai the Newtoners. However alter about 25 minutes play Dudgeon scored rather a soft goal for tile homesters, the ball falling at his to in an open goal, and only a rap was neerled and the ball was safely in the net. This was all the scorinz done to the interval, when the home- sters lei by one to nil.—ln the Second half the Newtoners had practically all the play, and before the fiuish they piled on three more goals: thus running out easy win- ners by four goa's t 3 nil. Tee score about represents the state of the game,although the visitors were severely handicapped by having to play two men short. The Newtoners have done very wed su far in the Combina- tion, as up to last Saturday they had played 7, won 4, lost two, drawn 1 ; goals for 12 ; against 10: points 9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Earlestown v. Wigan County,</head>
        <p>Earlestown v. Wigan County,</p>
        <p>The supportns of Wigan County fully anticipated that he Springfi-ed Park players would secure a victory at the expense of Earlestown, but although the visitors were not strongly represented, the Caunty men found themselv,.s unable to do more than ragister a draw. In the initial stages the Earlestown youths made matters hum a little, and though their potting was decidedly weak, Wnitehouse drew first blood for them. The E üb- sequent operations were of a more even character, and both sets of backs had plenty of work. Ultinaately,after experienciug hard lines, Wigan equalised, and at the in- terval both clubs had a goal to their credit. The second half was of a somewhat tame description, neither team apparently exerting itself over much, but on the whole the Meaner had the best of tha argument, the Earle-- town citadel being jeopardised more than once. Still the visitors kept their end up r xceedin,v,ly well and from a breakaway had a glorious chance of forging ahead. They however,failed to take advantage of an open goat, and the encounter ended in a draw of one goal each. The result fairly refleete3 the di3pinv of the two con- tingents, neither t( am showing itself to advan'age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Cheetham Hill.</p>
        <p>Played at Cneettiam on Saturday. The game was not one of the pleasantest,as it was plaFed in semi-darkness, and marred by constant appeals from the home team, accompanied by some very rash hitting. Soon after the commencement Latch.ord had two men temporarily injured, and so were severely handicapped. From the start-Latchford attacked. Caror failed to net the ball, tut from the 25 Cheetham made a determined rush and notched their first goal. Again Latchtord pressed, but failed to score, and alter some scrambling play at mid- field Chertham placed another to their credit. Latch- ford again rallied, and after a bit of good work by Car- ter, who passed to Grounds, the latter seared a beautiful goal. This, liy the way, is worthy of pp-cial mention, as F Grounds has not foiled to score a single matcir this reason. Ftom the bully off Chertham attacked, and Kirtland gave a corner tram which they scored a flukey goal, the ball rolling through the legs of the full back and right under the goalkeeper's foot, alai just managed to cross the line. Hall-time arrived. Cheat- ham 3, Latchford 1. At the restart it was almost too dark to play, as the ball could not be Peen any distance away. It was rather unfortunate for the visitors as they had the advantage of a god incline, but ere the game had been long in progress the ciptains agreed to abandon the idea of playing it out as it was very dan- gerous, several having narrow e.,:car.e3. Final more— Cheetham 3, Latchford 1.</p>
      </div>
