      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Leters to Me Maar.</head>
        <p>Leters to Me Maar.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letter.; intended for publication must in all cases send their names aud E.ddresses to the Editor. atzer inteutidor publication must 133 written on one side only ot the paper. Correspondents are s:rongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>IRafiling v. Gambling at Runcorn. Sir,—" Save me from my friends" may: wellp'have been Canon Wood's exAamatioa oa reading the epistle published in your last issue above the signature of "Churchman." " Cburcnman " is either a wolf in sheep's clothing or an unressoning member of the Episeopa'ean Church full of blind faith in his " adored " Vicar. If, as he insinuates, cakes were purv37ed at the recent bazaar as a mere subterfuge for selling bottled ale, no language would be too strong to condemn such an illegal and immoral practico which is indeed much worse than r,fil‘ng. In the absence of any denial the public will undoubtedly accept "Church- man's "statement to be true, and it behoves the vicar or the bazaar officials to take note of this. I have often found theory and p•actice in religion to be wide apart as the poles, but I cannot think that our temperance Vicar, and his mild mannered curates, who dare not risk th pollution of a public house, even when engaged in the sacred work of collecting subscrptions,have, even for four brief days, countenanced beer selling. Perhaps the Vicar will explain, and not allow the Church in Run- corn to remain under this stigma .—Yours, NEMESIS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Stockton Heath Parochial Committee</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Parochial Committee</p>
        <p>and its Methods.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At almost every mewing ot the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee, some one or other of its members goes very much out of his way to attack a certain mem- ber of their body.—Mr. Pemberton. I have noticed this again and again and, as a ratepayer and resident, most strongly protest against such discourteous behaviour. You wit know, sir, that in all governing boiies, an element of opposition is esseetisl to the interests of those governed. If such opposition did not exist measures would often b 3 introduced and passed without question or comment, no disoussion would ensue, and as a result, the public would remain in ignorance of many important details which they mill to know. Such a state of things would ceat airily be the came at Stockton Heath, were it not fe,r this opposition, small (in numbers) though it be. At the last meeting because he objects to a certain unnecessary detail of expense in a suggested improvement to Gaskell--street Mr. Pember- ton is, as usual, suhjected to personal attack and mis- representation. Why is it that this particular street, rXne of the newest in the villsee, should be selected for improvements at the propery owners' expense? Why not Bo•tock-street, in existroace about 25 years, or Bedford-street 12 years, or Mitehell-street 14 years ? There must be some motive. It has been admitted by liOnle of the committee that Gaskell-street,at least the top end of it, is kept in a perfectly satisfactory condition. that the street foundations, tiles and curbs are even better than those ot the County Council and Dis- trict Ceuneil. The latter ho ly on its several in- spections, has always expressed itself satisfied with the street. an has refwed to compel the property owners to incur further expense. And yet our local Council are persistently picking at this particular street and neighbsurhood. They have always been remarkably zflalous in entailing expense on the property owners of this particu'ar section of Stockton Heath. The other parts of the village are scarcely ever referred to, although they sadly require attention. It would be interesting to know the Committee's true reason for such devotion to this one corner, to the exclusion of the rest of Stockton. Heath. I trust that opposition awl consequent discus- sion will always he present, when necessary, at the meetings of our Parish Colwell and Parochial Committee; for it is only by this means rloat we, the public, may learn what work our representatives are doing and the manner in which they are doing it.—l am, sir, yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>ANTI-ANIMUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Sad Fatality to a Servant Girl at Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>The Sad Fatality to a Servant Girl at Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—Re your leaden:lt:a on the above. As a member of the jury who sat on this caso, and who agreed with the verdict re'urried viz., "Found drowned, but there 13 nothing to show how she got into the water tub." I think you ornit the latter portion of it. lam very sorry that both Coroner and Jury seemed to have failed in giving satisfaction to your repre,eotative. But we were sworn, not to try to saisfy the Frens, or public but to do our duty to :he hes,: of cur ability. Of your charity I trust you will adMit we did that. I would-not claim that as a jury v. e were perfect. There are few perfect persons, or thin gs to be met witii to-day. Bat all who sat on thii care were men of .years and experience in matters of this Nind, and were capable of grasping the brines of toe case, so far aslhy could be r.,tre-ded. We all ftlt as the Coroner truly said. "It was an extraordiniry case. and th4t thee was no evidence to poirf,... teiclae. The jury might have glvtal definite ehapa to tore verdict awl tiavo said " Accidental death." But Was the evidence sufficient for that finding ! The poor Wri was discov, red in a tub of watt dead. 17, is purssit le she was dazed or ituou'd by the tail, and was h:o4psb:o of extricating herself from the wat:-r, b.. t so far as could be teen by the jury, or as revealed by the evidence ad-luce.d, there was nothing to indicate death in any other way, but by drowning. And I aubasit sir, with all defevnce to your wider, and superior kn;;vc.,l?dv, that the jury in th's Mae were actuated by the putest motives, and did their duty to the beat of their ahility han atNr toe eviletce,aud the Coroner's summing up they found the above verdict.—l am, yours obediently, STEPHEN H. JENKINS.</p>
        <p>-- Mr. Jenkins sadly misunderstands us if he thinks we question either the purity`ot their motives or the ability of the jurymen. Our con,ention last week was that there was an insuffi-ieney of evidence—particularly medical and police evidence, none of which was called at all—and we adhere to our opinion that there was no testimony bearing dire !My on the cause of death. Mr. Jenkins himself says" but so far as could be seen by the jury. or as reve.led by the evidence adduced, there was nothing to indicate death in any other way but by drowning." That is the point exactly. The jury had to presume that the unfortunate girl was drowned, they hal no evidence to prove it, and in basing their verdict on such a pre&amp; •hiption they, in our opinion,made misttke. It may be in`ormation to Mr. Jenkins, and to the ether gentlemen of the jury, but we are given to understand that Miss Jeffs' neck was broken by the fall, which was the direct cause o: death, and she was not, therefore, " found drowned." Why was there no evi- dence on this point, by either medical man or police ? We repeat our assertion thtt the enquiry was cf a per- functory character, but for this we lay rather more blame on the Coroner than the jury.—En. 41, FIRST SYMPTOMS.</p>
