      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Rarlestown and District P.S.A. was held on Sunday afternoon. Mr. E. Kenyon presided. The principal speaker was Mr. k, 11. Jackson, an I recitations were given by Mr. J. Astall. Vocal items were submitted by Miss Lizzie Wright. TEMPERANCE SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>At the Wesleyan Place of Wore iip, Sunday was devoted to the question of temperance. The pulpit was occupied during the day by the Rev G Lunn, whose discourses on temperance were moat inter- esting. In the afternoon the ray gentleman ad- dressed the scholars in the upper room of the school, and Mr. G. Warburton addressed the junior scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>roLICR CASES</head>
        <p>roLICR CASES</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newtin, on Tuesday morning before Colonel Woad, William Abrams, of no fixed abode,was charged with being drunk and disorderly? in Junction-lane on Monday, and in default of pay- ing a fine of 2s 61 and costs, was sent to gwl for 7 days.—Elizabeth Harlow, of no addres;, was also sent to gaol for seven days on a charge of soliciting in Earlestown on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLLEY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLLEY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge held their meeting in the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave-road,on Monday evening, Bro Cutler, C.T., presiding. After the opening services four candidates were initiated. A member being reported sick the sick committee were asked to visit him. The tea party committee reported good meetings had been held and a bal- ance was handed over to the treasurer. Bro New- man gave the report of the District Lodge held at St. Helens which showed an increase in both the juvenile and adult sections of the order. The pro- gramme for the evening was then proceeded with Song, "She's my Kithleen," Bro Macklin; rpci- tation, A lady sold by auction," Sistei Ord; song, "Robin Adair," Sister Williams ; recitation, Bro Warburton ; recitation, " For Eva's Sake" Sister Warburton. Lodge clefsed by Bro Cutler, C.T., at 930.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>WARGRAVE CHURCH, PAROCHIAL TEA. AND CONCERT</head>
        <p>WARGRAVE CHURCH, PAROCHIAL TEA. AND CONCERT</p>
        <p>A congregational tea party and concert was held in the Wargrave Schools on Tuesday evening. In the afternoon there was a sale of work, consisting of articles of clothing &amp;.7„, left over from the late bazaar, and upwards of £5 was realised. At the tea which was held subsequently there was a large attendance. and the edibles provided were fully appreciated. The concert programme was sus- tained by members of the church choir, and the various items submitted wete much enjoyed. There was a capital glee party, and they rendered the glees" Now by day's retiring lamp," "Rainy day, 'and "Silent night." For the song "The brave patrol," Mr S thing received a hearty encore, and Master HS weetlove for "The Soldiers of the Queen," was enthusiastically recalled, the choir taking up the inspiriting chorus. Miss A. Molyneux gave a good rendering of" Saving the Colours. and was recalled. The other items were song " linsheen," Kiss 11olyneux ; quartette "When evening's twilight." Misses Shepnerd and Molyneux, Messrs Bullock and Shaw; song," Sons of our Empire," Mr R Carson; quartette' "Sleep, gentle lady," Misses Holt and Molyneux. Messrs 19 Carson and S Dring; song," Sunshine and rain," Miss Shepherd; duet " Excelsior." Messrs Leyland and Dring ; trio." Evening rest" (female voices) , song" The cup of life." Mr W Carson,; Coon song, and chorns, "Drum Major." Mr R Carson and male voices. rha accompanists were Mr E Leyland and Mr. S Dring, and Mr J 11 Peplow gave a couple of violin 80103.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>EgfRAORDINARY THEFTS,</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Pol.c.i Court, on Tue3day, before Colonel W. J. Tnomson and Mr. B. B. Glover, Kate Molloy, a married womao, of Helena-road, Sutton, who on Saturday wis fined 403. for stsaling provisions, was now charged with stealing a basket, value 13. 81., from the snop of Henry J. Craven, of Liverpaol-road ; some knives, of the value of is .from the stall of James Hart, on the market ground; aad a tea bottle from the shcp of William J. Mason, of 40, Livnrpoo'.-road. She was fined 103 6d or 14 days in each case, the seatences to fol- low each other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LECTURES ON EGG FARMING.</head>
        <p>LECTURES ON EGG FARMING.</p>
        <p>The third lecture of ths series of discourses ow Egg Farming by Mr. E. Brovva, Lit Reading, promoted by the Viaduct lizzitive Technical 104:uction Committae, was aven in the Ton Flail, Newron, on Tuesday egPning, in() inibject being " Feeding f it- egg production. Mr. F. J. Wood presided, and there was a very fair audience.</p>
        <p>Tee Chairman esid he Jo: ht d forward to that lecture ae one of the most intereslog Hr. Brown had given, for without proper feeding they could out get the desired result from their birds. The Lecturer said they must bear in mind that if they were to succeed in connection with egg production or any other branch of poultry culture, it was desirable that they should understand, as far as possible the basis upon which they worked, because if they appreciated the environment they would be better able to realise their object. Eggs were not intended as the food of man, but as tbe reproduction of the race therefore if they desired to increase the number of eggs it was essential that they shoeld supply in much greater pro- portion the materials which were required for the for- mation of the egg. There was another peint which they must understand, that if they called upon the various organs of the bedy to perform something like ten times the amount of work that would be required and that under the natural conditions it was meet iroportant that they should give those organs every opportunity of performing their work. Thus they would see they had to sueply the materials for making the eug, and ensure that the organs con- cerned in the operation should be able to work freely and be in correct condition. One of the secrets was the keeping of the birds in hard cOndition,uamely that they ensure there was only as much fat on the body as would require to keep the various puts of the body in action, Same of the peasants in Russia declare that they could make their hens lay exactly when they liked. When he was told this, his first impression was to laugh,but when they understood what was meant by the exoression, the declaration of the Russians was that the pullets or bens were selected before hand, and fr six v eeks were not given a particle of food artificially they must find the whole of their food, they most seek for the food and work for it, and at the end of ti,x weeks were brought down to a thoroughly lean CDtlditinli. All the particles of fat were used up, and they were in a thoroughly bard conditien and the people declared they could make them lay just whoa they wanted them. The lean- ness and bard condition of hens, had as much to do with the laying of eggs as the food. It was a common thing to say that the eggs were composed of three parts the shell whites,or albumen aud the yolkes,end he proceeded to give a description of the formation of each part: When a hen was in fell lay, there was always a greeter tendency in fur body to the increase of fat, unless they were specially careful. On the question of feeding, he said if they gave much bran to any animal it had a positively injurious effect upon them, the organs of the animal had to perform a very much greater amount of work in order to derive any benefit from it. Then there was wheat, barley and oats. Oats were undoubtedly the bast balanced of all foods. The excess of fats was to some extent compen- sated by the large amount of woody fibre and ash, and it was about the most perfect food we had for poultry. Wheat was found to be a useful fooi, especially dering the summer. For egg production the best foods were oats, weeat, fine thires, these stand in the first rank. In the second rank we have barley. 130 was a great believer in giving soft foods in the morning, and hard foods at night. To give soft food mixed with cold water, was more injurioes than by giving hard corn. The morning meal should consist of one part of Indian meal, one part pea meal, one part bran and two parts of potatoes or turnips, and a further part of meat scraps or meat meal. In the evening they should give as hard corn, equal parts of wheat end oats. Fowls ought never to be fed more than twice a day. If they want more let them seek for it. The food must be sufficient but at the same time it must not be abundant. They must keep the hirds active, and the more they could make them •wsek for their food the healthier and more productive they would be. (Applause.) Votes of thanks to the Leeturer and the Chairman, brought the proceedings to a Cl( Se.</p>
        <p>"GENERAL MANAGENIENr AND MARKETING."</p>
        <p>The last lecture of the aerie 4 was given on Wednesday evening in the Town Hall, Eirleszown. Dr. Patterson presided. The subject rli4coursed upon was 11,)w to improve the poultry, the variation in layinc,,, records in eggs, Inv: to avoid diseaso, how to preserve and pack eggs, and the marketing of them. Sev.-ral illustrations were given by means of lime light views.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NtARRiiGTON DRAUGHTS _ LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>NtARRiiGTON DRAUGHTS _ LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET v. ST. BARNABAS'.—PIayed at Cairo-street on Monday last. Score : Wins CAIRO STREET Draws ST. BARNABA.S'. Wins 1 Jli Grieve 1 B J Powell 0 2 8 Tavlin 0 0 Powell o 1) Plinston * : vl, Cummins 0 1 P Wright 0 W Hewitt 1 J Conolly 1 J Green 1 J Lee 0 2 E Eden</p>
        <p>7 5 1 *Only one game played.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EX AM IN ER, FRI DA Y, DECEMBER 1, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EX AM IN ER, FRI DA Y, DECEMBER 1, 1899.</p>
        <p>MIT J. WILSON M.P. AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Lecture on Labour.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening Mr. J. Wilson, M.P. for Mid- Durham delivered a !nature in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom. Ee,t,nom nt "Llbour ; its situation, aspirations p )wer and duties," Mr. J. Worthington, of Wigan Preiidel, but there was only a moderate audi• once.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had a great deal of sympethy with the subject that the lecturer had come to lecture upon, and he had it further sympathy with the lecturer . bad worke I his way into the place whee the laws of this CHILItrV were made. Ttlat was a credit to Mr. Wilson, and to M d-Durhacn that they should have selected him for Parliament. (Hear, hear.) The subject was serious enough in all couscience, aud important; enough as well, and about as lit-le understood as any subject they could talk about. Talk about labour, why it Was the vary thing people were trying to get out of. (Laughter.) Labour want d talking about, it needed talking about, it was a divine iaetituarre. It was mach better to work out than rust out. The man who had nething to d), repreiented the caterpillar, he was always eating soinsbody elses cabbages—(laughter)— and those;men wiles; would not work ,but were able to work were the caterpillars of society. Labour was divine, and what they wanted was to make it thoroughly divine. There was nothing degrading about labour except unnecessary labour, mischievious labour. For instance wnea a E 12413 Was running about behind a bar pulling at a fourpenny machine, he called that mischievous labour, and the sooe, r that maa got tired the better. He should like labour to be ennobled; all honest labour was noble. Mr Wilson had been amongst labour all his life, his sympathies were with them, and theirs with him. (Applause.) Mr. Wilson said the subject which he had to bring larfore them was one that was wry wide and one that very few people could say anything new about. He wanted to define exactly what he meant by labour. It was necessary bscause th .re were so many ideas as to what labour was. He had nothiug abstruse or technical about it. He had heard a man come forward and say to a body at working men," we are all workers to- , gether," when the man who was speaking, had not ia his life, in any shape or form doce a day's work, in anything that would conduce to the welfare of this country. But that did not apply altogether to the higher class. He saw a statement by the Bishop of Durham about the luxury of idleness, but that did not apply to one class more than another. They made a. mistake when they said that the man who worked with his hands was the only labourer in the country, and all wealth should come to the men who worked manually. A very large amount of our wealth was not the result of manual labour, but owing to the cunning genius of men in this country. He never took up the position te say that manual labour was the only source. and that they were the only labourers.A man who workA with his brain arid by the force of his inventive geniusef he gave that freely was working just 38 much as the man who worked with his hinds, and, it was by the two forces that this ceuntry had reached the point it had row reached. (Applause.) He knew there ewsre two classes of thought in the labour world of this coun- try. It was wrong to be dogmatic in any idea at all. Ha gave every man credit for making legitimate use of his oppertunities. Tnere was another idea, that which treated labour as a commodity. That had been one of the great means of working to their detriment in their commercial life in this cotentry, treating labour as a commod,ty. Labour was not a commodity. In his cpinion labourers must be treated as men and women the sons and daughters of God. (Hear, hear.) There were some who disputed that any great change had come in the situation of labour. Some said thae a change had come,and that greater changes would come. He had worked down the mines of this country 13 and 14 hours a day as a lad, week in and week out. He recognised that the hours of lads were 4 or 5 hours a day shorter than that. He had got coals at 11 hours a day, and he was now in a position to say that in the county from which he c ime there was not a miner down the mines longer than 7 hours a day, and the day of the coal getter to-day dig not average more than fit heurs. and that had been brought about by negotiation and compromise and arrangement between employers and employed. It renresented a great change, atm taught him thae the backward look they could take was a source of great inspiration. Be spoke at some length ripen the opportunities in the hands of working men for working out their own good, punting to the establishnsent of Parish. District and County Councils, and said that in the North they held in their hands as workmen, 70 per cent, of the places on the Parish and District, Councils. and on the County Council they had 22 bona-fide workmen, and six of them were aldermen of the Council and they were using this power rightly, usefully and for the welfare of the whole country. (Ap- plause.) There was never a time when more regard was paid for the people, and he thought it was because the peeple had more power. He touched upon the Work- men's Compensation Bill, and upon the question of old age pensions. A vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Wilson at the close on the motion of Mr. Traverse seconded by Mr. Dale, and a similar compliment was paid the Chairman</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>DR. MARY WILLIAMS AT THE TOWN HALL.</head>
        <p>DR. MARY WILLIAMS AT THE TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>Meeting of the British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>Dr. Mary Hall Williitns dolly n.efi an instructive ad- dress on the effect of Rielly)l on the human system at a meeting held on Tuesday afternoon in the large com- mittee room at the Town Hall (by permission of the Mayor and Mayoress). The meting was held under the auspices of the Warrington Branch of the British Women's Temperance Association. There was an en- couraging attendance. The MAYORESS presided and stated that the meeting had been delayed in consequence of th, late arrival of Dr. Williams. She woutd ask Mrs. Yonge to offer prayer._</p>
        <p>M-rs. YONCiE acceded to the request. Dr. WlLLtams then entered the room and commencel her lecture. She said she wished them to consider the position which alcohol took in the human eceaomy. In the first place they must consider that part of the systein which would be touched by alcohol,where it would find home.its centre,for sending out various manifestations. It would be found in that part of taem which they called their life, their nerve force, the brain. the reservoir— starting from the brain as from a spring, and spreadin: through the body very much as did the branches of a tree. Was the imp -elision made by alcohol for good or for evil ? She contended that it was for evil, and that all alcohol, wherever it was found, whether in brandy, whisky, beer or malts, or even in their carelessly baked fruit pie, touched the brain force and the rest of the economy of the system. There were fume Dente in the habit of taking what were called" night-cane," contending that they would have no desire, f drink on the following morning, and that in their ease there would be ne temptation. But a woman in these circumstances would deceive herself and others. Lying went along with thinking—that remark had been corroborated by men who were sot teetotalera —because women lost their power of appreciating the correctness of what they said. Dr. Williams then showed why the face of a drinker h canal redder and redder, and contended that drink had a tendency to harden the tie3ues, as it certainly had a great affinity for those things they wanted in their bodies. rime were two things they wanted specially—albumen and water.—and wherever alcohol came into contact with albumen and water, it seized them and held them as long as it could.' Alcohol had also the effect of hardesiag the liverywhich then lost all its working power and was never cured. It wae so much dead tissue. The people who had such a liver Were those very respectable poople who took a little alcahol every day. Their children inherited it and had a little stronger desire, almost a craving for alcohol. Alcohol set up an inflammatory condition in the body. Toe tissues were congested and then followed inflamma- tion. In the organs of the body there were tissues known as muscular fibres, and alcohol produced through them fatty degeneration. If they had a weakness of the heart alcohol would make it worse. Alcohol made the muscles and organs weak. It was quite c'ear that alcohol would not aid the digestion and would not make them strong.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer's services were acknowledged on the prooosition of Miss Dutton, seconded by Mrs. Yonge. The Mayoress generously provided afternoon tea for those present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
