      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Tea Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual tea meeting and couce—rt in connection with thia toziety, WJS held in toe Viaduct Institute, Earlestown, on Saturday evening. The tea was served in the dining-room of the In,tittice,and abew 600 peop'e were accommodated at the two sittings, their wants being attended to by Mesdames Hammett, Shallcross, .1 R Barr, Wright, J Barr, Halton, Roberts, Twemlow, A Evett, Anderton, Lord, Jones, Dudgeon, W Clare, Reward, Holt, Pearson, His e-s F N Barr, W Thad, A Pickup, and Partington. After tea an adjournment was made to the upper room wnera the concert was held. Mr D Forrester presided and he was supported by Mews J G Mul.ias, P Parr, Thomas Swift, E Jones. W Clare, G G if, J Goeding, J 11 Birks (manager), and A. Onions (secretary). Am ingst the delegates present from other Societies were, Mes-rs R 821 l ors, W Bushby (Toxteth. Liverpool). E Stubbs (Sr. Helens), J Rrthwell, and C W Eieley (Bamtarlong), G Winstanley (Sr. Helens), N Unswortn (Evldesley), C J Boardman, and ,W H Hutton (L igh), J Battersby (Tyldesley), T Manual, and C E R.ibinson (vVigan),J Wolstancrott (Farnwortb), Deveaey (Farnwortn) Brownlow, Hodge, and 8 Barrett (Bolton). ,The programme, which was of a lengthy character, was sunain,.d'abyl)lr. Charles Parker's" Aeolian Opera C incsrt Party," in gems from comic operas, etc., with Mr. Tourlow H. Johnson. as humorist. It would be invidious to refer in detail to the various itnns sub- mitted by the opera p trty, it being quite sufficient to say that all their meteciens were given in a manner which sho wed the high training and excellent perfection which they have attained as a concert party. Mr. Johnsoo showed himself to be a clever humorist, and he was quickly on good terms with his audience, whom ' be kept c)ovuesA with laughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>During an intervel the Secretary (Mr. Onions) re- ported as follows :—" In again submitting a brief account of the past year's work for your consideration, we are glad to be ab'e to congratulate you on the con- tinued success .'Of your Society. It shows that ,it is still incrieasing and ,teveloping in a meat marked degree, the sal3s;fer the year being £33,338 93 Bd, as against £24,490 16 ,21 for tee nrevious year, seowine an in- crease of £8,847 133 61. The number of new members ad- mitted dutiag the year is 274, withdrawn 47,which brieg the wet number of members to 1,494, or an increases of 227 derine the year. Tne amount nf share capital is £15,357 2s 84 I, against £l2 949 14s 41, an increase on the previou 4 year of £2.407 8351. The RPP.3ree Fund new stands at £5OB 153 21, and we have £2,760 93 ld ingested in rhe Co-operative movement. Toe amount credited to members in dividend end interest is £5,463 Bs, ale the averege divideed is 3s in the 2. we have written off £387 19s 81 for de- pr. ciation of buildtige, and neve now 6 shops besides a bakery and confectionery department, anct find emploi m Int for 34 persona, aril also own 5 horses. We hive purchased from the Ca-operative Wtaoles ale Society, and other Co-operative s nines. goods to the value of £16,307 15a., and have received in bonus £239. Oar brume shops are each doing an encouraging amount 'of trade, and are equipped to do double the amount if required. The butchering department his had very fair support from our menaners, and we would urge Upon every member to give it their trade. Mark progrese has been made in our bakery ani confectionery doper,- meats, the number of sacks of flour used being 2,640 durine the year, an increase of 647 over the previous year, and we may here also say that the quality of the confectionery and fancy bread praduced is appreciated by our members, the quantity sold each week being very large. Cleanliness and sanitation are also not over- loaked, our employee in this department also having instructions to use only goo 1 and reliable materials for their productions, and our membirs may rely that we are not like the cook, who, on her mistress saying " Mary, yen dec't mean to tell me you are making bread without first having washed your hands," repliea 1* Oh, mum, it doa't matter a bit; it's only brown bread I'm making." There are, at present 339 depositors in the small savings bank, and the savings amount to £306 18s 90. As ratepayers wo have contribu- ted £7O 193 9.1 to gas and water depart; , merits. and £55 17s to general and other rates and taxes. For Some time past your Commit:eel have been trying to provide more space for trade purposes at our central, especially for furniture and cfothing. and after due consideration, we decided to take the top, or small hall, for that purpose, and have now so altered the premises that easy access to same can be had from our drapery departmeet, and we hope that the members will justify by their increased support the expense incurred in alterations to this department. The children have not been overlooked, the committee having made arrangements to hold one or two entertainments during the winter to be devoted to them. From the above report you will gather that marked procrese has been made in all our departments, and we trust that your confidence and trade will be continued, and tiaat every member will try to obtain at least another new member dining the coming year, for, seeing that food and c:othing are essentials of existence, why shroula not the censsmers combine to secure toe supply to themselves of wholesome food and substantial cinthine ; and why s'eould y u contribute, in the form of profits, to bad up fortuees for individuals, whea, by ishaieg our Society, each consumer hag his portion of the profit, returned to him, which, if allowed to accumulate at interest rapidly forms a reliable fund, ready for any time when it may be needed." Latsr on in the evening the Chairmen made a few re- marks in which he stated thet one of the chief items in the Secretary's report was "Share Capital £15,000." He said that had there been no Co-operative Sseiety at Eerlestown, this £15.000 would not have been saved ; the Co-operative Society's system enabled ite members to save :noney in an unconscious mannrr. He called attention to their divided scheme and that some people tried to run this scheme down. He further remarked that the Society,took members from 16 years of age and consequently many of the persons who shared in the 215.000 before mentioned had not yes entered upon the field of matrimony and when they did come to this very important part of their life, there would very likely be a nice little sum invested in the Society. He said the, Co-operative Society's business in Earlestown was a re- tail business and he had no doubt but that there were mane gentlemen of good standing in the same business, in Earlestown at present. Votes of thanks to the Chairman and all who had helped in the success of the gatheriog,brought the meet- ing to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CHRISTMAS ALE AT WARRINGTON WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS ALE AT WARRING- TON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon, at Ebenezer Church and Suaday se tool, it being Tomperauce Sunday. Mr. a Thorntoa, the speaker, referred at length to the folly and wrong ef the Warrington B is.rd of Guardians having again decided to allow intoxicants to the work house inmates foe Christmas dinn,r, and prop Ned the following resolution asking them to pass it. and to send copy to all the papers and one to the Board of Guardians. Mr. Rees and Mr. N. CoAr warmly supported, and the resolution was passed unanimously " We regret to learn that at a meeting held November Bth, 1899, at our Warrington Workhouse, the Poor Law Guard: flas passed a resoluti m by en votes against nine to allow the inmates of the sakd Workteaus-t intoxicating drink to their Christmas dinner, as per previous years and custom. This was done in tias fscs of statements made and facts given by officials, guardians. ;tad the inmates themselves, of the dig urbances and consequent anaoyanca and dislatisfacti-n arising from the drink given last Christmas. Ka:awing as we do the terrible havoc made upos our folios+ creatures, in cinsequesoe of the drinking customs of the day by the use of intoxi- cants, which as we all know is a great source of crime, disease and death, blighting as it d aes the home,sapping health and conrititution,blasting individual and national prospects, and is a general, sure, and prolific creator of poverty, filling our workhouses with paupers, our prisons with criminals, and all our asylums as at this day to over-orowding with lunatics ; and also believing as we do ghat to the crannaon nterests of humanity and the protection of the weak, that it is a part of the mission, as well as the bonnticen duty of the Church of God, assisted by all Sabbath schools, Bands of Hope, P.S.A 's and al and every branch of tlao Temperance organisatious to do alf in their pawer to stop this terrible and blighting curs-, this meeting therefore, compose I of teacher., Bohol&amp; s, band of hope workers. many of whom are ratepayers, respectfully urge the Guardians of our poor ia their ien- pertain': and responsible positioa, to rescind this objet:' tionable resolution, refusing to al!ow the said intaxi- cant's. and give to the aged poor instead thereof some suitable comfort or luxury which will neither eadaager teir health nor future, neither will it do violence of con;eience to the large proportion of ratepayers favour- able to thia bin! dole. Signed on behalf of the school: WILLIAM EDWARD REES (superintendent), J. H. THORN- TON (secre?ary) " The same proposition was put again tic Mr. Thornton to the congregation at the evening service, secotOoi lov Mr. Jahn Drury (missionary), and Pivied on behalf of the Church by John Roscoe (presi- dent), Thomas Riley (secretary).</p>
      </div>
