      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS</p>
        <p>News From Ladysmith.</p>
        <p>British Losses During the Siege.</p>
        <p>The following has been posted at the War Offic 3 From the General Officer commanding-in-chief, South</p>
        <p>Africa, to the Snretary of State for War.</p>
        <p>(Received 30th November.)</p>
        <p>P lETERMARITZBURG, 29TH NOVEMBBR, 1899, 4-35 p.m. Mr. Mitchell, telegraphiit, has just made his way out of LadTsmith to Weenen, from whence he sends the following message:—On 9th No- vember we beat back the Boers • with great loss to the enemy. Our total casualties are remarkably small. There were only eight men killei during the siege by shells, and in all the battles, St.)., only 100 men killed and wounded. I left Ladysmith on the night of the 25th. Since the 9th no attempt has been made to attack Ladysmith in force.</p>
        <p>Successful Attack by the 12th Lancers.</p>
        <p>ESTCOURT. MONDAY.</p>
        <p>News hag been rec+red Lem that the 15:,11 Lancers attacked Piet Reid's commando, which was despatched by General Joubert to Weenen,and inflicted great loss on the enemy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Situation in Natal. Approaching Great Battle.</head>
        <p>The Situation in Natal. Approaching Great Battle.</p>
        <p>Such scraps of news as are allowed to leak through from Distal, paint to the im:ninenc) of a great and desperate battle there. Joubert will fight to oppose the British advance to tbe relief of Ladysmith. He will take his stand north of the Tugela River, probably in the neighblurisnd of Colons). The British commander will have to fight fo- thi passage of the river, and a desperate battle, s)mewhat on the lines of that fought on the Milder River ty Lord Methuen's column on Tuesday will ensue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Modder River Battle. Sensational Statement. "HALF THE GUARDS" KILLED AND WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>The Modder River Battle. Sensational Statement. "HALF THE GUARDS" KILLED AND WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>The " Chronicle" says private telegram received in London on Wednesday evening says the battle was " the bloodies'. &amp;Olt of the century," and roughly estimatea the loss in killed and wounded at half the Guards, which we are very loth to believe. There is no account yet to hand of the casualties.and we mwst hope for the best, though,evea putting aside the private telegrams of which we hear, Lord Methuen's words are decidedly ominous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Death of Lieutenant Blundell.</head>
        <p>The Death of Lieutenant Blundell.</p>
        <p>Many of our read:l-s will regret to hear of the death of Lieutenant Blundell (who fell a victim to Boer treachery by the use of the white flag), whilst tending a wounded Boer at the Battle of Belmont. Lieutenant Blundell was a visitor at the last Infirmary ball, and, together, with Lieutenant Cameron, who was woundod in Thursday's cattle, visited at several local houses.</p>
        <p>Letter From a Warrington Lancer.</p>
        <p>The following letter has been reeeived by Mr.D. Dolan, 24, E enotreet, Warrington, from his brother, Trooper Tnomas D.,lan, of the sth Royal Irish Lancers : "Lads smith, Advance Base, October 29, 1899.—Just a few lines to let you know I am all riht and in good health. I suppoie by thia time you will have heard of the War that has broken out here. 1 have been through two engagement a up to the time of writing, and I hop with Ged's hFlp, to go through the lot as we have won the two. I have been out every night in wet weather, and I have been sleeping with all my clothes on for a fortnight. We have got five of their guns in our camp at this moment, and the best of it is that we are going to use them against the Boers. At last Tuesday's battle we had five shells fired into us, and not one burst. We were unJer fire all day, and all we lost was one horse killed of this squadron, and a few men wounded in 0 sqoadroo. The way we lost them was that at sunset we had to retire for the night, and had to gallop for our lives a geod mile in the line of fire, that is to say, we could get no cover as it was all open veldt. It was all right, I can tell you. You could hear the bullets as you went along, and it was something, as near as I c,in tell you. like a circular saw going through a thin piece of boarding and the whistle of a steam engine, but we got out all round. I don't know at hat moment' boots and saddle 'will sound, so don't crib about the days you get these few words. All is welt hero at Ladysmith, and the troops are in good spirits."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CHRISTMAS CARDS. _ Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons' Art Novelties.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS CARDS. _ Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons' Art Novelties.</p>
        <p>We ge;-, a pleasant reminder every year that "Christmas is coming," by the arrival of a specimen parcel of the new season's Christmas and New Year cards, calendars, booklets, and art novelties from Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons, the world-famed art publishers. There is a sensation of pleasure derived from seeing something worth seeing before anybody else and that sensation is heightened when Ithe thing (or things), seen are of so beautiful a character as the Christmas goods from such a high- class firm as that of Tuck and sons. The latter are now installed in their new and spacious premises, Raphael House, and have moreover prepared a magnificent collection of novelties for the 1899th anniversary of Christmas, or, as they style it, "the meeting of the centuries" Messrs Tuck are noted for following enter- prise by enterprise and they have only just finished taking our breath away by the beauty of one season's goois, before they spring on us a col- lection of new creations even more entrancing to the artistic eye. This year they have quite sur- passed even themselves,and their quest into novelty departure has been productive of 1200 entirely new sets of cards, independent of the large number of other goods, such as calendars, booklets, and the like. Now we have no intention of going into the merits of 1200 types of Christmas Cards—the in- , ventces have not yet succeeded in rendering braid- fag an imposgbility —but we do not object, in justice to Messrs. Tuck and, the general public, to give some idea of the nature of a few of them. Of these the most important without question are the "Early English," the "Modern Art," and the "New Century" series, in which simplicity of design and effective appropriateness of lettering form the leading special features. Nekt in import- ance come the " Wedgwood" series, and here Messrs. Tuck's exclusive arrangements with Messrs Josiah Wedgwood and Sons, enable them to repro- duce the unique designs of eminent artists of this and other decades which belong entirely and solely to his firm. 'there is something peculiarly chaste and attractive to the eye, in the cards of this class. the royal blue background and the severe white design or lettering, form what we may term an harmonious contrast, and one, too, which will be difficult to beat. They will have a large sale, we are certain, for there is undoubtedly going to be a " fashion " in this blue and white " Wedg- wood ware." Add to these, numberless mis- cellaneous cards and a remarkable collection of upwards of 200 sets of black and white cards, beaded by entirely new representations of platino panels, and a collection of cards is arrived at which may fairly claim to be unprecedented. A strong ins in calendars has been prepared and with some 200 varieties to choose from no one should have Any difficulty in securing a few perfectly ideal pro- ductions of this useful and ornamental type. loy books and juvenile gift books there are galore, and the new and attractive publications will be bailed by the rising generation as the most desirable of gilts, and this no less from an entertaining than an educational point of view. Altogether Messrs. Tuck eave made a remarkable advance on previous years, and only those who know what the previous years' 'pork has been like can realise the wide range and fmricalled choice which the productions for 1899- 1900 comprise. They may get a very good idea, however, by writing to the firm for a copy of their 111ustrated catalogue, which contains 240 pages of eet-criptive matter.</p>
        <p>'ALL COUNTRY PEOPLE should rea.-1 The Rural World lti. weekly, which contains articles on Horses, Cattle. the Dairy, Poultry, Bees, Rabbits, Pigs. Gardening, Natural history. Botany of the Farm and Garden, the Home, Foreign and Colonial Intelligtnce. Anwers to Correspondents, etc. It is profusely illustrated and is altogether, a most inter est- a'ng and instructive farming and country paper. A good medium for servants requiring situations. The Pdinager offers to send a specimen tree. The address is 110 and 111 BTAIND, London, W.—Advt.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Perional.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. E. Morrison, of Liverpool, has just com- pleted a three-quarter length painting in oils of Sir l'eter Carla,w Walker, Bart., which has been sub- scribed for and presented to aim by the employ6s of Peter Walker and Son, Warrington and Burton, ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATIMS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Spiritualism.</head>
        <p>Spiritualism.</p>
        <p>Mr. (3. Llewellyn, a local medium was the speaker at the meetings of the Spiritualist Society on Sun- day and Monday. His addresses on Sunday were simple and practical, being based upon " Woman, why weepest thou ?" and "Profession." At. each meeting he gave a number of clairvoyant delinea- tions.</p>
        <p>Warrington and the Cheshire Agricultural Show.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the members of the Cheshire Agricultural Society, held on Wednesday at the Royal Hotel. Crewe, Colonel France-Hay- hurst presiding, a deputation was appointed to wait upon the Mayor of Warrington to ascertain what reception would be given to the society in cede they held their ext year's show at Warrington.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCK FoR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRII.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. George Allen.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Saturday afternoon of Mr. George Allen. at his residence in Egerton-street. The deceased carried on business of smith, &amp;c., in Brook-street, but for the last few months had suf- fered from a painful malady, prohibiting him tak- ing an active part in the business. Despite the constant attention of his medical adviser, Dr Joseph Mr Allen experienced a serious relapse on Friday afternoon, from which he never rallied, and nradu- ally sinking died a little after five o'clock on Satur- day afternoon. lie was prominently identified at one time with the Ancient Order of Foresters (Court Mercy, 240). Presentation to an Oddfellow</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the Benevolent Star Lodge No 449, of the Grand United Order ot Oddfellows was held at. the " White Bull" Inn, Horsemarket-street, on Saturday evening, and was well attended. After the routine business had been transacted, Bro. W. Smith was presented with an emblem of the Order for services rendered to the lodge. Bro. Appleton made the presentation in a few well chosen remarks and hoped Bro. Smith would treasure the emblem not for its value alone but also for the honour that the presentation car- ied with it. Bro. Smith suitably replied.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK. FIRIDGE-STREBT.—A.dvt. • Ellesmere-street Wesleyan School.</p>
        <p>The officers of the Ellesmere-street Wesleyan School transformed the afternoon session into a temperance meeting, over which Mr. Gregson pre- sided. An interesting address was given by Mr. Horrocks (Irlam-street), on "The beginning of things." During the meeting Miss Gregson contri- buted a recitation, entitled 'The angel in the dram shop," and Miss Gertie Jones gave, with her usual pathos, "The serpent in the wine." Suitable hymns were sung by the children, and at the close about 20 signed the pledge. Special sermons were also preached by the Revs. Theodore Bishop and John Gunnell in the Bold-street and Bewsey-road Chapels.</p>
        <p>FOR 'LIVENING WEAR: W. T. WILLIAMS hIS the latest in Fans; Gloves • Hose &amp;c. Choice assortments of Jetted Nets; Silks and Pansy Materials; Dressmaking. 74, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington. Latchford Improvement Society.</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of this society, held in the Temperance Hall, Mr. E Ford in the chair, a miscellaneous programme was arranged for im- promptu speeches. Among theialletted questions dealt, with some were of local interest, and others imperial. Mr. Harry Blease asked if the Victoria Park would be open soon, and was able to say that there was a good reason for believing the park would be ready in the near future, not only from the speech of the Mayor on November 9th, but from the fact that a committee had been appointed and ,Councillor Ellison had been made convenor. Mr. -Albinson gave his opinion on" Gladstone," Mr. Drewitt had to give his views upon " swimming, bicycling, and football.," which were of an experi- mental kinfl. Other speakers discussed " Women's Rights," ;."Socialism," "Strikes," "'Paul Krugsr." " Christianity," " What Warrington needs before the Millennium 1" &amp;c.</p>
        <p>TIIJ GLEV-9PEY AND STRATHMILL DISTILERRIES Situated in the finest Whisky-producing district of Scotland re the property of W. and A. Gilbey, and the ,Nhiskv made from Rom -grown Barley only. Glen-Spey," 3s. 6d nd —ttrathrnill. ' 3s. 6d.. sold by W. and A. GILBEY gents in every town.— Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Brick-street Mission Band of Hope.</head>
        <p>Brick-street Mission Band of Hope.</p>
        <p>The third entertainment of this society took place on Tuesday evening in the Mission Hall. There was a good attendance and the various items of the programme were satisfactorily carried out. Mr. A Mounfield presided, and with his usual hdaptability for addressing young people gave a practical address to the boys and girls, which was listened to with much interest. Recitations were given by Misses Sarah J Iligham, May Warburton, Amy Howard, Emily Holmes, Ellen Clarke, and Masters Arthur Yates, Arthur Jolley, and Fred Brown. Miss Bessie Shaw creditably sang two solos entitled "Onward Friends of Temperance," and "Gather the Children." and the juvenile choir rendered choruses, viz "Sweetly singing," " Merry Laughing Water" and "The Children are gathering. The characters acquitted themselves very well in the three dialogues "A pleasant evening," "Making it easy to say no "and "sarah Sprigg's Choice," which were much appreciated, as they were mirthful and nstructive. The singing of the Doxology cencluded helpful entertainment ASEi or OUNNINGHAMIHIGII-CLA.S3 'MINERAL WATINITS —Ada Independent Order of Good Templars, S.W. Lancashire District Lodge.</p>
        <p>The quarterly session was held at—St. Helens on Saturday. where nearly 100 representatives assembled to consider the position of the order and devise means to extend its scope and influence as well as to take council together for promoting the electoral policy of the Order and take a survey of the past, present, and future outlook of the Tem- perance question from a Good Templar standpoint. It was thus considered wise to support Lord Peel's recommendations mainly as contained in his report of the Royal Commission on the liquor question while maintaining the principle of airect popular control. The Secretary reported an increase in membership and the Treasurer a good balance in hand while tne 20th Century Fund was pressed upon the attention of the representatives. Juvenile Templary was reported to be progressing satisfac- torily. Invitations were given for next session in February to be held at Warrington, Bootle and Liverpool, and on a third vote Liverpool was selected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Oddfellows' "Smoker."</head>
        <p>Oddfellows' "Smoker."</p>
        <p>A smoking cAicert was held in connection with the" Prince of Wales "Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity,on Saturday in the lodge room at the" Pelican" Hotel, Butter- market-street. P.G.M. Barnwell presided and was ably supported by P.D.G.M. Salt, P.P.G.M. Emson, N.G. Freeman, V.G. Farrar, and others. The first item on the programme was a piano solo by Bro. Webster, which was well rendered. P.P.G.M. Emson read a letter from Mr. R. I. Wynne Jones, as to the enlargement of the lodge room, which was heartily received. During the evening P.P.G.M. iviassey recited the "Absent minded Beggar "and was loudly applauded for his splendid effort. A piano solo by V.G. Farrar and a whistling solo by Bro W Whittaker received great applause as also did Bro Atherton for his comic songs. P.G. Emson enlivened the meeting with a few readings of "Everyday occurrences in Schools." Songs were also excellently given by P.P.G.M. Matthew Knowles, P.G. NV hitham, Bro Reddich, and Bro Marsh, and were cordially received. After a most enjoyable evening the concert was brought to a close by t•inging "God Save the Queen."</p>
