      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>FRODS HAM.</head>
        <p>FRODS HAM.</p>
        <p>S. CRUEL HUSBAND AND FATELER.—SHOCKING CLOSIIRES.—.I t the Frodgham Petty Seesions, on eVe Ines- day, before Messrs. J. C. Taylor (presiding), and J. Croefield,sorne terrible disclosures of cruelty and neglect ef a. wife and 6 children at Bradley were brought to light as the result of a prosecution by the N.S.P.C.C., 'who were represented by Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, Warring- ton.—The defendant was a "rag-and-tone" hawker.. named David Ainsworth, who resided at Bradley. It the man married his wife,Mary Jane Aiuswortla about 12 years previously,and that there were 6 children ef the marriage. Ever since the date of the first child's birth, he had ill-treated his wife and other members of the family at various periods. This was all dve to his drunken habits. His career as a brute and wife-beater was, however, brought to a termination on Wednesday last, when he was arraigned on three charges: (1). Assaulting his wife on the 17th of November ; (2) to show cause why a separation order should not be granted her with an injunction for the custody of the children (3) for neglect and cruelty to the children Nee , Devio, 11 years ; Leonard, 7 years; Evelyn, 5, years; Charles, 3 years; Ernest, 2 years; and Elsie, 15 months.—Evidence was given by the wife, Lucy Maseey (a neighbour) ; Rebecca Orme (defendant's nice her-in-law), P.C. Wage, Inspector Hackett, N.S.P.Q.C., and the boy David, to the effect that or, the 17th of November, defendant came home from his hawking round in a drunken state, gave his wife two black eyes, assaulted the boy David, who was at the time nursing the baby in a chair. Then he had produced two loaves and ems bacon ; the latter of which he cooked for himself and left the dry bread for his dependents. He was in the habit of generally giving his wife 33 6.1 to 43 a week to Ilecep themselves upon, and preserving the rest of his earnings for himself. The children were occasionally provided with food by the neighbours or else had to starve. The boy, David, said he was starved on Thurs• - day, Friday, and Saturday,and would have bad nothing at all only he was given something by a lady. His father usecl.to come home late, drunk.and make him get out of his bed to put up the horse. The defendant it was also stated had a horse and trap which his wife had provided for their joint use by soliciting public subscripe • (ions. The woman was a clean and tidy per- son always sober, whilst the husband was the reverse.- -On his own behalf, defendant denied the whole evi-- dence,and accused the witnesseSof telling nothing but a " pack of lies."—The Bench cofivicted on each charge. The assault they termed an aggravated one and took. cognisance of it in the seperation Case. For that be would have to pay £1 or 7 days. He would also have to pro- vide 103 a week for his wife whom they granted the separation, and order for the custody of the children. For the neglect el his children he would be fined 103 61 and 103 6d costs or 14 days in default. The Chairman intimated they took that lenient view of the case so as to hive the man an opportunity of paying themoney, especially the weekly allowance to the wife.—Defendant paid the fines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-01_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>RHODESIAN SHARES BOOMING!</head>
        <p>RHODESIAN SHARES BOOMING!</p>
        <p>In view of the great rise in the price of the shares of the Chartered Company (with its capital cf several mil- lions sterling), to nearly £4 per share, there has been great activity in the shares of other RhAlesian companien and it is generally agreed that Reodesian shares are meeting with an amount of attention nenr before be- stowed upon them. More than half the business in the Kaffir ma, ke'lis taking place,inßbode, ian sharcs.The new treaty with Germany will enable the Chartered Company to connect its railwaysystem with the South-West Coast of Africa. This will give another route to Rhodesia, which will probably be the shortest available and the most convenient, and it is calculated that Rhodesia will thus be brought within twenty days of London. The almost complete cessation of mining operations it the Transvaal has enabled the Rhodesian mines to obtain the native labour of which they s.ood in need, and we ought, as a consequence, to witness a great develop- ment of gold mines and land. One of ti o most promis- ing companies devoted to the exploration and develop- ment of lands and gold mines in Rhodesia is the Austen (Gwelo) Development Syndicate Limited. Taking into consideration the smallness of its capital, which is only £50,000. in £1 fully paid up shares, of which £20,000 is for w Tiring capital ; that it owns a farm of 6.000 acres and 507 claims in the be4t-known and most important gold raining districts of Rhodesia, and that the latest re- ports frcm the manager in Rhodesia state that he has struck ore giving over 13:a of gold per ton, the present price of about £l. 103 per share, at which tha shares of the Austen (Gwelo) Developmelt Syndicate, Limited, are now dealt in-on the London Stock Exchange, should make them a good speculative investment.</p>
